{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; sed -n '430,465p' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n     2\t\n     3\t> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n     4\t> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n     5\t> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n     6\t> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n     7\t> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n     8\t> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n     9\t> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n    10\t> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n    13\t\n    14\tBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n    17\t2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n    18\t3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n    19\t   word for it is already given.\n    20\t4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n    21\t   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n    22\t5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n    23\t   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n    24\t\n    25\t## 1. Goal (end to end)\n    26\t\n    27\tEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\n    28\tnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\n    29\tany one step's own score.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n    32\t\n    33\t**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n    34\t\n    35\tThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n    36\t\n    37\t- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n    38\t  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n    39\t  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n    40\t  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n    41\t  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n    42\t- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n    43\t  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n    44\t  nothing at all and were never judged.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Evidence:**\n    47\t\n    48\t| Evidence | Content | Source |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n    51\t| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n    52\t| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n    53\t| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n    54\t\n    55\tAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n    58\t\n    59\t| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n    60\t|---|---|---|\n    61\t| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n    62\t| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n    63\t| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n    64\t| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n    65\t| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n    66\t| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n    67\t| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n    68\t| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n    69\t\n    70\t## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n    71\t\n    72\t- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n    73\t  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n    74\t  system (D263).\n    75\t- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n    76\t  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n    77\t  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n    78\t- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n    79\t- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n    80\t  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n    81\t  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n    82\t  competition or pricing\".\n    83\t- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n    84\t  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n    85\t  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n    86\t  of the previous filing topic).\n    87\t- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n    88\t  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n    89\t  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n    90\t  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n    91\t- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n    92\t\n    93\t## 5. What to do\n    94\t\n    95\tStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n    96\t\n    97\t1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n    98\t   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n    99\t   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   100\t   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n   101\t     the existing rule).\n   102\t   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   103\t   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   104\t   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   105\t   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n   106\t     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n   107\t2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   108\t   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   109\t   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   110\t   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   111\t   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   112\t   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   113\t   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   114\t   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n   115\t3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   116\t   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n   117\t4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   118\t   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n   119\t\n   120\t## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n   121\t\n   122\t- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n   123\t  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n   124\t  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n   125\t- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n   126\t  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n   127\t  measured and reported apart.\n   128\t- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n   129\t- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n   130\t  counted after.\n   131\t- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n   132\t  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n   133\t  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n   134\t  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n   135\t    question\";\n   136\t  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n   137\t  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n   138\t  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n   139\t    or no question uses);\n   140\t  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n   141\t  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n   142\t    ones that matter one by one.\n   143\t- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n   144\t  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n   145\t  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n   146\t  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n   147\t    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n   148\t    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n   149\t      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n   150\t    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n   151\t      facts must not fall.\n   152\t  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n   153\t    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n   154\t      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n   155\t      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n   156\t      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n   157\t    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n   158\t      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n   159\t    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n   160\t      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n   161\t      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n   162\t      what was measured.\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n\nThe council's reversible decisions on Alex's page are recorded in D606 and D607.\n\n**The merge test (D678–D680, 2026-11-19): closed; the registered test did not pass.** The old system opens questions, clean Step 1 reads\nand files under them, and the frozen judge answers. It ran on 70 articles × old-2 and old-3, with a hybrid arm and a control arm, for\n$2.13 ([result](research/2026-11-19-merge-council/RESULT.md)).\n- **Held:**\n  - delivery: the decisive statements reached the judge on 4/4 primary questions in both draws;\n  - talks stayed talks;\n  - both blind readers supported all 4 hybrid answers;\n  - no five-company or 2027 figure was taken as the answer;\n  - c8 proposed no NVIDIA-figure question.\n- **Missed:**\n  - P2's proxy clause: total capex is used for data-center capex without being named a proxy, which old also misses;\n  - V: the rebuilt control's v47 was unsupported, and a reconstruction effect is not ruled out.\n- **Not shown:** that the merge answers better. The old answers were already supported, and the capex band was set by 09-06 history.\n- **Next:** superseded by D681, below.\n\n**The one next task: re-run the v13s blind test once with fresh writers and readers, after the reader calibration on Alex's rulings (D740, D741).**\n\n- **The first blind test stopped** (D740, `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md`):\n  - 12 of 12 first answers were schema-valid, and 11 of 12 passed every check.\n  - **Stop 1:** S1 read \"from $35 billion to $170 billion\" as a range. This was Claude's change, now fixed: an \"X to Y\" after \"from\" is a\n    change between two values.\n  - **Stop 2:** the readers marked a guidance-implied growth benchmark as \"two forecasts of one quarter\".\n- **Alex ruled the readers' boundary questions** (D741):\n  - a guidance-implied growth benchmark is not a defect;\n  - consensus total × a past quarter's share is not the market's reference, and S5 now refuses it;\n  - a total used for a narrower quantity must state its deduction or a supported bound.\n- **Checks:** cases 104 of 104, mutants 40 of 40, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the old harness unchanged.\n- **The reader calibration on six stored boundary replies** (`calibration-D741/`) runs before the re-run.\n- **A clean re-run** goes to Alex as the paid re-send request.\n\nThe record before D740:\n\n- **The format shrank** (D737). Alex chose to shrink it after round 51 (D736) found the full restatement unbounded.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md && nl -ba BLIND2-RESULT.md && nl -ba BLIND-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md && nl -ba BLIND2-RESULT.md && nl -ba BLIND-RESULT.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex (D737) chose to shrink the format. Code refuses an answer only for these five defects:\n     4\t- a number misread;\n     5\t- wrong arithmetic;\n     6\t- a part above its whole, or added into it;\n     7\t- two forecasts of one quarter;\n     8\t- an own estimate passed off as the market's.\n     9\t\n    10\tIt also refuses broken structure. Each rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of `DESIGN.md`'s\n    11\tchecks becomes something the readers see, not a refusal.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Review scope (D737):** a counterexample that lets none of the five defects through, and breaks no structure, is a reader's matter,\n    14\tnot a finding. **Out of scope:**\n    15\t- the frozen ruler and `poc-v118`;\n    16\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules;\n    17\t- the old run and its checker `calc_check.py`, which stay as they are, so the old run stays reproducible.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n    20\t\n    21\t| Today's rule (DESIGN.md) | v13s |\n    22\t|---|---|\n    23\t| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n    24\t| scenarios, scenario membership, `range` summaries (C4) | one forecast per shared quantity; an outcome may be a range |\n    25\t| relations `time_aggregation` / `accounting_parts` / `stated_in_addition` / `overlap_unknown`, and `disputed` | periods of one quantity must make up the output's period (S8); an uncertain overlap is written as an estimate with its assumption |\n    26\t| bridges and `converts` (C8) | a conversion between quantities is computed or estimated as the analyst chooses; code records each computed figure whose inputs measure another quantity, and readers judge it |\n    27\t| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n    28\t| `adjusts` | an estimate rests on the figure it changes |\n    29\t| C9 (same base), C10 (earlier forecasts) | none of the gate's families declares a same base; the request shows no earlier forecast (D728) |\n    30\t| the exact-expression grammar (C1) | the number as written (`quote_number`) plus a qualifier from a closed list |\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n    33\t\n    34\t**[52] Shared definitions.**\n    35\t- **Uses.** A figure *uses* another when the other is among its `inputs`, at any depth. An estimate's `rests_on` is a judgement, not\n    36\t  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n    37\t- **Recomputed values.** The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range. S3, S4 and S6 compare\n    38\t  recomputed values. S2's tolerance applies only between a declared value and its recomputed value.\n    39\t\n    40\t| # | Defect | Rule [52] (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    41\t|---|---|---|---|\n    42\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number exactly as the quote writes it (`quote_number`). The number is in US dollars ($, US$) with its scale (trillion, billion, bn, million), or is a percentage, or is one of the words double, triple or twice. The value equals it, with money in billions. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) goes in `qualifier`. A written range (\"$3–4 billion\", \"between $3 billion and $4 billion\") gives low and high. \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\" is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value. Money in another currency cannot be a source. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    43\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). Units are those `operation` states for each operation; a sum or difference adds one unit. A range input gives a range: both extremes are computed first, and a midpoint may be declared as value. Division by a range that contains zero is refused. | `operation` |\n    44\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, and for the outcomes, the part's lowest recomputed value used for that period may not exceed the whole's highest. | the family header |\n    45\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity, each period is written FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and together they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers. | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    46\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n    47\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    48\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    49\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique and never equal a passage id. `inputs` name figures; `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure uses itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, rung null, `reference_unavailable` null and no marked figure. | the field descriptions |\n    50\t\n    51\t**[52] Operation units (stated in `operation`):**\n    52\t- sum and difference: one unit in, the same out.\n    53\t- product: USD bn × x → USD bn; x × x → x; % × x → %; never USD bn × %.\n    54\t- ratio: same unit → x; USD bn ÷ x → USD bn.\n    55\t- share(whole, percent): whole's unit.\n    56\t- growth(old, new): same unit → %.\n    57\t- apply_growth(base, rate %): base's unit.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 2-old. The rules as first written (superseded by the revision above)\n    60\t\n    61\t| # | Defect | Rule (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    62\t|---|---|---|---|\n    63\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number as the quote writes it (`quote_number`): with currency, scale or percent sign, or the words double, triple or twice. The figure's value equals it, in billions of dollars for money. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) is given in `qualifier`, and none is given when none is written. A written range gives low and high. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    64\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A range input gives a range: low and high, or their midpoint as value. Money is never multiplied by a percentage (use share). Arity is as `operation` states. | `operation` |\n    65\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, the part's forecast figure is at most the whole's: each end of the part's value or range is at most the same end of the whole's. The part's outcome is at most the whole's outcome. | the family header; `outcome_for` |\n    66\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's (Relations). When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity written as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    67\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | For each shared quantity the request lists, at most one figure has basis forecast. When a member's outcome uses that quantity, exactly one does, and every figure of that quantity the outcome uses is reached only through it (the forecast itself may be computed from guidance or consensus). | the family header; `basis` |\n    68\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | A figure marked in `reference_for` has, at any depth beneath it, no estimate and no forecast, and at least one source of basis consensus, guidance or third_party. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    69\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    70\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique. `inputs` name figures and `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure depends on itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, no rung, no marked figure and no reference. | the field descriptions |\n    71\t\n    72\t**Recorded for readers, never refused:**\n    73\t- each estimate's assumption;\n    74\t- each computed figure whose money inputs measure another population, measure or scope than its output;\n    75\t- each growth applied to a base of another period;\n    76\t- each source qualifier.\n    77\t\n    78\t## 3. The wire schema, word for word (examples from no covered company, AGENTS §6)\n    79\t\n    80\t**[52] These descriptions replace the ones in the table below:**\n    81\t\n    82\t| Field | Description [52] |\n    83\t|---|---|\n    84\t| `figures` (array) | \"The family's calculation. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. Figures name each other by id; no figure may use itself.\" |\n    85\t| `id` | \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\" |\n    86\t| `quote_number` | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion'), given as low and high. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\" |\n    87\t| `qualifier` | enum adds `near`; \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n    88\t| `operation` | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; USD bn ÷ x gives USD bn); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure built on the part's quantity to one built on the whole's (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole only to give the rest. When its inputs are periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n    89\t| `outcome_for` | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n    90\t| `reference_for` | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure.\" |\n    91\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\" |\n    92\t\n    93\t**[52] The family header is rendered from the family's data** (no member id is fixed in code):\n    94\t- \"Relations: <part> is part of <whole>. For each of <compare periods> and for the outcomes, the part's lowest value may not exceed the\n    95\t  whole's highest. A sum never adds the part into the whole.\"\n    96\t- \"Shared quantities: …\" (unchanged list).\n    97\t- \"Outcome keys …, in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n    98\t- For O: \"<key>: the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it.\"\n    99\t\n   100\t**figures[]**\n   101\t\n   102\t| Field | Type | Description |\n   103\t|---|---|---|\n   104\t| (array) | | \"The family's calculation: every number an answer rests on, once. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\" |\n   105\t| `id` | string | \"Short and unique, such as F1.\" |\n   106\t| `what` | object | \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\" |\n   107\t| `what.measure` | string | \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\" |\n   108\t| `what.period` | string | \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\" |\n   109\t| `kind` | enum source, computed, estimate | \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\" |\n   110\t| `basis` | enum reported, guidance, consensus, third_party, forecast | \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\" |\n   111\t| `value` | number or null | \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\" |\n   112\t| `low`, `high` | number or null | \"A range, low at most high.\" |\n   113\t| `unit` | enum USD bn, %, x | \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\" |\n   114\t| `evidence_id` | enum of passage ids, or null | \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\" |\n   115\t| `quote_number` | string or null | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency, scale or percent sign and nothing else, for example '$4.5 billion', '$300 million', '40%', '-3%', 'double'; a written range ('$3–4 billion') in full, given as low and high.\" |\n   116\t| `qualifier` | enum none, more than, over, at least, about, approximately, around, roughly, nearly, almost, up to, less than, below, under, or more, or less; or null | \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n   117\t| `inputs` | array of strings or null | \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\" |\n   118\t| `operation` | enum sum, difference, product, ratio, share, growth, apply_growth; or null | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + …; difference(a, b) = a − b; product(a, b, …) = a × b × …, never money by a percentage; ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b; share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change from old to new; apply_growth(base, rate) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). difference, ratio, share, growth and apply_growth take exactly two inputs. A range input gives a range (low and high, or their midpoint as value). The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's; when its inputs are periods of one quantity, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n   119\t| `basis_text` | string or null | \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\" |\n   120\t| `rests_on` | array of strings or null | \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\" |\n   121\t| `outcome_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: exactly one figure for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n   122\t| `reference_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference: a consensus, guidance or outside figure from a shown passage, or one computed only from such figures and reported ones. No estimate or forecast anywhere beneath it.\" |\n   123\t\n   124\t**answers[]**\n   125\t\n   126\t| Field | Description |\n   127\t|---|---|\n   128\t| `view_id` | (enum, unchanged) |\n   129\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why, and nothing else.\" |\n   130\t| `rung` | \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\" |\n   131\t| `reference_unavailable` | \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\" |\n   132\t| `reasons`, `why`, `cites`, `pushes`, `theme_pushes` | unchanged; `why`: \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\" |\n   133\t\n   134\t**The family header (user message)**\n   135\t- \"Relations: v8 is part of v4: for each of FY2027 Q3, FY2027 Q4, FY2027, the part's forecast figure may not exceed the whole's, and\n   136\t  a sum or difference never combines the two.\"\n   137\t- \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure that uses the quantity takes it: …\"\n   138\t- \"Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n   139\t\n   140\t**The system text:** one change. \"a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario\" becomes \"a quantity the family shares\n   141\thas one forecast\". Every other sentence is unchanged.\n   142\t\n   143\t## 4. Build\n   144\t\n   145\t- **New files.** `calc_small.py` holds `from_answer(data, family)` (wire to internal, with S6/S7 marker errors), `check`,\n   146\t  `publish_decision`, `evaluate`, `upstream_of` and `key`, and returns `{errors, recorded, published}` as `calc_check` does.\n   147\t  `render_v13.v13s_schema` builds the schema; `judging-v13s.txt` holds the system text.\n   148\t- **The plan.** `paid_gate.item` takes a format. A v13s plan (`paid-plan-v13s.json`, pinned) uses `calc_small`. A plan without a\n   149\t  format uses `calc_check` and `strip`, so the old run's `results.json` reproduces byte for byte on a copy.\n   150\t- **Rehearsal and mutants.** The rehearsal takes the plan path. Its stub answers are written in v13s. Each rule S1–S8 gets a mutant.\n   151\t- **Size.** Characters and estimated tokens for the whole request, before and after, per item. The schema is expected to be smaller\n   152\t  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\n   153\t\n   154\t**Value × reader.**\n   155\t- `from_answer`'s output is read by `check`, `publish_decision`, `merge`, `card_view` and `normalise`. It uses the internal names\n   156\t  those readers read (`evidence`, `inputs`, `rests_on`, `outcome`, `reference`), and the dropped keys never appear.\n   157\t- `merge`'s unification of a shared forecast uses `evaluate`'s values with no bound.\n   158\t- The reader brief is unchanged. Its `deciding_gap` question is where readers judge the recorded conversions and estimates.\n   159\t\n   160\t**State × event:** unchanged from D731. A refusal is retried once, diagnostic only; the first attempt is scored.\n   161\t\n   162\t## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n   163\t\n   164\t**Inputs.** Each case's archive, call, question, ladder and unit are registered, and the build refuses a heading that differs.\n   165\t\n   166\t| Case | Archive / call | Key | Ladder edges ($bn) | Role |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n   169\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\n   170\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n   171\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 50/40/30/20 | control |\n   172\t\n   173\t**Part A, code, $0.** Each case asserts its intended rule and message. The schema's validity is checked first, so a schema error\n   174\tcannot mask the rule under test.\n   175\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each passes:\n   176\t  - a $4.5 billion ± 3% guidance as two sources with apply_growth ends;\n   177\t  - Q1 + Q2 = H1;\n   178\t  - FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4;\n   179\t  - a year-over-year rate on last year's quarter (recorded);\n   180\t  - whole × mix through share;\n   181\t  - \"over $300 billion\" with qualifier over;\n   182\t  - \"-3%\";\n   183\t  - \"$300 million or more\" with qualifier \"or more\";\n   184\t  - $1.2 trillion as 1200 USD bn;\n   185\t  - a range outcome overlapping its rung;\n   186\t  - one figure marked for two members (its own family);\n   187\t  - a computed external reference;\n   188\t  - an unresolved member;\n   189\t  - a conversion between quantities (recorded);\n   190\t  - \"not only still expects $20 billion\" as a source;\n   191\t  - 12% × 2x = 24%.\n   192\t- **A2, defects.** Each is refused by its named rule:\n   193\t  - **S1:** 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\"; a qualifier dropped; one invented; \"-3%\" as 3; a number not in the quote; a passage's\n   194\t    summary number; trillions as 1.2 USD bn.\n   195\t  - **S2:** a plain wrong result; a wrong end of a range; division by zero; money × %; a three-input difference.\n   196\t  - **S3:** a part forecast above the whole for a listed quarter; an outcome above the whole's.\n   197\t  - **S8:** company + Data Center in one sum; Q2 + Q3 labelled H1; FY − Q1 labelled Q4.\n   198\t  - **S4:** two forecasts of one shared quarter; an outcome reaching guidance for a shared quarter beside the forecast.\n   199\t  - **S5:** a forecast beneath a reference; an estimate beneath a reference; a reported-only reference; both a marked reference and\n   200\t    a reason; neither.\n   201\t  - **S6:** no outcome marker; two; a marker on an unresolved member; an outcome key mismatch; a rung outside the ladder; a rung\n   202\t    whose band misses the outcome; at the band's upper edge.\n   203\t  - **S7:** value with low; a cycle; a dangling input; a passage id in inputs; a source with basis forecast; duplicate answers.\n   204\t- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** from both journals, each encoded in v13s. Each is impossible under the schema or refused with a\n   205\t  reason the request states (the sentence is quoted):\n   206\t  - prose outcome, reference and evidence;\n   207\t  - unit spellings;\n   208\t  - value with range;\n   209\t  - a four-input difference;\n   210\t  - money × % through product;\n   211\t  - \"first half\";\n   212\t  - a passage id in inputs;\n   213\t  - a trillion outcome.\n   214\t\n   215\t**[52] Additions to Part A.**\n   216\t- **A1 adds:**\n   217\t  - the remainder company − Data Center = the rest;\n   218\t  - \"US$10 billion\", \"$96.2bn\", \"roughly twice\", \"between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion\";\n   219\t  - \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" as a range and as a midpoint;\n   220\t  - a point Data Center forecast beside a company range (S3 holds);\n   221\t  - a final range estimate resting on a computed point of the same quantity (S4 holds).\n   222\t- **A2 adds:**\n   223\t  - Data Center × 1x added to company (S8);\n   224\t  - an estimate resting on both part and whole (recorded, not refused, and the record asserted);\n   225\t  - two Data Center Q4 figures on two paths (S4);\n   226\t  - Q3 guidance used directly beside a Q3 forecast (S4);\n   227\t  - an estimate itself marked as the reference (S5);\n   228\t  - a computed reference with basis forecast (S5);\n   229\t  - a declared value within tolerance whose recomputed value crosses the rung edge (S6 on the recomputed value);\n   230\t  - a part whose recomputed lowest value exceeds the whole's recomputed highest, while its declared value does not (S3);\n   231\t  - sum($108B, 2%) (S2 unit);\n   232\t  - division by [-1, 1] (S2);\n   233\t  - [-3, -2] × [2, 4] declared other than [-12, -4] (S2);\n   234\t  - a source as the outcome (S6);\n   235\t  - a figure id equal to a passage id (S7);\n   236\t  - \"plus or minus 2%\" copied as \"2%\" and used as +2% for the range (S1);\n   237\t  - a euro amount (S1).\n   238\t- **A3:** each mistake must be refused by the schema or **actually refused** by its stated rule (asserted). \"first half\" is refused by\n   239\t  S8 when summed. \"1.05 USD trillion\" fails the schema, and \"1050 USD bn\" passes.\n   240\t- **A4, the end to end rehearsal.** One v13s reply goes through the first attempt, the separate-arm reconciliation, the card render and\n   241\t  recovery. For each named defect, the defective answer fails its rule while its paired correct answer reaches the reader's card. The\n   242\t  old run's `results.json` reproduces from its journals with the ledger snapshot saved inside it.\n   243\t\n   244\t**[52] Readers.**\n   245\t- **What they get.** Both readers get the same complete shown passages, with the writer's identity and the checker's decision masked.\n   246\t- **What they mark, on every first reply:**\n   247\t  - whether each forecast is supported, naming the passage or assumption that puts it in its band;\n   248\t  - any of D737's five defects;\n   249\t  - broken structure.\n   250\t- **The wording review is separate.** Refusals are revealed only after that marking, for the review of whether the request states\n   251\t  the rule.\n   252\t- **An accepted reply with a named defect** (marked by both readers, or by the third reader) is a stop.\n   253\t- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\n   254\t\n   255\t**Part B, blind writers ($0 at the provider).**\n   256\t- **The writers.** Each of the six cases is written once by a fresh Claude subagent and once by a fresh read-only Codex session, 12\n   257\t  sessions in all. Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema.\n   258\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   259\t- **The readers.** Two readers who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) grade **every**\n   260\t  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n   261\t  - for each refusal, whether the exact request states the condition (the sentence quoted) and whether the answer was right;\n   262\t  - for each accepted answer, whether each forecast is supported, and whether its recorded conversions or estimates decide its band.\n   263\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements.\n   264\t- **Usable** means accepted, publishable by `publish_decision`, and supported by the readers. For the joint case, both forecasts must\n   265\t  be supported in one reply.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   268\t- an A1 form refused;\n   269\t- an A2 defect not refused by its rule;\n   270\t- an A3 mistake neither impossible nor stated;\n   271\t- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a right answer;\n   272\t- an INCOMPLETE run;\n   273\t- a case with zero usable first replies across both writers;\n   274\t- the joint case without both supported forecasts in one reply.\n   275\t\n   276\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n   277\tD725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, re-run after D741: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5; D740, D741)\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP**, on one registered stop condition: a Part B refusal that refuses a right answer.\n     4\t- P-v4's Claude writer was refused by S4: \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\".\n     5\t- F4 (the $108.0B Q3 guidance) never enters the total as a Q3 value. It builds F9 = growth(Q2 $96.2B, F4) = 12.27%, the guided Q2 to\n     6\t  Q3 growth, and Q4 = apply_growth(F8 $112.5B, F9) = $126.3B. The only Q3 value in the revenue sum is F8.\n     7\t- Both refusal reviewers: the request states the rule (the header's \"Shared quantities … through which every figure an outcome uses\n     8\t  for that quantity goes\"), and the answer is right.\n     9\t- Both readers and both adjudicators call the forecast supported.\n    10\t\n    11\tEvery other registered line passes:\n    12\t- Part A (104 cases, 40 mutants, 19 rehearsal checks) was green before the run.\n    13\t- The run is complete: 12 first replies, 24 readings, 6 adjudication packets, each read by two adjudicators.\n    14\t- Every case has at least one usable reply. The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R05 and R09).\n    15\t- No accepted reply carries a settled defect: no defect was upheld by both adjudicators, and no reader pair marked the same defect.\n    16\t\n    17\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/`. It cost $0 at the provider: writers, readers, reviewers and adjudicators were fresh\n    18\tClaude subagents and fresh read-only Codex sessions. Adjudication packets are built by `blind_adjudicate.py`; the tally is\n    19\t`TALLY.json`.\n    20\t\n    21\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    22\t\n    23\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    24\t|---|---|---|\n    25\t| P (joint) | publish: v4 413.1, v8 382.4, both rung 2 | publish: v4 419.2, v8 388.3, both rung 2 |\n    26\t| P-v4 | **refused (S4)**: 416.6, rung 2 | publish: 416.4, rung 2 |\n    27\t| P-v8 | publish: 383.6, rung 2 | publish: 388.6, rung 2 |\n    28\t| S (CY2026) | publish: 717.8, rung 4 | publish: 680.8, rung 3 |\n    29\t| O | publish: 14, rung 1 | publish: 16, rung 1 |\n    30\t| E | publish: 36, rung 3 | publish: 36, rung 3 |\n    31\t\n    32\t- 12 of 12 were schema-valid.\n    33\t- 11 of 12 passed every code check.\n    34\t- D741's three rulings were applied:\n    35\t  - S applies a stated deduction from the total in both replies (Claude 3% non-data-center share; Codex 92% data-center share).\n    36\t  - No v8 reference carries a past quarter's share (the S5 [D741] check refused none).\n    37\t  - D740's \"from … to …\" fix is exercised: E's writers copied the CPU market figures without a range refusal.\n    38\t\n    39\t## 2. The refusal: mechanism and census\n    40\t\n    41\t- **The mechanism.** S4's bypass walk starts at each outcome and follows `inputs`. It records every figure whose key is a listed shared\n    42\t  quantity, and it descends through every non-forecast figure, rates included. F9's unit is %, yet the walk descended into F9's inputs\n    43\t  and reached F4. A rate computed from a quarter's guidance is another quantity. The quarter's value in the calculation is still F8\n    44\t  alone.\n    45\t- **The ruling it contradicts.** D741 ruling 1 (Alex: \"不算错\"): a growth rate computed from guidance and used as a benchmark is not a\n    46\t  second value when one figure supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation. The question Alex answered named the deciding point:\n    47\t  \"进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个\". The system text also asks the writer to give \"a run-rate that carries the guided growth\n    48\t  forward\". The schema's benchmark sentence names \"an estimate\", and that is the narrower wording the code follows.\n    49\t- **The census, $0** (`s4_census.py`, run against the copy `s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt`). Every stored v13s first reply from both blind tests (24) goes through the current\n    50\t  checker and through a copy that stops the walk beneath a % or x figure.\n    51\t  - 1 of 24 changes: this refusal, which becomes publish.\n    52\t  - The other 23 keep their decisions and errors.\n    53\t  - The 104 registered cases all pass on the copy.\n    54\t\n    55\t## 3. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    56\t\n    57\tEach reply had a Claude reader and a Codex reader. Six replies drew a disagreement. Two adjudicators, one per family, settled each point\n    58\tagainst the passages and the format rules (the D741 rulings included). A point counts as settled when both agree.\n    59\t\n    60\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    61\t- every \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance given as its midpoint (R06 F7, R09 F4, R10 F3). The format permits this.\n    62\t- R11 F4 (\"up to\" stands before the $3B total in e1919's quote, not before the $1B tranche).\n    63\t- R11 F12 (42 + 25 = 67 with \"more than\" kept on the source).\n    64\t\n    65\t**Unsettled: the families split** (in every split, the Codex adjudicator upholds and the Claude adjudicator rejects). They fall into\n    66\ttwo classes.\n    67\t1. **O's \"$18 billion\" (R07, R11).** e2889: \"it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year\".\n    68\t   Both O writers read it as the part of e2620's $25B balance funded by year-end, stated that reading as their assumption, and built\n    69\t   the reference as 25 − 18 = 7. The rung depends on that reading: without the deduction the balance starts at $25B, which is rung 2.\n    70\t   - **R07, \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\" on F3.** D741 ruling 2 decides it: the market's reference may be \"a\n    71\t     calculation that carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period\". F3 is reported − guidance with no\n    72\t     carried share, so by the ruling it is not this defect.\n    73\t   - **R07, \"two forecasts of one quarter\" on F3 and F10.** F3 has the outcome's key but is the July balance still unfunded, a part of\n    74\t     the year-end total. The Claude adjudicator calls it the company-schedule baseline beneath the forecast, which the format allows.\n    75\t     The Codex adjudicator calls it a part labelled as the whole.\n    76\t   - **R11.** The Codex family calls F2's \"guidance to fund in Q3–Q4\" an unsupported schedule and the forecast not supported. The\n    77\t     Claude family says \"for the remainder of its fiscal year\" gives the timing and the answer states the reading.\n    78\t   - The first test split the same way on the same passage (D740, R02).\n    79\t2. **Statements in the answer's prose that no figure carries** (R03, R09, R11). Codex upholds them as \"number misread\" or \"wrong\n    80\t   arithmetic\". Claude rejects them because those types are defined on source and computed figures. I read each one against its\n    81\t   passage:\n    82\t   - R11: \"if the $18B funding slips into FY2028 … probably keep it below $25B\" is wrong on the answer's own numbers: a full slip gives\n    83\t     25 + 7 = 32, rung 2.\n    84\t   - R09: \"$115bn, then 20% sequential growth\" is offered as reaching rung 1, but it gives 430.8 against the 431.2 edge.\n    85\t   - R09: \"both 6 GW\" drops e1041's \"up to\".\n    86\t   - R03: \"Vera Rubin is already about 20%\" states e2721's expectation as achieved.\n    87\t   - R03 \"flat second half\" is disputed: the Claude adjudicator shows a reading under which it holds.\n    88\t   None of these enters a figure, an outcome or a rung. They are what the card would show an analyst. Code does not check prose\n    89\t   (D737), and the registered defect types do not cover it.\n    90\t\n    91\t**Support.** Every forecast is supported by both adjudicators except R11 (O, Claude writer), where the families split as in class 1.\n    92\t\n    93\t## 4. What the test shows\n    94\t\n    95\t- **The request and the rulings hold.** There were no schema refusals and no unstated rule. D741's three rulings were applied by the\n    96\t  writers and are visible to code (S5) and readers.\n    97\t- **One checker rule still conflicts with a ruling.** It is S4's walk through rates. A one-line change flips the refused case and\n    98\t  changes no other stored decision.\n    99\t- **The reader families still split on two things.**\n   100\t  - One is a source reading: what \"$18 billion committed … for the remainder of its fiscal year\" covers.\n   101\t  - The other is whether a wrong number in prose is a defect.\n   102\t  - Neither is a registered stop, because neither was settled. Both reach the product: the first decides O's rung, and the second is\n   103\t    what an analyst reads.\n   104\t- **One draw per writer.** P's six replies are 413–419 (v4) and 382–389 (v8), all rung 2. That is no evidence about gpt-6-luna.\n     1\t# judging-v13s blind conformance test: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5; D737–D739)\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP**, on two registered stop conditions.\n     4\t1. A check refused a right answer. E's Claude writer copied \"$35 billion\" from \"from $35 billion to $170 billion\", the CPU market's\n     5\t   2026 value in a grow-from-to sentence, and S1 read the sentence as a range.\n     6\t2. An accepted reply carries a named defect. P-v4's Codex writer used two values for company Q3 revenue: the $108B guidance to set a\n     7\t   growth benchmark, and its own $113.6B forecast as the base for Q4. Both readers and both adjudicators marked it.\n     8\t\n     9\tWhat passed:\n    10\t- All 12 first answers were schema-valid, with none refused by the schema.\n    11\t- 11 of 12 passed every code check.\n    12\t- One refusal is disputed on whether the request states its condition. The two reviewers split on that, and agree the refused\n    13\t  amount is right.\n    14\t\n    15\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`. It cost $0 at the provider: the writers, readers and adjudicators were fresh Claude\n    16\tsubagents and fresh read-only Codex sessions. Every Codex writer's stream shows it read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n    17\t`schema.json`.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    20\t\n    21\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    22\t|---|---|---|\n    23\t| P (joint) | publish: v4 416.3, v8 384.9, both rung 2 | publish: v4 417.4, v8 386.5, both rung 2 |\n    24\t| P-v4 | publish: 417.4, rung 2 | publish: 419.8, rung 2 |\n    25\t| P-v8 | publish: 385.9, rung 2 | publish: 387.1, rung 2 |\n    26\t| S (CY2026) | publish: 705–735, rung 4 | publish: 715, rung 4 |\n    27\t| O | publish: 8–24, rung 1 | publish: 16, rung 1 |\n    28\t| E | **refused (S1)**: \"$35 billion\" read as part of \"$35 billion to $170 billion\" | publish: 36, rung 3 |\n    29\t\n    30\t**The refusal review** (`refusal-review/`):\n    31\t- Both reviewers say the refused figure is right: $35B is the 2026 value, and $170B is the 2030 value of the same market.\n    32\t- On whether the request states the condition, the Claude reviewer says no and the Codex reviewer says yes.\n    33\t- **The mechanism is mine.** Round 54's change added \"X to Y\" as a range form so that \"$195 billion to $205 billion\" would pass. The\n    34\t  old checker had excluded \"to\" for exactly this ambiguity. Its comment reads: \"'to' is ambiguous ('from $253 billion to $392\n    35\t  billion')\".\n    36\t\n    37\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`reader-packets/`, `adjudication/`, `TALLY.json`)\n    38\t\n    39\tEach reply had two blind readers, a Claude subagent and a Codex session. Each saw the whole request and the whole reply, and nothing\n    40\tof the writer or the checker. Where the two disagreed, two adjudicators, one per family, settled each point against the passages and\n    41\tthe format rules. A point counts as settled when both adjudicators agree.\n    42\t\n    43\t**Settled defects on accepted replies.**\n    44\t- **R06 (P-v4, Codex writer), two forecasts of one quarter.** Both readers and both adjudicators marked it.\n    45\t  - F7 = growth(Q2, F4) takes Q3 from the $108B guidance outside the Q3 forecast F6 ($113.6B). It reaches the outcome through an\n    46\t    estimate's `rests_on` (F8, which rests on F7), so S4's walk, which follows `inputs` only, did not see it. Under D738 this is an\n    47\t    indirect form, recorded for readers.\n    48\t  - The effect is about $6B on Q4, and the rung is unchanged.\n    49\t\n    50\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    51\t- every \"number misread\" claim. These were a \"plus or minus\" expression given as its midpoint, which the format permits; \"up to\",\n    52\t  which in e1919's quote belongs to the $3B total and not to the $1B tranche; and \"about $70 billion\", which was kept;\n    53\t- both \"wrong arithmetic\" claims:\n    54\t  - R09 declared the midpoint of a computed range, which the format permits;\n    55\t  - R02 summed \"more than $42 billion\" and $25 billion as 67, keeping the qualifier on its source;\n    56\t- R12's two-forecast claim (guidance used as a benchmark beneath an estimate, with one Q3 value in the outcome chain).\n    57\t\n    58\t**Unsettled: the two families split** (the Codex adjudicator upholds, the Claude adjudicator rejects).\n    59\t- **A computed market reference.**\n    60\t  - The cases:\n    61\t    - consensus company revenue ($411B) × the reported Q2 Data Center share (92.5%) gives a Data Center reference of about $380B\n    62\t      (R05, R08, R12);\n    63\t    - the reported $25B balance − the $18B \"committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year\" gives $7B (R02).\n    64\t  - **The Codex adjudicator's view:** applying a quarter's share to a full year, or netting the schedule, is the writer's assumption,\n    65\t    so it is not the market's reference.\n    66\t  - **The Claude adjudicator's view:** the rule allows a figure computed only from consensus, guidance and reported figures.\n    67\t  - The original design took the Claude view: DESIGN.md C5 passes q02's \"$392B consensus × 92% reported mix\" as a reference.\n    68\t- **A label that changes the quantity (R09, S's Claude writer).** Company-wide capex was labelled \"data-center capex\".\n    69\t  - The Codex adjudicator calls it not supported (an outcome not of the question's quantity).\n    70\t  - The Claude adjudicator calls it supported: the passages use the two terms interchangeably (e5376 against e5081).\n    71\t  - Round 55 notes that those two articles describe five companies, while R09 answers for four.\n    72\t\n    73\t**Support.** Every forecast is supported by both adjudicators, except R09, where the families split as above.\n    74\t\n    75\t## 3. What the test shows\n    76\t\n    77\t- **The request now states its rules.**\n    78\t  - Twelve first answers from two writer families produced no schema refusal and no refusal on an unstated rule.\n    79\t  - The one code refusal was a checker error.\n    80\t  - Compare the paid gate (D733), where 0 of 90 outcomes named a figure.\n    81\t- **The checker still holds one error of mine:** the \"from … to …\" range.\n    82\t- **The indirect route through an estimate's `rests_on` let one real inconsistency through.** D738 chose to record such routes for\n    83\t  readers. The readers caught it, and the registered stop rule counts their catch.\n    84\t- **The two reader families apply different thresholds** to a computed market reference and to a relabelled quantity. These are ruler\n    85\t  questions. By the council-loop rule \"Model readers carry their family's threshold … take a balanced set of boundary cases to Alex;\n    86\t  his rulings are the ruler\", they go to Alex.\n    87\t- **The joint and separate answers in P agree closely.** v4 is 416–420 and v8 385–387, all rung 2. This is one draw each, a strong\n    88\t  writer, and not gpt-6-luna. It is no evidence for D725's joint-versus-separate question.\n    89\t\n    90\t## 4. Council B (round 55, `codex-r55.md`)\n    91\t\n    92\t- **STOP is correct as registered**, and every count reconciles. Three wordings above are corrected.\n    93\t- **R06 and R12 use the same method.** Each derives a guidance-implied Q3 growth benchmark of about 12.25%, chooses 13% for Q4, and\n    94\t  applies it to its own higher Q3 forecast. Only one Q3 dollar figure enters the revenue calculation. The adjudicators upheld one and\n    95\t  rejected the other on reasoning that fits both, so the readers' boundary is unstable. It goes to Alex.\n    96\t- **Questions for Alex**, with the council's recommendations:\n    97\t  - (1) May a guidance-implied growth benchmark inform a growth estimate, when one Q3 forecast supplies the revenue calculation? The\n    98\t    council recommends allowing it.\n    99\t  - (2) May \"consensus total × last quarter's segment share\" be called the market's segment estimate? The council recommends no: call\n   100\t    it our comparison estimate unless an outside source gives the full-year share or segment figure.\n   101\t  - (3) May total capex stand in for data-center capex? The council recommends requiring a stated deduction or a supported bound.\n   102\t- **The next step:**\n   103\t  - fix the S1 \"from … to …\" range;\n   104\t  - apply Alex's rulings in the reader brief, and in code only where the structure decides them (D738);\n   105\t  - check that both reader families apply the rulings the same way to the stored boundary replies;\n   106\t  - run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers. A clean result is the basis for the paid re-send request.\n   107\t\n   108\t## 5. Not measured\n   109\t\n   110\tWhether gpt-6-luna at medium effort follows the format: only the paid re-send measures that. Readers' quality judgements beyond the\n   111\tfive defects were not graded.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11300,11400p'; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && diff -u calc_small.py s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt; rg -n 'def _shared|run.rate|benchmark|shared.quantit|S[1-5]' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{calc_small.py,render_v13.py,judging-v13s.txt,s4_census.py}\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11300,11400p'; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && diff -u calc_small.py s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt; rg -n 'def _shared|run.rate|benchmark|shared.quantit|S[1-5]' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{calc_small.py,render_v13.py,judging-v13s.txt,s4_census.py}\"","aggregated_output":" 11300\t  - **S8:**\n 11301\t    - a correct sum of the part and the whole for different periods was refused;\n 11302\t    - a remainder across periods was accepted;\n 11303\t    - a period added twice was accepted.\n 11304\t  - **S4:** an unused or superseded figure was counted. Only the forecasts the outcomes adopt now count.\n 11305\t  - **S5/S7:** `rests_on` is enforced for estimates only and `inputs` for computed figures only, and numbers must be finite.\n 11306\t  - **The runner:** it now refuses a reply that is not exactly one JSON object, an incomplete inventory, and a ladder out of order.\n 11307\t    A checker error is recorded per reply.\n 11308\t- **Checks:**\n 11309\t  - `small_cases.py`: 100 of 100, each an exact rule set;\n 11310\t  - `small_mutants.py`: 38 mutants caught, and the control passes;\n 11311\t  - `small_rehearsal.py`: 19 of 19, including a defective and a correct answer for each named defect through the gate path, and the old\n 11312\t    run reproduced byte for byte;\n 11313\t  - the old harness unchanged: rehearsal 63 of 63, the $0 gate 87 of 87, the old plan's bytes identical.\n 11314\t- **Size:**\n 11315\t  - The v13s schema is about 14,300 characters against about 9,600, and a whole pair request about 140,000 characters against\n 11316\t    134,000. It is larger, not smaller as SMALL-DESIGN.md §4 expected.\n 11317\t  - The reason: the fields the old request left undefined now carry their rules (AGENTS §6).\n 11318\t- **Decided:**\n 11319\t  - The blind test (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5) runs now, without a further design round. It costs $0 at the provider.\n 11320\t  - Its stop conditions measure the same classes empirically: readers mark the five defects on every reply, and an accepted reply with\n 11321\t    a marked defect stops the test.\n 11322\t  - Its result goes to a council round (Council B) before anything goes to Alex.\n 11323\t- **Output:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`.\n 11324\t\n 11325\t## D740 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s blind test stops on a checker error and a reader-marked defect; council round 55 sends three reader rulings to Alex\n 11326\t\n 11327\t- **Result** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`; $0 at the\n 11328\t  provider):\n 11329\t  - All 12 first answers (six cases, Claude and Codex writers) were schema-valid, and 11 passed every check.\n 11330\t  - **Stop 1:** S1 refused a right answer. \"From $35 billion to $170 billion\" was read as a range, through round 54's \"to\" range form\n 11331\t    (Claude's mistake). The old checker had excluded that form for exactly this ambiguity.\n 11332\t  - **Stop 2:** both readers and both adjudicators marked R06, a guidance-implied Q3 growth benchmark used while forecasting Q3 above\n 11333\t    guidance, as \"two forecasts of one quarter\". Both adjudicators rejected R12's claim, which has the same pattern.\n 11334\t  - The reader families split on a computed market reference (consensus total × last quarter's segment share; R02, R05, R08, R12) and\n 11335\t    on total capex used as data-center capex (R09).\n 11336\t- **Council round 55** (`codex-r55.md`):\n 11337\t  - STOP is correct as registered, and the counts reconcile (three wordings corrected).\n 11338\t  - R06 and R12 are the same method, so the readers' boundary is unstable.\n 11339\t  - The three rulings go to Alex.\n 11340\t  - **Next:**\n 11341\t    - fix the S1 range;\n 11342\t    - apply the rulings, in the reader brief and in code only where the structure decides them;\n 11343\t    - verify both reader families on the stored boundary replies;\n 11344\t    - re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers, then the paid re-send request.\n 11345\t\n 11346\t## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\n 11347\t\n 11348\t- **The questions** (after D740), each with its options; the recommended option is listed first:\n 11349\t  - 【增速参考】\"写手用公司指引的三季度 1080 亿算出“指引隐含增速约 12%”，参考它定四季度增速 13%，再套在自己预测的三季度 1136 亿上。进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个。这算不算“同一季度用了两个数”的错？\" Options: 不算错 (推荐); 算错.\n 11350\t  - 【市场参照】\"分析师预期公司全年总收入 4110 亿，最近一个季度数据中心占 92.5%，乘出来约 3800 亿。能不能把这 3800 亿叫作“市场对数据中心全年的预期”？\" Options: 不能 (推荐); 能.\n 11351\t  - 【总开支当数据中心开支】\"问题问四大云厂商“数据中心资本开支”，原文只给了“总资本开支”（并说大部分投向 AI 基础设施）。一份答案直接拿总开支当数据中心开支，没扣非数据中心的部分。可以吗？\" Options: 必须写明扣多少 (推荐); 可以直接用.\n 11352\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"不算错 (推荐)\"; \"不能 (推荐)\"; \"必须写明扣多少 (推荐)\".\n 11353\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11354\t  1. **Not a defect:** a growth rate computed from guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate, when one figure\n 11355\t     supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation.\n 11356\t  2. **Not the market's reference:** consensus for a total × a past quarter's reported share. That figure is our comparison estimate.\n 11357\t     Only an outside figure for the member's own quantity (for example Morningstar's direct segment estimate), or a calculation that\n 11358\t     carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period, is the market's reference.\n 11359\t  3. **A total used for a narrower quantity** (total capex for data-center capex) must state the deduction as its own figure, or give\n 11360\t     a supported bound that shows where the narrower quantity lands.\n 11361\t- **How they are applied (D738):**\n 11362\t  - Ruling 2's carried share is structural, so S5 refuses it.\n 11363\t  - Rulings 1 and 3 go in the request and in the reader brief, because they turn on what a label or an estimate means.\n 11364\t  - Then three steps:\n 11365\t    1. fix the S1 \"from … to …\" range (D740);\n 11366\t    2. check that both reader families apply the rulings the same way to the stored boundary replies;\n 11367\t    3. re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers.\n--- calc_small.py\t2026-11-22 19:42:05\n+++ s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt\t2026-11-22 20:04:37\n@@ -640,6 +640,8 @@\n                     reached.append(fid)\n                     if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n                         continue                           # through the forecast figure: what lies beneath it is its basis\n+                if f['unit'] in ('%', 'x'):\n+                    continue                               # a rate or multiple is another quantity: what it is computed from supplies no value of this one\n                 stack.extend(f.get('inputs') or [])\n             if not reached:\n                 continue\nrg: docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nrg: docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nrg: docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nrg: docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4_census.py: No such file or directory (os error 2)\n","exit_code":2,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba BLIND2-RESULT.md && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '141,277p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba BLIND2-RESULT.md && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '141,277p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, re-run after D741: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5; D740, D741)\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP**, on one registered stop condition: a Part B refusal that refuses a right answer.\n     4\t- P-v4's Claude writer was refused by S4: \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\".\n     5\t- F4 (the $108.0B Q3 guidance) never enters the total as a Q3 value. It builds F9 = growth(Q2 $96.2B, F4) = 12.27%, the guided Q2 to\n     6\t  Q3 growth, and Q4 = apply_growth(F8 $112.5B, F9) = $126.3B. The only Q3 value in the revenue sum is F8.\n     7\t- Both refusal reviewers: the request states the rule (the header's \"Shared quantities … through which every figure an outcome uses\n     8\t  for that quantity goes\"), and the answer is right.\n     9\t- Both readers and both adjudicators call the forecast supported.\n    10\t\n    11\tEvery other registered line passes:\n    12\t- Part A (104 cases, 40 mutants, 19 rehearsal checks) was green before the run.\n    13\t- The run is complete: 12 first replies, 24 readings, 6 adjudication packets, each read by two adjudicators.\n    14\t- Every case has at least one usable reply. The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R05 and R09).\n    15\t- No accepted reply carries a settled defect: no defect was upheld by both adjudicators, and no reader pair marked the same defect.\n    16\t\n    17\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/`. It cost $0 at the provider: writers, readers, reviewers and adjudicators were fresh\n    18\tClaude subagents and fresh read-only Codex sessions. Adjudication packets are built by `blind_adjudicate.py`; the tally is\n    19\t`TALLY.json`.\n    20\t\n    21\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    22\t\n    23\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    24\t|---|---|---|\n    25\t| P (joint) | publish: v4 413.1, v8 382.4, both rung 2 | publish: v4 419.2, v8 388.3, both rung 2 |\n    26\t| P-v4 | **refused (S4)**: 416.6, rung 2 | publish: 416.4, rung 2 |\n    27\t| P-v8 | publish: 383.6, rung 2 | publish: 388.6, rung 2 |\n    28\t| S (CY2026) | publish: 717.8, rung 4 | publish: 680.8, rung 3 |\n    29\t| O | publish: 14, rung 1 | publish: 16, rung 1 |\n    30\t| E | publish: 36, rung 3 | publish: 36, rung 3 |\n    31\t\n    32\t- 12 of 12 were schema-valid.\n    33\t- 11 of 12 passed every code check.\n    34\t- D741's three rulings were applied:\n    35\t  - S applies a stated deduction from the total in both replies (Claude 3% non-data-center share; Codex 92% data-center share).\n    36\t  - No v8 reference carries a past quarter's share (the S5 [D741] check refused none).\n    37\t  - D740's \"from … to …\" fix is exercised: E's writers copied the CPU market figures without a range refusal.\n    38\t\n    39\t## 2. The refusal: mechanism and census\n    40\t\n    41\t- **The mechanism.** S4's bypass walk starts at each outcome and follows `inputs`. It records every figure whose key is a listed shared\n    42\t  quantity, and it descends through every non-forecast figure, rates included. F9's unit is %, yet the walk descended into F9's inputs\n    43\t  and reached F4. A rate computed from a quarter's guidance is another quantity. The quarter's value in the calculation is still F8\n    44\t  alone.\n    45\t- **The ruling it contradicts.** D741 ruling 1 (Alex: \"不算错\"): a growth rate computed from guidance and used as a benchmark is not a\n    46\t  second value when one figure supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation. The question Alex answered named the deciding point:\n    47\t  \"进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个\". The system text also asks the writer to give \"a run-rate that carries the guided growth\n    48\t  forward\". The schema's benchmark sentence names \"an estimate\", and that is the narrower wording the code follows.\n    49\t- **The census, $0** (`s4_census.py`, run against the copy `s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt`). Every stored v13s first reply from both blind tests (24) goes through the current\n    50\t  checker and through a copy that stops the walk beneath a % or x figure.\n    51\t  - 1 of 24 changes: this refusal, which becomes publish.\n    52\t  - The other 23 keep their decisions and errors.\n    53\t  - The 104 registered cases all pass on the copy.\n    54\t\n    55\t## 3. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    56\t\n    57\tEach reply had a Claude reader and a Codex reader. Six replies drew a disagreement. Two adjudicators, one per family, settled each point\n    58\tagainst the passages and the format rules (the D741 rulings included). A point counts as settled when both agree.\n    59\t\n    60\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    61\t- every \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance given as its midpoint (R06 F7, R09 F4, R10 F3). The format permits this.\n    62\t- R11 F4 (\"up to\" stands before the $3B total in e1919's quote, not before the $1B tranche).\n    63\t- R11 F12 (42 + 25 = 67 with \"more than\" kept on the source).\n    64\t\n    65\t**Unsettled: the families split** (in every split, the Codex adjudicator upholds and the Claude adjudicator rejects). They fall into\n    66\ttwo classes.\n    67\t1. **O's \"$18 billion\" (R07, R11).** e2889: \"it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year\".\n    68\t   Both O writers read it as the part of e2620's $25B balance funded by year-end, stated that reading as their assumption, and built\n    69\t   the reference as 25 − 18 = 7. The rung depends on that reading: without the deduction the balance starts at $25B, which is rung 2.\n    70\t   - **R07, \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\" on F3.** D741 ruling 2 decides it: the market's reference may be \"a\n    71\t     calculation that carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period\". F3 is reported − guidance with no\n    72\t     carried share, so by the ruling it is not this defect.\n    73\t   - **R07, \"two forecasts of one quarter\" on F3 and F10.** F3 has the outcome's key but is the July balance still unfunded, a part of\n    74\t     the year-end total. The Claude adjudicator calls it the company-schedule baseline beneath the forecast, which the format allows.\n    75\t     The Codex adjudicator calls it a part labelled as the whole.\n    76\t   - **R11.** The Codex family calls F2's \"guidance to fund in Q3–Q4\" an unsupported schedule and the forecast not supported. The\n    77\t     Claude family says \"for the remainder of its fiscal year\" gives the timing and the answer states the reading.\n    78\t   - The first test split the same way on the same passage (D740, R02).\n    79\t2. **Statements in the answer's prose that no figure carries** (R03, R09, R11). Codex upholds them as \"number misread\" or \"wrong\n    80\t   arithmetic\". Claude rejects them because those types are defined on source and computed figures. I read each one against its\n    81\t   passage:\n    82\t   - R11: \"if the $18B funding slips into FY2028 … probably keep it below $25B\" is wrong on the answer's own numbers: a full slip gives\n    83\t     25 + 7 = 32, rung 2.\n    84\t   - R09: \"$115bn, then 20% sequential growth\" is offered as reaching rung 1, but it gives 430.8 against the 431.2 edge.\n    85\t   - R09: \"both 6 GW\" drops e1041's \"up to\".\n    86\t   - R03: \"Vera Rubin is already about 20%\" states e2721's expectation as achieved.\n    87\t   - R03 \"flat second half\" is disputed: the Claude adjudicator shows a reading under which it holds.\n    88\t   None of these enters a figure, an outcome or a rung. They are what the card would show an analyst. Code does not check prose\n    89\t   (D737), and the registered defect types do not cover it.\n    90\t\n    91\t**Support.** Every forecast is supported by both adjudicators except R11 (O, Claude writer), where the families split as in class 1.\n    92\t\n    93\t## 4. What the test shows\n    94\t\n    95\t- **The request and the rulings hold.** There were no schema refusals and no unstated rule. D741's three rulings were applied by the\n    96\t  writers and are visible to code (S5) and readers.\n    97\t- **One checker rule still conflicts with a ruling.** It is S4's walk through rates. A one-line change flips the refused case and\n    98\t  changes no other stored decision.\n    99\t- **The reader families still split on two things.**\n   100\t  - One is a source reading: what \"$18 billion committed … for the remainder of its fiscal year\" covers.\n   101\t  - The other is whether a wrong number in prose is a defect.\n   102\t  - Neither is a registered stop, because neither was settled. Both reach the product: the first decides O's rung, and the second is\n   103\t    what an analyst reads.\n   104\t- **One draw per writer.** P's six replies are 413–419 (v4) and 382–389 (v8), all rung 2. That is no evidence about gpt-6-luna.\n   141\thas one forecast\". Every other sentence is unchanged.\n   142\t\n   143\t## 4. Build\n   144\t\n   145\t- **New files.** `calc_small.py` holds `from_answer(data, family)` (wire to internal, with S6/S7 marker errors), `check`,\n   146\t  `publish_decision`, `evaluate`, `upstream_of` and `key`, and returns `{errors, recorded, published}` as `calc_check` does.\n   147\t  `render_v13.v13s_schema` builds the schema; `judging-v13s.txt` holds the system text.\n   148\t- **The plan.** `paid_gate.item` takes a format. A v13s plan (`paid-plan-v13s.json`, pinned) uses `calc_small`. A plan without a\n   149\t  format uses `calc_check` and `strip`, so the old run's `results.json` reproduces byte for byte on a copy.\n   150\t- **Rehearsal and mutants.** The rehearsal takes the plan path. Its stub answers are written in v13s. Each rule S1–S8 gets a mutant.\n   151\t- **Size.** Characters and estimated tokens for the whole request, before and after, per item. The schema is expected to be smaller\n   152\t  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\n   153\t\n   154\t**Value × reader.**\n   155\t- `from_answer`'s output is read by `check`, `publish_decision`, `merge`, `card_view` and `normalise`. It uses the internal names\n   156\t  those readers read (`evidence`, `inputs`, `rests_on`, `outcome`, `reference`), and the dropped keys never appear.\n   157\t- `merge`'s unification of a shared forecast uses `evaluate`'s values with no bound.\n   158\t- The reader brief is unchanged. Its `deciding_gap` question is where readers judge the recorded conversions and estimates.\n   159\t\n   160\t**State × event:** unchanged from D731. A refusal is retried once, diagnostic only; the first attempt is scored.\n   161\t\n   162\t## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n   163\t\n   164\t**Inputs.** Each case's archive, call, question, ladder and unit are registered, and the build refuses a heading that differs.\n   165\t\n   166\t| Case | Archive / call | Key | Ladder edges ($bn) | Role |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n   169\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\n   170\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n   171\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 50/40/30/20 | control |\n   172\t\n   173\t**Part A, code, $0.** Each case asserts its intended rule and message. The schema's validity is checked first, so a schema error\n   174\tcannot mask the rule under test.\n   175\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each passes:\n   176\t  - a $4.5 billion ± 3% guidance as two sources with apply_growth ends;\n   177\t  - Q1 + Q2 = H1;\n   178\t  - FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4;\n   179\t  - a year-over-year rate on last year's quarter (recorded);\n   180\t  - whole × mix through share;\n   181\t  - \"over $300 billion\" with qualifier over;\n   182\t  - \"-3%\";\n   183\t  - \"$300 million or more\" with qualifier \"or more\";\n   184\t  - $1.2 trillion as 1200 USD bn;\n   185\t  - a range outcome overlapping its rung;\n   186\t  - one figure marked for two members (its own family);\n   187\t  - a computed external reference;\n   188\t  - an unresolved member;\n   189\t  - a conversion between quantities (recorded);\n   190\t  - \"not only still expects $20 billion\" as a source;\n   191\t  - 12% × 2x = 24%.\n   192\t- **A2, defects.** Each is refused by its named rule:\n   193\t  - **S1:** 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\"; a qualifier dropped; one invented; \"-3%\" as 3; a number not in the quote; a passage's\n   194\t    summary number; trillions as 1.2 USD bn.\n   195\t  - **S2:** a plain wrong result; a wrong end of a range; division by zero; money × %; a three-input difference.\n   196\t  - **S3:** a part forecast above the whole for a listed quarter; an outcome above the whole's.\n   197\t  - **S8:** company + Data Center in one sum; Q2 + Q3 labelled H1; FY − Q1 labelled Q4.\n   198\t  - **S4:** two forecasts of one shared quarter; an outcome reaching guidance for a shared quarter beside the forecast.\n   199\t  - **S5:** a forecast beneath a reference; an estimate beneath a reference; a reported-only reference; both a marked reference and\n   200\t    a reason; neither.\n   201\t  - **S6:** no outcome marker; two; a marker on an unresolved member; an outcome key mismatch; a rung outside the ladder; a rung\n   202\t    whose band misses the outcome; at the band's upper edge.\n   203\t  - **S7:** value with low; a cycle; a dangling input; a passage id in inputs; a source with basis forecast; duplicate answers.\n   204\t- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** from both journals, each encoded in v13s. Each is impossible under the schema or refused with a\n   205\t  reason the request states (the sentence is quoted):\n   206\t  - prose outcome, reference and evidence;\n   207\t  - unit spellings;\n   208\t  - value with range;\n   209\t  - a four-input difference;\n   210\t  - money × % through product;\n   211\t  - \"first half\";\n   212\t  - a passage id in inputs;\n   213\t  - a trillion outcome.\n   214\t\n   215\t**[52] Additions to Part A.**\n   216\t- **A1 adds:**\n   217\t  - the remainder company − Data Center = the rest;\n   218\t  - \"US$10 billion\", \"$96.2bn\", \"roughly twice\", \"between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion\";\n   219\t  - \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" as a range and as a midpoint;\n   220\t  - a point Data Center forecast beside a company range (S3 holds);\n   221\t  - a final range estimate resting on a computed point of the same quantity (S4 holds).\n   222\t- **A2 adds:**\n   223\t  - Data Center × 1x added to company (S8);\n   224\t  - an estimate resting on both part and whole (recorded, not refused, and the record asserted);\n   225\t  - two Data Center Q4 figures on two paths (S4);\n   226\t  - Q3 guidance used directly beside a Q3 forecast (S4);\n   227\t  - an estimate itself marked as the reference (S5);\n   228\t  - a computed reference with basis forecast (S5);\n   229\t  - a declared value within tolerance whose recomputed value crosses the rung edge (S6 on the recomputed value);\n   230\t  - a part whose recomputed lowest value exceeds the whole's recomputed highest, while its declared value does not (S3);\n   231\t  - sum($108B, 2%) (S2 unit);\n   232\t  - division by [-1, 1] (S2);\n   233\t  - [-3, -2] × [2, 4] declared other than [-12, -4] (S2);\n   234\t  - a source as the outcome (S6);\n   235\t  - a figure id equal to a passage id (S7);\n   236\t  - \"plus or minus 2%\" copied as \"2%\" and used as +2% for the range (S1);\n   237\t  - a euro amount (S1).\n   238\t- **A3:** each mistake must be refused by the schema or **actually refused** by its stated rule (asserted). \"first half\" is refused by\n   239\t  S8 when summed. \"1.05 USD trillion\" fails the schema, and \"1050 USD bn\" passes.\n   240\t- **A4, the end to end rehearsal.** One v13s reply goes through the first attempt, the separate-arm reconciliation, the card render and\n   241\t  recovery. For each named defect, the defective answer fails its rule while its paired correct answer reaches the reader's card. The\n   242\t  old run's `results.json` reproduces from its journals with the ledger snapshot saved inside it.\n   243\t\n   244\t**[52] Readers.**\n   245\t- **What they get.** Both readers get the same complete shown passages, with the writer's identity and the checker's decision masked.\n   246\t- **What they mark, on every first reply:**\n   247\t  - whether each forecast is supported, naming the passage or assumption that puts it in its band;\n   248\t  - any of D737's five defects;\n   249\t  - broken structure.\n   250\t- **The wording review is separate.** Refusals are revealed only after that marking, for the review of whether the request states\n   251\t  the rule.\n   252\t- **An accepted reply with a named defect** (marked by both readers, or by the third reader) is a stop.\n   253\t- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\n   254\t\n   255\t**Part B, blind writers ($0 at the provider).**\n   256\t- **The writers.** Each of the six cases is written once by a fresh Claude subagent and once by a fresh read-only Codex session, 12\n   257\t  sessions in all. Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema.\n   258\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   259\t- **The readers.** Two readers who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) grade **every**\n   260\t  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n   261\t  - for each refusal, whether the exact request states the condition (the sentence quoted) and whether the answer was right;\n   262\t  - for each accepted answer, whether each forecast is supported, and whether its recorded conversions or estimates decide its band.\n   263\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements.\n   264\t- **Usable** means accepted, publishable by `publish_decision`, and supported by the readers. For the joint case, both forecasts must\n   265\t  be supported in one reply.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   268\t- an A1 form refused;\n   269\t- an A2 defect not refused by its rule;\n   270\t- an A3 mistake neither impossible nor stated;\n   271\t- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a right answer;\n   272\t- an INCOMPLETE run;\n   273\t- a case with zero usable first replies across both writers;\n   274\t- the joint case without both supported forecasts in one reply.\n   275\t\n   276\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n   277\tD725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | sed -n '157,203p'; tail -n 5 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; sed -n '442,464p' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; rg -n '## D73[789]' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'def _shared|run.rate|benchmark|shared.quantit|S[1-5]' calc_small.py render_v13.py judging-v13s.txt s4_census.py && nl -ba s4_census.py && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '540,730p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | sed -n '157,203p'; tail -n 5 docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md; sed -n '442,464p' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; rg -n '## D73[789]' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"   157\t    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n   158\t      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n   159\t    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n   160\t      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n   161\t      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n   162\t      what was measured.\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n| 37 | 11-05 | Alex：\"为什么没过？…你确定是正确的rejection 吗？你又下去看trace吗？\"；\"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信下一步你要跑什么才能收敛\"。普查全部 7 次读法、606 次文件读（`census_all_reads.py`，$0） | 第三版 0 个错数、156 次读漏 3 次（全是资本开支）；第二版 2 个错数；第四版漏 24 次（5 月）、16 次（8 月）。我自己判错的：6 个对的数被我的引用检查拒（漏星号、跳格），8 月\"只认本季\"规则错，第 13 轮 3 条\"有争议\"的数是标准答案自己用的行，第三版全过后又改第四版 | 收敛方案：提示词定为第三版不再改；修引用检查（$0 重放全部 606 次读核对）；在 8 月财报上用第三版读一次（约 8 美分，只有\"收错数\"能叫停）；然后做进生产 |\n| 38 | 11-05 | Alex：\"好，按这三步做，你不要再搬石头砸自己脚了，你要收敛上生产\"。第 1 步：引用检查搬进正式代码 `src/hivemind/baseline.py`，加\"行名是报告的、数在同一行就存原文那一整行\"的修复（漏脚注记号、跳格）；对照 36 例全对，三个弄坏的版本各抓出 1 个错；重放全部存下的读法，改变的正好是那 6 个被拒的对数。第 2 步：第三版加修好的检查读 8 月留出财报 132 次（0.096 美元），两人分类 | 第 1 步：只改了那 6 条。第 2 步过：44 个数 0 个错（3 条\"剩余资本开支承诺 70 亿\"两人不一致，对着原文定为\"自己的量，别的期间\"）；v10 3/3、v12 2/3 | 读法定为第三版加修好的检查，不再改；第 3 步写正式代码 |\n| 39 | 11-05 | 第 3 步：做进正式系统（设计四张表，Codex 审 3 轮：第 1 轮 4 条严重、第 2 轮 2 条严重，都改；第 3 轮无严重问题） | 建成 `baseline.run` + poc-v116 + baseline-v3 提示词文件；$0 核对：44 份请求和验证时逐字节相同，3 次存下的回答走正式代码写出的记录完全一样，第一天判断正好判这些题，网页从开始跟踪那天可见；新测试 12 条、6 个弄坏版本都抓出、全套 272 条通过；设计页和 PRD 00/03/04/05/06 更新，`docs/check.py` 通过 | 交 Alex：要不要跑 14 天三遍完整验收（约 5 美元） |\n| 40 | 11-05 | Alex：\"跑验收\"。14 天三遍完整验收：poc-v116（开始跟踪时读财报）三遍、当天重跑 poc-v108 三遍作对照，冻结尺子打分（事先锁定 `acceptance-design.md`）；电脑中途在电池上睡了约 5 小时，6 遍被暂停，插电后自己接着跑完 | **全过**：v10、v12 各 3/3（对照从没判过）；合计 53 对 45（满分 57，+8）；唯一少两次的 v17（0/3 对 2/3）两人都认定不是起点数：同一份抽取请求，新配置两遍碰巧留下\"中国份额归零\"那句，对照没留；约 4.79 美元 | 交 Alex：poc-v116 是否成为默认 |\n| 41 | 11-05 | Alex：\"都做\"（poc-v116 设成默认；提交代码、开 PR）。默认配置改成 poc-v116，默认的条目数据库换成带财报网页原文的那一份（旧的没有原文，新默认会在它上面拒跑）；测试按用户的用法补上：不给种子题就拒绝，给了就先读财报再跑第一天 | 测试全过，5 个弄坏的版本都抓出；配置的设置和验收三遍记下的校验码一致（只改了配置里的注释）；花钱前的检查脚本在新配置的运行数据上照常跑完，但不复查读财报那一步的引用检查（记为未补的缺口）；D366 | 提交、开 PR；下一类问题等 Alex |\n- **Missed:**\n  - P2's proxy clause: total capex is used for data-center capex without being named a proxy, which old also misses;\n  - V: the rebuilt control's v47 was unsupported, and a reconstruction effect is not ruled out.\n- **Not shown:** that the merge answers better. The old answers were already supported, and the capex band was set by 09-06 history.\n- **Next:** superseded by D681, below.\n\n**The one next task: re-run the v13s blind test once with fresh writers and readers, after the reader calibration on Alex's rulings (D740, D741).**\n\n- **The first blind test stopped** (D740, `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md`):\n  - 12 of 12 first answers were schema-valid, and 11 of 12 passed every check.\n  - **Stop 1:** S1 read \"from $35 billion to $170 billion\" as a range. This was Claude's change, now fixed: an \"X to Y\" after \"from\" is a\n    change between two values.\n  - **Stop 2:** the readers marked a guidance-implied growth benchmark as \"two forecasts of one quarter\".\n- **Alex ruled the readers' boundary questions** (D741):\n  - a guidance-implied growth benchmark is not a defect;\n  - consensus total × a past quarter's share is not the market's reference, and S5 now refuses it;\n  - a total used for a narrower quantity must state its deduction or a supported bound.\n- **Checks:** cases 104 of 104, mutants 40 of 40, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the old harness unchanged.\n- **The reader calibration on six stored boundary replies** (`calibration-D741/`) runs before the re-run.\n- **A clean re-run** goes to Alex as the paid re-send request.\n\nThe record before D740:\n\n11236:## D737 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: shrink judging-v13's format to the checks that stop the named defects, then a $0 blind test\n11258:## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n11290:## D739 · 2026-11-22 · v13s built and rehearsed; council round 54's findings fixed; the blind test runs, and its result goes to Council B\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'def _shared|run.rate|benchmark|shared.quantit|S[1-5]' calc_small.py render_v13.py judging-v13s.txt s4_census.py && nl -ba s4_census.py && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '540,730p'\"","aggregated_output":"judging-v13s.txt:4:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\njudging-v13s.txt:7:- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\ns4_census.py:25:            s4 = [e for e in res['cur'][0] if e[0] == 'S4']\ns4_census.py:28:            print(f'{folder[-13:]:13} {name:5} {w:7} {flag:7} cur={res[\"cur\"][1]} fix={res[\"fix\"][1]} S4cur={s4} errs_fix={res[\"fix\"][0]}'[:400])\nrender_v13.py:88:               'What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.')\nrender_v13.py:154:                            'point a final range rests on, are fine). A growth rate computed from guidance may serve as a stated benchmark for an estimate. '\ncalc_small.py:2:the five named defects (S1 a number misread, S2 wrong arithmetic, S3/S8 a part above its whole or added into it, S4 two forecasts of one\ncalc_small.py:3:quarter, S5 an own estimate passed off as the market's) and for broken structure (S6, S7), and only where the calculation's own\ncalc_small.py:162:    `reference`; marker errors go in `decode_errors` (S5, S6, S7). An empty string counts as absent.\"\"\"\ncalc_small.py:199:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: needs exactly one of a figure marked as its reference or reference_unavailable'))\ncalc_small.py:460:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: evidence_id {ev} is not a shown passage'))\ncalc_small.py:467:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is part of the longer expression \"{inside[0]}\" in {ev}; copy it whole' if inside else\ncalc_small.py:472:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not a US-dollar amount, a percentage or double, triple or twice, as written'))\ncalc_small.py:475:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is in {parsed[\"unit\"]}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\ncalc_small.py:485:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" gives {want} {parsed[\"unit\"]}'))\ncalc_small.py:496:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\ncalc_small.py:509:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} cannot take inputs in {\", \".join(figs[i][\"unit\"] for i in ins)}{hint}'))\ncalc_small.py:512:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} of these inputs gives {unit}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\ncalc_small.py:520:                errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: divides by a figure whose value can be zero'))\ncalc_small.py:523:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: a share takes a percentage from 0 to 100'))\ncalc_small.py:527:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\ncalc_small.py:537:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: declared {declared} but {op} of {\", \".join(ins)} gives {got_text}'))\ncalc_small.py:580:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on an estimate'))\ncalc_small.py:582:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on a forecast'))\ncalc_small.py:584:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} has no quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure'))\ncalc_small.py:589:                    errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} carries the reported {\", \".join(carried)} of another period to '\ncalc_small.py:615:def _shared(family, figs, outcomes):\ncalc_small.py:627:            errors.append(('S4', f'{\" | \".join(k)}: {len(ids)} forecasts ({\", \".join(sorted(ids))}); compute it once and use that figure'))\ncalc_small.py:647:                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\ncalc_small.py:651:                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\", \".join(bypass)} for {\" | \".join(key)} beside its forecast figure {node[0]}'))\ncalc_small.py:676:                errors.append(('S3', f'{label}: the part {part} ({pv[0]:g}–{pv[1]:g}) exceeds the whole {whole} ({wv[0]:g}–{wv[1]:g})'))\ncalc_small.py:773:            errors.append(('S2', f'{f[\"id\"]}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n     1\t\"\"\"Census: every stored v13s first reply (both blind tests) through the current checker and the rate-stop copy; print any change.\"\"\"\n     2\timport importlib.util, json, sys\n     3\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     4\tR = Path('/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers')\n     5\tsys.path.insert(0, str(R)); sys.path.insert(0, '/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src')\n     6\timport calc_small as cur\n     7\tspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('calc_fix', sys.argv[1]); fix = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(fix)\n     8\timport blind_test as bt\n     9\tfrom hivemind import model\n    10\tjudged = changed = 0\n    11\tfor folder in ('judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22', 'judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22'):\n    12\t    out = Path('/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data') / folder\n    13\t    for name in bt.CASES:\n    14\t        it = json.loads((out / name / 'item.json').read_text())\n    15\t        for w in bt.WRITERS:\n    16\t            data = bt.first_json((out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').read_text())\n    17\t            if model.check_schema(data, it['schema']):\n    18\t                print(folder, name, w, 'schema error'); continue\n    19\t            res = {}\n    20\t            for label, mod in (('cur', cur), ('fix', fix)):\n    21\t                calc = mod.from_answer(data, it['family'])\n    22\t                r = mod.check(it['family'], it['passages'], calc)\n    23\t                res[label] = (sorted(map(tuple, r['errors'])), mod.publish_decision(it['family'], calc, r))\n    24\t            judged += 1\n    25\t            s4 = [e for e in res['cur'][0] if e[0] == 'S4']\n    26\t            flag = 'CHANGED' if res['cur'] != res['fix'] else 'same'\n    27\t            changed += flag == 'CHANGED'\n    28\t            print(f'{folder[-13:]:13} {name:5} {w:7} {flag:7} cur={res[\"cur\"][1]} fix={res[\"fix\"][1]} S4cur={s4} errs_fix={res[\"fix\"][0]}'[:400])\n    29\tprint(f'judged {judged}, changed {changed}')\n   540\t\n   541\tdef _overlaps_band(interval, band):\n   542\t    lo, hi = interval\n   543\t    b_lo = band[0] if band[0] is not None else -math.inf\n   544\t    b_hi = band[1] if band[1] is not None else math.inf\n   545\t    if lo == hi:\n   546\t        return b_lo <= lo < b_hi\n   547\t    return lo < b_hi and hi >= b_lo\n   548\t\n   549\t\n   550\tdef _answers(family, figs, calc, answers):\n   551\t    errors, outcomes = [], {}\n   552\t    members = family['members']\n   553\t    for a in answers:\n   554\t        v = a.get('view_id')\n   555\t        member = members.get(v)\n   556\t        if member is None or a.get('shape') == 'unresolved':\n   557\t            continue\n   558\t        out = figs.get(a.get('outcome'))\n   559\t        if out is None:\n   560\t            continue                                       # reported by from_answer\n   561\t        if out['kind'] not in ('computed', 'estimate') or out['basis'] != 'forecast':\n   562\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: its outcome {out[\"id\"]} must be your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast)'))\n   563\t        if _key(out['what']) != _key(member['what']):\n   564\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} measures {\" | \".join(_key(out[\"what\"]))}, not the question\\'s {\" | \".join(_key(member[\"what\"]))}'))\n   565\t        if out['unit'] != member.get('unit', 'USD bn'):\n   566\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} is in {out[\"unit\"]}, not {member.get(\"unit\", \"USD bn\")}'))\n   567\t        outcomes[v] = out['id']\n   568\t        rung = a.get('rung')\n   569\t        if not isinstance(rung, int) or isinstance(rung, bool) or not 1 <= rung <= len(member['bands']):\n   570\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung!r} is not one of 1..{len(member[\"bands\"])}'))\n   571\t            continue\n   572\t        interval = calc.interval(out['id'])\n   573\t        if interval is not None and not _overlaps_band(interval, member['bands'][rung - 1]):\n   574\t            shown = f'{interval[0]:g}' if interval[0] == interval[1] else f'{interval[0]:g}–{interval[1]:g}'\n   575\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung} {member[\"bands\"][rung - 1]} does not hold its outcome {shown} as recomputed'))\n   576\t        ref = (a.get('reference') or {}).get('figure')\n   577\t        if ref in figs:\n   578\t            chain = {ref} | _beneath(ref, figs)\n   579\t            if any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'estimate' for g in chain):\n   580\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on an estimate'))\n   581\t            elif any(figs[g]['basis'] == 'forecast' for g in chain):\n   582\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on a forecast'))\n   583\t            elif not any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'source' and figs[g]['basis'] in EXTERNAL for g in chain):\n   584\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} has no quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure'))\n   585\t            else:                                          # [D741] a past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period\n   586\t                carried = sorted(g for g in chain if figs[g]['basis'] == 'reported' and figs[g]['unit'] in ('%', 'x')\n   587\t                                 and figs[g]['what']['period'] != figs[ref]['what']['period'])\n   588\t                if carried:\n   589\t                    errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} carries the reported {\", \".join(carried)} of another period to '\n   590\t                                         f'{figs[ref][\"what\"][\"period\"]}; that makes it our comparison estimate, not the market\\'s reference'))\n   591\t    return errors, outcomes\n   592\t\n   593\t\n   594\tdef _adopted(outcomes, figs):\n   595\t    \"\"\"[54] The figures the outcomes adopt: each reached from an outcome (inputs and rests_on) with no figure of its own quantity and\n   596\t    period between them. A figure beneath another of its own quantity and period (a point a final range rests on) is its basis, not a\n   597\t    second value.\"\"\"\n   598\t    tops, seen_ids = set(), set()\n   599\t\n   600\t    def walk(fid, keys):\n   601\t        if fid not in figs or (fid, keys) in seen_ids:\n   602\t            return\n   603\t        seen_ids.add((fid, keys))\n   604\t        f = figs[fid]\n   605\t        k = _key(f['what'])\n   606\t        if k not in keys:\n   607\t            tops.add(fid)\n   608\t        for r in list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs]:\n   609\t            walk(r, keys | {k})\n   610\t    for out in outcomes.values():\n   611\t        walk(out, frozenset())\n   612\t    return tops\n   613\t\n   614\t\n   615\tdef _shared(family, figs, outcomes):\n   616\t    errors = []\n   617\t    by_key = {}\n   618\t    for fid in _adopted(outcomes, figs):                   # [54] forecasts the outcomes adopt; an unused alternative is not counted\n   619\t        if figs[fid]['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   620\t            by_key.setdefault(_key(figs[fid]['what']), []).append(fid)\n   621\t    nodes = {}\n   622\t    for fid, f in figs.items():\n   623\t        if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   624\t            nodes.setdefault(_key(f['what']), []).append(fid)\n   625\t    for k, ids in sorted(by_key.items()):\n   626\t        if len(ids) > 1:\n   627\t            errors.append(('S4', f'{\" | \".join(k)}: {len(ids)} forecasts ({\", \".join(sorted(ids))}); compute it once and use that figure'))\n   628\t    for want in family.get('shared', []):\n   629\t        key = _key(want)\n   630\t        node = by_key.get(key) or nodes.get(key, [])\n   631\t        for v, out in sorted(outcomes.items()):\n   632\t            reached, stack, seen_ids = [], [out], set()\n   633\t            while stack:\n   634\t                fid = stack.pop()\n   635\t                if fid in seen_ids or fid not in figs:\n   636\t                    continue\n   637\t                seen_ids.add(fid)\n   638\t                f = figs[fid]\n   639\t                if _key(f['what']) == key:\n   640\t                    reached.append(fid)\n   641\t                    if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   642\t                        continue                           # through the forecast figure: what lies beneath it is its basis\n   643\t                stack.extend(f.get('inputs') or [])\n   644\t            if not reached:\n   645\t                continue\n   646\t            if len(node) != 1:\n   647\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\n   648\t                continue\n   649\t            bypass = sorted(r for r in reached if r != node[0])      # the walk never descends below the forecast figure\n   650\t            if bypass:\n   651\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\", \".join(bypass)} for {\" | \".join(key)} beside its forecast figure {node[0]}'))\n   652\t    return errors\n   653\t\n   654\t\n   655\tdef _part_whole(family, figs, calc, outcomes):\n   656\t    errors = []\n   657\t    members = family['members']\n   658\t    for rel in family.get('relations', []):\n   659\t        if rel.get('type') != 'part_of' or rel['part'] not in outcomes or rel['whole'] not in outcomes:\n   660\t            continue\n   661\t        p_what, w_what = members[rel['part']]['what'], members[rel['whole']]['what']\n   662\t        pairs = [(outcomes[rel['part']], outcomes[rel['whole']], 'outcome')]\n   663\t        for period in family.get('compare_periods', []):\n   664\t            if period == p_what['period']:\n   665\t                continue\n   666\t            p_key = (p_what['population'], p_what['measure'], p_what['scope'], period)\n   667\t            w_key = (w_what['population'], w_what['measure'], w_what['scope'], period)\n   668\t            for p in _tops(outcomes[rel['part']], p_key, figs):\n   669\t                for w in _tops(outcomes[rel['whole']], w_key, figs):\n   670\t                    pairs.append((p, w, period))\n   671\t        for part, whole, label in pairs:\n   672\t            pv, wv = calc.interval(part), calc.interval(whole)\n   673\t            if pv is None or wv is None:\n   674\t                continue\n   675\t            if not calc.fits(part, whole):\n   676\t                errors.append(('S3', f'{label}: the part {part} ({pv[0]:g}–{pv[1]:g}) exceeds the whole {whole} ({wv[0]:g}–{wv[1]:g})'))\n   677\t    return errors\n   678\t\n   679\t\n   680\tdef _sums(family, figs):\n   681\t    errors = []\n   682\t    scopes = family.get('part_scopes', [])\n   683\t    for f in figs.values():\n   684\t        if f['kind'] != 'computed' or f['operation'] not in ('sum', 'difference'):\n   685\t            continue\n   686\t        fid, ins = f['id'], [figs[i] for i in f['inputs']]\n   687\t        for ps in scopes:\n   688\t            same = [i for i in ins if (i['what']['population'], i['what']['measure']) == (ps['population'], ps['measure'])]\n   689\t            parts = [i for i in same if i['what']['scope'] == ps['part']]\n   690\t            wholes = [i for i in same if i['what']['scope'] == ps['whole']]\n   691\t            if not parts or not wholes:\n   692\t                continue\n   693\t            if f['operation'] == 'sum':\n   694\t                if any(_overlap(p['what']['period'], w['what']['period']) for p in parts for w in wholes):\n   695\t                    errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: adds {ps[\"part\"]} into {ps[\"whole\"]} for the same period, which already includes it'))\n   696\t            elif not (ins[0]['what']['scope'] == ps['whole'] and ins[1]['what']['scope'] == ps['part']\n   697\t                      and ins[0]['what']['period'] == ins[1]['what']['period'] == f['what']['period']\n   698\t                      and f['what']['scope'] not in (ps['part'], ps['whole'])):\n   699\t                errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: a difference may take {ps[\"part\"]} from {ps[\"whole\"]} of the same period only to give the rest'))\n   700\t        group = ins + [f]\n   701\t        if len({_quantity(g['what']) for g in group}) != 1:\n   702\t            continue\n   703\t        periods = [g['what']['period'] for g in group]\n   704\t        inputs_periods = periods[:-1]\n   705\t        if f['operation'] == 'sum' and len(set(inputs_periods)) < len(inputs_periods):\n   706\t            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: adds one quantity for the same period more than once ({\", \".join(inputs_periods)})'))\n   707\t            continue\n   708\t        if len(set(periods)) == 1:\n   709\t            continue\n   710\t        parsed = [_period(p) for p in periods]\n   711\t        if not any(parsed):\n   712\t            continue\n   713\t        if not all(parsed):\n   714\t            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: when periods of one quantity are added or subtracted, write each as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or '\n   715\t                                 f'FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise); got {\", \".join(periods)}'))\n   716\t            continue\n   717\t        years = {p[0] for p in parsed}\n   718\t        *parts_p, out = parsed\n   719\t        if len(years) != 1:\n   720\t            ok = False\n   721\t        elif f['operation'] == 'sum':\n   722\t            total = [q for p in parts_p for q in p[1]]\n   723\t            ok = len(total) == len(set(total)) and set(total) == out[1]\n   724\t        else:\n   725\t            ok = parts_p[1][1] <= parts_p[0][1] and parts_p[0][1] - parts_p[1][1] == out[1]\n   726\t        if not ok:\n   727\t            sign = ' + ' if f['operation'] == 'sum' else ' − '\n   728\t            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: {sign.join(periods[:-1])} does not make up {periods[-1]}'))\n   729\t    return errors\n   730\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11236,11290p'; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,142p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11236,11290p'; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '1,142p'\"","aggregated_output":" 11236\t## D737 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: shrink judging-v13's format to the checks that stop the named defects, then a $0 blind test\n 11237\t\n 11238\t- **The question** (after D736): \"判数字新版本的修补连续三轮审查都在冒新规则（填写说明将从 1 万字涨到约 2 万字，每个答案约 16 个数、每个数要同时满足几十条规则）。Codex 主张修完再审最后一轮；我主张现在改结构。下一步怎么走？\"\n 11239\t  Options:\n 11240\t  - 砍成小格式再盲测 (recommended): keep only the checks that stop five defects (a number misread, wrong arithmetic, a part\n 11241\t    above its whole or added into it, two forecasts of one quarter, an own estimate passed off as the market's); the other rules\n 11242\t    become assumptions shown to readers; council, then the $0 blind test, then a request for the paid re-send;\n 11243\t  - 按原计划再修一轮 (Codex's position);\n 11244\t  - 关掉这条线.\n 11245\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22), the recommended option (\"…成小格式再盲测 (推荐)\").\n 11246\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11247\t  - The judging-v13 format shrinks. Code refuses an answer only for the five defects above and for broken structure (links that do\n 11248\t    not resolve, a cycle, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the question's quantity, a rung that does not hold the\n 11249\t    outcome).\n 11250\t  - Each such rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of DESIGN.md's checks becomes an assumption\n 11251\t    recorded and shown to the readers, never a refusal.\n 11252\t  - The council reviews within that scope. A counterexample that lets none of the five defects through is a reader's matter, not a\n 11253\t    finding.\n 11254\t  - Then the $0 blind test (REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5, with round 51's reader fix). A clean result returns to Alex for the paid re-send under\n 11255\t    D725's lines.\n 11256\t- **Superseded:** REPAIR-DESIGN.md v2's full restatement (D735's finish line), replaced by the small format.\n 11257\t\n 11258\t## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n 11259\t\n 11260\t- **Rounds 52 and 53** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r52.md`, `codex-r53.md`) reviewed the small format within D737's\n 11261\t  scope. Each closing rule written for an indirect form of a named defect produced new counterexamples in both directions:\n 11262\t  - \"uses the part\" lineage refuses a correct sum of two Data Center quarters, each computed as a share of company revenue;\n 11263\t  - \"one figure per quantity\" refuses two outside estimates averaged into one starting point;\n 11264\t  - multiplying by 1, relabelling a basis, or routing through an estimate's judgement still passes.\n 11265\t- **Settled (how D737's rule is built; D737 says code refuses only for the five defects, not that code catches every instance):**\n 11266\t  - Code refuses a named defect where the calculation's own structure decides it without reading intent:\n 11267\t    - **S8:** a sum whose inputs include the part's quantity and the whole's quantity themselves.\n 11268\t    - **S4:** two figures with basis forecast for one quantity and period, or a figure of a listed shared quantity used by an outcome\n 11269\t      on a path that bypasses its forecast figure.\n 11270\t    - **S3:** a part above its whole, compared with paired ends when the part is computed from the whole and lowest against highest\n 11271\t      otherwise, on recomputed values. A share takes 0 to 100 percent.\n 11272\t    - **S5:** an estimate or forecast at or beneath the marked reference.\n 11273\t    - **S1, S2, S6, S7** as written.\n 11274\t  - Indirect forms (a multiplier of one, a relabelled basis, an estimate's judgement) are recorded for readers. They are never\n 11275\t    refused.\n 11276\t  - **The readers** in the blind test and the gate mark the five defects on every reply. In the blind test, an accepted reply with\n 11277\t    a reader-marked defect is a stop, so whether real writers use these forms is measured, not argued.\n 11278\t  - **Round 53's false refusals and test conflicts are fixed in the design:**\n 11279\t    - DC Q3 + Q4;\n 11280\t    - averaged outside inputs;\n 11281\t    - cycles through `rests_on`;\n 11282\t    - a figure used twice taking one value in range arithmetic;\n 11283\t    - the \"to\", \"+/-\" and \"±\" spellings;\n 11284\t    - one-source \"±\";\n 11285\t    - the separate arm carrying the part-of relation for S8;\n 11286\t    - readers given the whole reply and every shown passage.\n 11287\t- **Next:** build `calc_small.py`, the v13s schema, header and plan, and the A1–A4 cases and mutants. Council round 54 reviews the code\n 11288\t  and the end-to-end rehearsal. Then comes the blind test.\n 11289\t\n 11290\t## D739 · 2026-11-22 · v13s built and rehearsed; council round 54's findings fixed; the blind test runs, and its result goes to Council B\n     1\t# judging-v13 small format (v13s): code refuses only the five defects and broken structure (D737)\n     2\t\n     3\tAlex (D737) chose to shrink the format. Code refuses an answer only for these five defects:\n     4\t- a number misread;\n     5\t- wrong arithmetic;\n     6\t- a part above its whole, or added into it;\n     7\t- two forecasts of one quarter;\n     8\t- an own estimate passed off as the market's.\n     9\t\n    10\tIt also refuses broken structure. Each rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of `DESIGN.md`'s\n    11\tchecks becomes something the readers see, not a refusal.\n    12\t\n    13\t**Review scope (D737):** a counterexample that lets none of the five defects through, and breaks no structure, is a reader's matter,\n    14\tnot a finding. **Out of scope:**\n    15\t- the frozen ruler and `poc-v118`;\n    16\t- D725's pass lines and D731's harness rules;\n    17\t- the old run and its checker `calc_check.py`, which stay as they are, so the old run stays reproducible.\n    18\t\n    19\t## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n    20\t\n    21\t| Today's rule (DESIGN.md) | v13s |\n    22\t|---|---|\n    23\t| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n    24\t| scenarios, scenario membership, `range` summaries (C4) | one forecast per shared quantity; an outcome may be a range |\n    25\t| relations `time_aggregation` / `accounting_parts` / `stated_in_addition` / `overlap_unknown`, and `disputed` | periods of one quantity must make up the output's period (S8); an uncertain overlap is written as an estimate with its assumption |\n    26\t| bridges and `converts` (C8) | a conversion between quantities is computed or estimated as the analyst chooses; code records each computed figure whose inputs measure another quantity, and readers judge it |\n    27\t| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n    28\t| `adjusts` | an estimate rests on the figure it changes |\n    29\t| C9 (same base), C10 (earlier forecasts) | none of the gate's families declares a same base; the request shows no earlier forecast (D728) |\n    30\t| the exact-expression grammar (C1) | the number as written (`quote_number`) plus a qualifier from a closed list |\n    31\t\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n    33\t\n    34\t**[52] Shared definitions.**\n    35\t- **Uses.** A figure *uses* another when the other is among its `inputs`, at any depth. An estimate's `rests_on` is a judgement, not\n    36\t  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n    37\t- **Recomputed values.** The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range. S3, S4 and S6 compare\n    38\t  recomputed values. S2's tolerance applies only between a declared value and its recomputed value.\n    39\t\n    40\t| # | Defect | Rule [52] (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    41\t|---|---|---|---|\n    42\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number exactly as the quote writes it (`quote_number`). The number is in US dollars ($, US$) with its scale (trillion, billion, bn, million), or is a percentage, or is one of the words double, triple or twice. The value equals it, with money in billions. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) goes in `qualifier`. A written range (\"$3–4 billion\", \"between $3 billion and $4 billion\") gives low and high. \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\" is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value. Money in another currency cannot be a source. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    43\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). Units are those `operation` states for each operation; a sum or difference adds one unit. A range input gives a range: both extremes are computed first, and a midpoint may be declared as value. Division by a range that contains zero is refused. | `operation` |\n    44\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, and for the outcomes, the part's lowest recomputed value used for that period may not exceed the whole's highest. | the family header |\n    45\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity, each period is written FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and together they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers. | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    46\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n    47\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    48\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    49\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique and never equal a passage id. `inputs` name figures; `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure uses itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, rung null, `reference_unavailable` null and no marked figure. | the field descriptions |\n    50\t\n    51\t**[52] Operation units (stated in `operation`):**\n    52\t- sum and difference: one unit in, the same out.\n    53\t- product: USD bn × x → USD bn; x × x → x; % × x → %; never USD bn × %.\n    54\t- ratio: same unit → x; USD bn ÷ x → USD bn.\n    55\t- share(whole, percent): whole's unit.\n    56\t- growth(old, new): same unit → %.\n    57\t- apply_growth(base, rate %): base's unit.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 2-old. The rules as first written (superseded by the revision above)\n    60\t\n    61\t| # | Defect | Rule (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    62\t|---|---|---|---|\n    63\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number as the quote writes it (`quote_number`): with currency, scale or percent sign, or the words double, triple or twice. The figure's value equals it, in billions of dollars for money. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) is given in `qualifier`, and none is given when none is written. A written range gives low and high. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    64\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A range input gives a range: low and high, or their midpoint as value. Money is never multiplied by a percentage (use share). Arity is as `operation` states. | `operation` |\n    65\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, the part's forecast figure is at most the whole's: each end of the part's value or range is at most the same end of the whole's. The part's outcome is at most the whole's outcome. | the family header; `outcome_for` |\n    66\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's (Relations). When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity written as FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    67\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | For each shared quantity the request lists, at most one figure has basis forecast. When a member's outcome uses that quantity, exactly one does, and every figure of that quantity the outcome uses is reached only through it (the forecast itself may be computed from guidance or consensus). | the family header; `basis` |\n    68\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | A figure marked in `reference_for` has, at any depth beneath it, no estimate and no forecast, and at least one source of basis consensus, guidance or third_party. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    69\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    70\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique. `inputs` name figures and `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure depends on itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, no rung, no marked figure and no reference. | the field descriptions |\n    71\t\n    72\t**Recorded for readers, never refused:**\n    73\t- each estimate's assumption;\n    74\t- each computed figure whose money inputs measure another population, measure or scope than its output;\n    75\t- each growth applied to a base of another period;\n    76\t- each source qualifier.\n    77\t\n    78\t## 3. The wire schema, word for word (examples from no covered company, AGENTS §6)\n    79\t\n    80\t**[52] These descriptions replace the ones in the table below:**\n    81\t\n    82\t| Field | Description [52] |\n    83\t|---|---|\n    84\t| `figures` (array) | \"The family's calculation. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. Figures name each other by id; no figure may use itself.\" |\n    85\t| `id` | \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\" |\n    86\t| `quote_number` | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion'), given as low and high. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\" |\n    87\t| `qualifier` | enum adds `near`; \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n    88\t| `operation` | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; USD bn ÷ x gives USD bn); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure built on the part's quantity to one built on the whole's (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole only to give the rest. When its inputs are periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n    89\t| `outcome_for` | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n    90\t| `reference_for` | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure.\" |\n    91\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\" |\n    92\t\n    93\t**[52] The family header is rendered from the family's data** (no member id is fixed in code):\n    94\t- \"Relations: <part> is part of <whole>. For each of <compare periods> and for the outcomes, the part's lowest value may not exceed the\n    95\t  whole's highest. A sum never adds the part into the whole.\"\n    96\t- \"Shared quantities: …\" (unchanged list).\n    97\t- \"Outcome keys …, in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n    98\t- For O: \"<key>: the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it.\"\n    99\t\n   100\t**figures[]**\n   101\t\n   102\t| Field | Type | Description |\n   103\t|---|---|---|\n   104\t| (array) | | \"The family's calculation: every number an answer rests on, once. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\" |\n   105\t| `id` | string | \"Short and unique, such as F1.\" |\n   106\t| `what` | object | \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\" |\n   107\t| `what.measure` | string | \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\" |\n   108\t| `what.period` | string | \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\" |\n   109\t| `kind` | enum source, computed, estimate | \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\" |\n   110\t| `basis` | enum reported, guidance, consensus, third_party, forecast | \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\" |\n   111\t| `value` | number or null | \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\" |\n   112\t| `low`, `high` | number or null | \"A range, low at most high.\" |\n   113\t| `unit` | enum USD bn, %, x | \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\" |\n   114\t| `evidence_id` | enum of passage ids, or null | \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\" |\n   115\t| `quote_number` | string or null | \"Source only: the number exactly as the quote writes it, with its currency, scale or percent sign and nothing else, for example '$4.5 billion', '$300 million', '40%', '-3%', 'double'; a written range ('$3–4 billion') in full, given as low and high.\" |\n   116\t| `qualifier` | enum none, more than, over, at least, about, approximately, around, roughly, nearly, almost, up to, less than, below, under, or more, or less; or null | \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\" |\n   117\t| `inputs` | array of strings or null | \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\" |\n   118\t| `operation` | enum sum, difference, product, ratio, share, growth, apply_growth; or null | \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + …; difference(a, b) = a − b; product(a, b, …) = a × b × …, never money by a percentage; ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b; share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100; growth(old, new) = the % change from old to new; apply_growth(base, rate) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). difference, ratio, share, growth and apply_growth take exactly two inputs. A range input gives a range (low and high, or their midpoint as value). The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum or difference never combines the part's quantity with the whole's; when its inputs are periods of one quantity, they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\" |\n   119\t| `basis_text` | string or null | \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\" |\n   120\t| `rests_on` | array of strings or null | \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\" |\n   121\t| `outcome_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: exactly one figure for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n   122\t| `reference_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference: a consensus, guidance or outside figure from a shown passage, or one computed only from such figures and reported ones. No estimate or forecast anywhere beneath it.\" |\n   123\t\n   124\t**answers[]**\n   125\t\n   126\t| Field | Description |\n   127\t|---|---|\n   128\t| `view_id` | (enum, unchanged) |\n   129\t| `shape` | \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why, and nothing else.\" |\n   130\t| `rung` | \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\" |\n   131\t| `reference_unavailable` | \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\" |\n   132\t| `reasons`, `why`, `cites`, `pushes`, `theme_pushes` | unchanged; `why`: \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\" |\n   133\t\n   134\t**The family header (user message)**\n   135\t- \"Relations: v8 is part of v4: for each of FY2027 Q3, FY2027 Q4, FY2027, the part's forecast figure may not exceed the whole's, and\n   136\t  a sum or difference never combines the two.\"\n   137\t- \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure that uses the quantity takes it: …\"\n   138\t- \"Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: …\"\n   139\t\n   140\t**The system text:** one change. \"a quantity the family shares has one forecast per scenario\" becomes \"a quantity the family shares\n   141\thas one forecast\". Every other sentence is unchanged.\n   142\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba s4_census.py && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '560,655p' && rg -n 'def v13s_|benchmark|Shared quantities|run-rate|def check|def _compute' render_v13.py judging-v13s.txt calc_small.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba s4_census.py && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '560,655p' && rg -n 'def v13s_|benchmark|Shared quantities|run-rate|def check|def _compute' render_v13.py judging-v13s.txt calc_small.py\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"Census: every stored v13s first reply (both blind tests) through the current checker and the rate-stop copy; print any change.\"\"\"\n     2\timport importlib.util, json, sys\n     3\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     4\tR = Path('/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers')\n     5\tsys.path.insert(0, str(R)); sys.path.insert(0, '/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src')\n     6\timport calc_small as cur\n     7\tspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('calc_fix', sys.argv[1]); fix = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(fix)\n     8\timport blind_test as bt\n     9\tfrom hivemind import model\n    10\tjudged = changed = 0\n    11\tfor folder in ('judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22', 'judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22'):\n    12\t    out = Path('/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data') / folder\n    13\t    for name in bt.CASES:\n    14\t        it = json.loads((out / name / 'item.json').read_text())\n    15\t        for w in bt.WRITERS:\n    16\t            data = bt.first_json((out / name / 'answers' / f'{w}.json').read_text())\n    17\t            if model.check_schema(data, it['schema']):\n    18\t                print(folder, name, w, 'schema error'); continue\n    19\t            res = {}\n    20\t            for label, mod in (('cur', cur), ('fix', fix)):\n    21\t                calc = mod.from_answer(data, it['family'])\n    22\t                r = mod.check(it['family'], it['passages'], calc)\n    23\t                res[label] = (sorted(map(tuple, r['errors'])), mod.publish_decision(it['family'], calc, r))\n    24\t            judged += 1\n    25\t            s4 = [e for e in res['cur'][0] if e[0] == 'S4']\n    26\t            flag = 'CHANGED' if res['cur'] != res['fix'] else 'same'\n    27\t            changed += flag == 'CHANGED'\n    28\t            print(f'{folder[-13:]:13} {name:5} {w:7} {flag:7} cur={res[\"cur\"][1]} fix={res[\"fix\"][1]} S4cur={s4} errs_fix={res[\"fix\"][0]}'[:400])\n    29\tprint(f'judged {judged}, changed {changed}')\n   560\t            continue                                       # reported by from_answer\n   561\t        if out['kind'] not in ('computed', 'estimate') or out['basis'] != 'forecast':\n   562\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: its outcome {out[\"id\"]} must be your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast)'))\n   563\t        if _key(out['what']) != _key(member['what']):\n   564\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} measures {\" | \".join(_key(out[\"what\"]))}, not the question\\'s {\" | \".join(_key(member[\"what\"]))}'))\n   565\t        if out['unit'] != member.get('unit', 'USD bn'):\n   566\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: outcome {out[\"id\"]} is in {out[\"unit\"]}, not {member.get(\"unit\", \"USD bn\")}'))\n   567\t        outcomes[v] = out['id']\n   568\t        rung = a.get('rung')\n   569\t        if not isinstance(rung, int) or isinstance(rung, bool) or not 1 <= rung <= len(member['bands']):\n   570\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung!r} is not one of 1..{len(member[\"bands\"])}'))\n   571\t            continue\n   572\t        interval = calc.interval(out['id'])\n   573\t        if interval is not None and not _overlaps_band(interval, member['bands'][rung - 1]):\n   574\t            shown = f'{interval[0]:g}' if interval[0] == interval[1] else f'{interval[0]:g}–{interval[1]:g}'\n   575\t            errors.append(('S6', f'{v}: rung {rung} {member[\"bands\"][rung - 1]} does not hold its outcome {shown} as recomputed'))\n   576\t        ref = (a.get('reference') or {}).get('figure')\n   577\t        if ref in figs:\n   578\t            chain = {ref} | _beneath(ref, figs)\n   579\t            if any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'estimate' for g in chain):\n   580\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on an estimate'))\n   581\t            elif any(figs[g]['basis'] == 'forecast' for g in chain):\n   582\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} is or rests on a forecast'))\n   583\t            elif not any(figs[g]['kind'] == 'source' and figs[g]['basis'] in EXTERNAL for g in chain):\n   584\t                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} has no quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure'))\n   585\t            else:                                          # [D741] a past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period\n   586\t                carried = sorted(g for g in chain if figs[g]['basis'] == 'reported' and figs[g]['unit'] in ('%', 'x')\n   587\t                                 and figs[g]['what']['period'] != figs[ref]['what']['period'])\n   588\t                if carried:\n   589\t                    errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: its reference {ref} carries the reported {\", \".join(carried)} of another period to '\n   590\t                                         f'{figs[ref][\"what\"][\"period\"]}; that makes it our comparison estimate, not the market\\'s reference'))\n   591\t    return errors, outcomes\n   592\t\n   593\t\n   594\tdef _adopted(outcomes, figs):\n   595\t    \"\"\"[54] The figures the outcomes adopt: each reached from an outcome (inputs and rests_on) with no figure of its own quantity and\n   596\t    period between them. A figure beneath another of its own quantity and period (a point a final range rests on) is its basis, not a\n   597\t    second value.\"\"\"\n   598\t    tops, seen_ids = set(), set()\n   599\t\n   600\t    def walk(fid, keys):\n   601\t        if fid not in figs or (fid, keys) in seen_ids:\n   602\t            return\n   603\t        seen_ids.add((fid, keys))\n   604\t        f = figs[fid]\n   605\t        k = _key(f['what'])\n   606\t        if k not in keys:\n   607\t            tops.add(fid)\n   608\t        for r in list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs]:\n   609\t            walk(r, keys | {k})\n   610\t    for out in outcomes.values():\n   611\t        walk(out, frozenset())\n   612\t    return tops\n   613\t\n   614\t\n   615\tdef _shared(family, figs, outcomes):\n   616\t    errors = []\n   617\t    by_key = {}\n   618\t    for fid in _adopted(outcomes, figs):                   # [54] forecasts the outcomes adopt; an unused alternative is not counted\n   619\t        if figs[fid]['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   620\t            by_key.setdefault(_key(figs[fid]['what']), []).append(fid)\n   621\t    nodes = {}\n   622\t    for fid, f in figs.items():\n   623\t        if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   624\t            nodes.setdefault(_key(f['what']), []).append(fid)\n   625\t    for k, ids in sorted(by_key.items()):\n   626\t        if len(ids) > 1:\n   627\t            errors.append(('S4', f'{\" | \".join(k)}: {len(ids)} forecasts ({\", \".join(sorted(ids))}); compute it once and use that figure'))\n   628\t    for want in family.get('shared', []):\n   629\t        key = _key(want)\n   630\t        node = by_key.get(key) or nodes.get(key, [])\n   631\t        for v, out in sorted(outcomes.items()):\n   632\t            reached, stack, seen_ids = [], [out], set()\n   633\t            while stack:\n   634\t                fid = stack.pop()\n   635\t                if fid in seen_ids or fid not in figs:\n   636\t                    continue\n   637\t                seen_ids.add(fid)\n   638\t                f = figs[fid]\n   639\t                if _key(f['what']) == key:\n   640\t                    reached.append(fid)\n   641\t                    if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   642\t                        continue                           # through the forecast figure: what lies beneath it is its basis\n   643\t                stack.extend(f.get('inputs') or [])\n   644\t            if not reached:\n   645\t                continue\n   646\t            if len(node) != 1:\n   647\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\" | \".join(key)} but the calculation has {len(node)} forecast figures for it'))\n   648\t                continue\n   649\t            bypass = sorted(r for r in reached if r != node[0])      # the walk never descends below the forecast figure\n   650\t            if bypass:\n   651\t                errors.append(('S4', f'{v}: uses {\", \".join(bypass)} for {\" | \".join(key)} beside its forecast figure {node[0]}'))\n   652\t    return errors\n   653\t\n   654\t\n   655\tdef _part_whole(family, figs, calc, outcomes):\njudging-v13s.txt:4:- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\nrender_v13.py:81:def v13s_schema(member_ids, evidence_ids, theme_ids):\nrender_v13.py:154:                            'point a final range rests on, are fine). A growth rate computed from guidance may serve as a stated benchmark for an estimate. '\nrender_v13.py:161:def v13s_header(family, shown):\nrender_v13.py:177:    lines.append(f'Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: {shared}.')\nrender_v13.py:220:        header = (f'Relations: {rel}.\\nShared quantities, one forecast each per scenario: {shared}.\\n'\ncalc_small.py:764:def check(family, passages, calc):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
