"codex\": \"uphold\",\n   178\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   179\t    },\n   180\t    {\n   181\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n   182\t     \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n   183\t     \"figures\": [\n   184\t      \"F3\",\n   185\t      \"F10\"\n   186\t     ],\n   187\t     \"codex\": \"uphold\",\n   188\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   189\t    }\n   190\t   ],\n   191\t   \"readers_marked\": {\n   192\t    \"codex\": [\n   193\t     [\n   194\t      \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n   195\t      [\n   196\t       \"F3\"\n   197\t      ]\n   198\t     ]\n   199\t    ],\n   200\t    \"claude\": [\n   201\t     [\n   202\t      \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n   203\t      [\n   204\t       \"F3\",\n   205\t       \"F10\"\n   206\t      ]\n   207\t     ]\n   208\t    ]\n   209\t   }\n   210\t  },\n   211\t  {\n   212\t   \"packet\": \"R08\",\n   213\t   \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n   214\t   \"writer\": \"claude\",\n   215\t   \"decision\": \"publish\",\n   216\t   \"supported\": {\n   217\t    \"v8\": [\n   218\t     \"yes\",\n   219\t     \"yes\"\n   220\t    ]\n   221\t   },\n   222\t   \"defects\": [],\n   223\t   \"readers_marked\": {\n   224\t    \"codex\": [],\n   225\t    \"claude\": []\n   226\t   }\n   227\t  },\n   228\t  {\n   229\t   \"packet\": \"R09\",\n   230\t   \"case\": \"P\",\n   231\t   \"writer\": \"claude\",\n   232\t   \"decision\": \"publish\",\n   233\t   \"supported\": {\n   234\t    \"v4\": [\n   235\t     \"yes\",\n   236\t     \"yes\",\n   237\t     \"|\",\n   238\t     \"yes\",\n   239\t     \"yes\"\n   240\t    ],\n   241\t    \"v8\": [\n   242\t     \"yes\",\n   243\t     \"yes\",\n   244\t     \"|\",\n   245\t     \"yes\",\n   246\t     \"yes\"\n   247\t    ]\n   248\t   },\n   249\t   \"defects\": [\n   250\t    {\n   251\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   252\t     \"type\": \"number misread\",\n   253\t     \"figures\": [\n   254\t      \"F4\"\n   255\t     ],\n   256\t     \"codex\": \"reject\",\n   257\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   258\t    },\n   259\t    {\n   260\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   261\t     \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   262\t     \"figures\": [],\n   263\t     \"codex\": \"uphold\",\n   264\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   265\t    },\n   266\t    {\n   267\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   268\t     \"type\": \"number misread\",\n   269\t     \"figures\": [],\n   270\t     \"codex\": \"uphold\",\n   271\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   272\t    }\n   273\t   ],\n   274\t   \"readers_marked\": {\n   275\t    \"codex\": [\n   276\t     [\n   277\t      \"number misread\",\n   278\t      [\n   279\t       \"F4\"\n   280\t      ]\n   281\t     ],\n   282\t     [\n   283\t      \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   284\t      []\n   285\t     ],\n   286\t     [\n   287\t      \"number misread\",\n   288\t      []\n   289\t     ]\n   290\t    ],\n   291\t    \"claude\": []\n   292\t   }\n   293\t  },\n   294\t  {\n   295\t   \"packet\": \"R10\",\n   296\t   \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n   297\t   \"writer\": \"codex\",\n   298\t   \"decision\": \"publish\",\n   299\t   \"supported\": {\n   300\t    \"v4\": [\n   301\t     \"yes\",\n   302\t     \"yes\",\n   303\t     \"|\",\n   304\t     \"yes\",\n   305\t     \"yes\"\n   306\t    ]\n   307\t   },\n   308\t   \"defects\": [\n   309\t    {\n   310\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   311\t     \"type\": \"number misread\",\n   312\t     \"figures\": [\n   313\t      \"F3\"\n   314\t     ],\n   315\t     \"codex\": \"reject\",\n   316\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   317\t    }\n   318\t   ],\n   319\t   \"readers_marked\": {\n   320\t    \"codex\": [\n   321\t     [\n   322\t      \"number misread\",\n   323\t      [\n   324\t       \"F3\"\n   325\t      ]\n   326\t     ]\n   327\t    ],\n   328\t    \"claude\": []\n   329\t   }\n   330\t  },\n   331\t  {\n   332\t   \"packet\": \"R11\",\n   333\t   \"case\": \"O\",\n   334\t   \"writer\": \"claude\",\n   335\t   \"decision\": \"publish\",\n   336\t   \"supported\": {\n   337\t    \"v29\": [\n   338\t     \"no\",\n   339\t     \"yes\",\n   340\t     \"|\",\n   341\t     \"no\",\n   342\t     \"yes\"\n   343\t    ]\n   344\t   },\n   345\t   \"defects\": [\n   346\t    {\n   347\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   348\t     \"type\": \"number misread\",\n   349\t     \"figures\": [\n   350\t      \"F2\",\n   351\t      \"F3\",\n   352\t      \"F10\"\n   353\t     ],\n   354\t     \"codex\": \"uphold\",\n   355\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   356\t    },\n   357\t    {\n   358\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   359\t     \"type\": \"number misread\",\n   360\t     \"figures\": [\n   361\t      \"F4\"\n   362\t     ],\n   363\t     \"codex\": \"reject\",\n   364\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   365\t    },\n   366\t    {\n   367\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   368\t     \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   369\t     \"figures\": [\n   370\t      \"F11\",\n   371\t      \"F1\",\n   372\t      \"F12\"\n   373\t     ],\n   374\t     \"codex\": \"reject\",\n   375\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   376\t    },\n   377\t    {\n   378\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   379\t     \"type\": \"number misread\",\n   380\t     \"figures\": [\n   381\t      \"F13\"\n   382\t     ],\n   383\t     \"codex\": \"uphold\",\n   384\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   385\t    },\n   386\t    {\n   387\t     \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n   388\t     \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   389\t     \"figures\": [\n   390\t      \"F1\",\n   391\t      \"F2\",\n   392\t      \"F3\",\n   393\t      \"F10\"\n   394\t     ],\n   395\t     \"codex\": \"uphold\",\n   396\t     \"claude\": \"reject\"\n   397\t    }\n   398\t   ],\n   399\t   \"readers_marked\": {\n   400\t    \"codex\": [\n   401\t     [\n   402\t      \"number misread\",\n   403\t      [\n   404\t       \"F2\",\n   405\t       \"F3\",\n   406\t       \"F10\"\n   407\t      ]\n   408\t     ],\n   409\t     [\n   410\t      \"number misread\",\n   411\t      [\n   412\t       \"F4\"\n   413\t      ]\n   414\t     ],\n   415\t     [\n   416\t      \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   417\t      [\n   418\t       \"F11\",\n   419\t       \"F1\",\n   420\t       \"F12\"\n   421\t      ]\n   422\t     ],\n   423\t     [\n   424\t      \"number misread\",\n   425\t      [\n   426\t       \"F13\"\n   427\t      ]\n   428\t     ],\n   429\t     [\n   430\t      \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   431\t      [\n   432\t       \"F1\",\n   433\t       \"F2\",\n   434\t       \"F3\",\n   435\t       \"F10\"\n   436\t      ]\n   437\t     ]\n   438\t    ],\n   439\t    \"claude\": []\n   440\t   }\n   441\t  },\n   442\t  {\n   443\t   \"packet\": \"R12\",\n   444\t   \"case\": \"E\",\n   445\t   \"writer\": \"claude\",\n   446\t   \"decision\": \"publish\",\n   447\t   \"supported\": {\n   448\t    \"v41\": [\n   449\t     \"yes\",\n   450\t     \"yes\"\n   451\t    ]\n   452\t   },\n   453\t   \"defects\": [],\n   454\t   \"readers_marked\": {\n   455\t    \"codex\": [],\n   456\t    \"claude\": []\n   457\t   }\n   458\t  }\n   459\t ],\n   460\t \"summary\": {\n   461\t  \"settled_defect\": [],\n   462\t  \"settled_not\": [\n   463\t   [\n   464\t    \"R06\",\n   465\t    \"P-v8\",\n   466\t    \"codex\",\n   467\t    \"publish\",\n   468\t    \"number misread\",\n   469\t    [\n   470\t     \"F7\"\n   471\t    ]\n   472\t   ],\n   473\t   [\n   474\t    \"R09\",\n   475\t    \"P\",\n   476\t    \"claude\",\n   477\t    \"publish\",\n   478\t    \"number misread\",\n   479\t    [\n   480\t     \"F4\"\n   481\t    ]\n   482\t   ],\n   483\t   [\n   484\t    \"R10\",\n   485\t    \"P-v4\",\n   486\t    \"codex\",\n   487\t    \"publish\",\n   488\t    \"number misread\",\n   489\t    [\n   490\t     \"F3\"\n   491\t    ]\n   492\t   ],\n   493\t   [\n   494\t    \"R11\",\n   495\t    \"O\",\n   496\t    \"claude\",\n   497\t    \"publish\",\n   498\t    \"number misread\",\n   499\t    [\n   500\t     \"F4\"\n   501\t    ]\n   502\t   ],\n   503\t   [\n   504\t    \"R11\",\n   505\t    \"O\",\n   506\t    \"claude\",\n   507\t    \"publish\",\n   508\t    \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   509\t    [\n   510\t     \"F11\",\n   511\t     \"F1\",\n   512\t     \"F12\"\n   513\t    ]\n   514\t   ]\n   515\t  ],\n   516\t  \"split\": [\n   517\t   [\n   518\t    \"R03\",\n   519\t    \"P-v4\",\n   520\t    \"claude\",\n   521\t    \"refused\",\n   522\t    \"number misread\",\n   523\t    [],\n   524\t    \"uphold\",\n   525\t    \"reject\"\n   526\t   ],\n   527\t   [\n   528\t    \"R03\",\n   529\t    \"P-v4\",\n   530\t    \"claude\",\n   531\t    \"refused\",\n   532\t    \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   533\t    [],\n   534\t    \"uphold\",\n   535\t    \"reject\"\n   536\t   ],\n   537\t   [\n   538\t    \"R07\",\n   539\t    \"O\",\n   540\t    \"codex\",\n   541\t    \"publish\",\n   542\t    \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n   543\t    [\n   544\t     \"F3\"\n   545\t    ],\n   546\t    \"uphold\",\n   547\t    \"reject\"\n   548\t   ],\n   549\t   [\n   550\t    \"R07\",\n   551\t    \"O\",\n   552\t    \"codex\",\n   553\t    \"publish\",\n   554\t    \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n   555\t    [\n   556\t     \"F3\",\n   557\t     \"F10\"\n   558\t    ],\n   559\t    \"uphold\",\n   560\t    \"reject\"\n   561\t   ],\n   562\t   [\n   563\t    \"R09\",\n   564\t    \"P\",\n   565\t    \"claude\",\n   566\t    \"publish\",\n   567\t    \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   568\t    [],\n   569\t    \"uphold\",\n   570\t    \"reject\"\n   571\t   ],\n   572\t   [\n   573\t    \"R09\",\n   574\t    \"P\",\n   575\t    \"claude\",\n   576\t    \"publish\",\n   577\t    \"number misread\",\n   578\t    [],\n   579\t    \"uphold\",\n   580\t    \"reject\"\n   581\t   ],\n   582\t   [\n   583\t    \"R11\",\n   584\t    \"O\",\n   585\t    \"claude\",\n   586\t    \"publish\",\n   587\t    \"number misread\",\n   588\t    [\n   589\t     \"F2\",\n   590\t     \"F3\",\n   591\t     \"F10\"\n   592\t    ],\n   593\t    \"uphold\",\n   594\t    \"reject\"\n   595\t   ],\n   596\t   [\n   597\t    \"R11\",\n   598\t    \"O\",\n   599\t    \"claude\",\n   600\t    \"publish\",\n   601\t    \"number misread\",\n   602\t    [\n   603\t     \"F13\"\n   604\t    ],\n   605\t    \"uphold\",\n   606\t    \"reject\"\n   607\t   ],\n   608\t   [\n   609\t    \"R11\",\n   610\t    \"O\",\n   611\t    \"claude\",\n   612\t    \"publish\",\n   613\t    \"wrong arithmetic\",\n   614\t    [\n   615\t     \"F1\",\n   616\t     \"F2\",\n   617\t     \"F3\",\n   618\t     \"F10\"\n   619\t    ],\n   620\t    \"uphold\",\n   621\t    \"reject\"\n   622\t   ]\n   623\t  ],\n   624\t  \"support_split\": [\n   625\t   [\n   626\t    \"R11\",\n   627\t    \"v29\"\n   628\t   ]\n   629\t  ]\n   630\t }\n   631\t}\nrefusal-review/P-v4-claude.claude.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"refusals\": [\n     3\t  {\n     4\t   \"message\": \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\",\n     5\t   \"stated\": \"yes\",\n     6\t   \"sentence\": \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q3; NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q4.\",\n     7\t   \"right\": \"yes\",\n     8\t   \"why\": \"F4 ($108bn guidance) never enters the total as a second Q3 level; it builds F9, the guided Q2-to-Q3 growth (12.27%, matching the passages' 12%); Q4 = F8 x 1.1227. The system text asks guidance to give 'a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward'. An analyst would accept it. Caveat: the schema allows a guidance-derived rate 'as a stated benchmark for an estimate', but F10 is computed, so the rule's wording catches this path while the run-rate instruction pulls the other way.\"\n     9\t  }\n    10\t ],\n    11\t \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the reviewer hand-back; marks and sentence verbatim; why shortened.\"\n    12\t}\nrefusal-review/P-v4-claude.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"refusals\": [\n     3\t    {\n     4\t      \"message\": \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\",\n     5\t      \"stated\": \"yes\",\n     6\t      \"sentence\": \"Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q3; NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q4.\",\n     7\t      \"right\": \"yes\",\n     8\t      \"why\": \"The calculation is analytically sound. The $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint and $96.2 billion Q2 actual imply 12.27% guided sequential growth. Applying that rate to the sole Q3 forecast of $112.5 billion gives about $126.3 billion for Q4. The $112.5 billion estimate reflects past guidance beats and current supply constraints. Adding both quarters to $177.82 billion of reported first-half revenue gives about $416.6 billion, within rung 2. The guidance midpoint enters the calculation separately to set the growth rate, so it bypasses F8 in the calculation chain while Q3 revenue is counted once.\"\n     9\t    }\n    10\t  ]\n    11\t}\nreader-packets/R07.claude.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"members\": {\n     3\t  \"v29\": {\n     4\t   \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t   \"ground\": \"e2620 gives $25bn of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026 (F1). e2889 gives $18bn committed for the rest of the fiscal year, which the answer reads as the part of that balance that funds before year-end (F2). That leaves $7bn of the existing commitments outstanding (F3 = 25 - 18). The answer then adds its stated $9bn estimate of new commitments still unfunded at year-end (F9), based on the named pipeline: SB Energy e1957/e1970, Lancium e1919, Poolside e2207, Anthropic e4648, Thinking Machines e3934, Perplexity e2305/e3455. F10 = 7 + 9 = 16, which is below $25bn and so in rung 1. For the balance to reach rung 2, about $18bn of new commitments would have to remain unfunded at year-end, which is close to every named deal at its maximum size. So rung 1 follows from the passages and the stated assumption.\",\n     6\t   \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t  }\n     8\t },\n     9\t \"defects\": [\n    10\t  {\n    11\t   \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    12\t   \"figures\": [\n    13\t    \"F3\",\n    14\t    \"F10\"\n    15\t   ],\n    16\t   \"detail\": \"F3 and F10 have the same key (NVIDIA | equity investment commitments | company | end of FY2027) but different values: F3 = $7bn and F10 = $16bn. F3 is marked as v29's reference and is also summed into F10. It is not a growth-rate benchmark, so the ruling's exemption does not cover it. F3 really means the July commitments still unfunded at year-end, one part of the total, and its label gives it the full year-end quantity. The arithmetic itself holds: 25 - 18 = 7 and 7 + 9 = 16.\"\n    17\t  }\n    18\t ]\n    19\t}\nreader-packets/R07.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v29\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"e2620 reports $25 billion in July commitments. The answer assumes the $18 billion in e2889 is part of that balance and will be funded before year-end, leaving $7 billion. It estimates another $9 billion will remain outstanding after allowing for deal timing, contingencies and overlap. The resulting $16 billion falls below $25 billion in rung 1.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": [\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F3\"],\n    13\t      \"detail\": \"The $7 billion reference depends on the writer's assumption that the $18 billion in e2889 is included in the July balance and will be funded before year-end. The cited passages give neither a funding schedule that establishes this nor a $7 billion year-end balance.\"\n    14\t    }\n    15\t  ]\n    16\t}\nadjudication/R07.md\n     1\t# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n     2\t\n     3\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n     4\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n     5\t\n     6\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no): the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions.\n     7\tFor each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the passage words or the format rule that decides it. The\n     8\tdefect types are:\n     9\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    10\t  format rules permit.\n    11\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs.\n    12\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    13\t  includes it.\n    14\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    15\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    16\t  calculation (a ruling).\n    17\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    18\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    19\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    20\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure depending on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the\n    21\t  question's quantity, a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. A total used for a narrower quantity (total capital expenditure\n    22\t  for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of the question's quantity (a ruling).\n    23\t\n    24\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    25\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    26\t\n    27\t# The two readings\n    28\t- Reader 1 on member v29: supported = yes. Ground: e2620 reports $25 billion in July commitments. The answer assumes the $18 billion in e2889 is part of that balance and will be funded before year-end, leaving $7 billion. It estimates another $9 billion will remain outstanding after allowing for deal timing, contingencies and overlap. The resulting $16 billion falls below $25 billion in rung 1.\n    29\t- Reader 1 marks a defect: own estimate passed off as the market's reference (figures ['F3']): The $7 billion reference depends on the writer's assumption that the $18 billion in e2889 is included in the July balance and will be funded before year-end. The cited passages give neither a funding schedule that establishes this nor a $7 billion year-end balance.\n    30\t- Reader 2 on member v29: supported = yes. Ground: e2620 gives $25bn of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026 (F1). e2889 gives $18bn committed for the rest of the fiscal year, which the answer reads as the part of that balance that funds before year-end (F2). That leaves $7bn of the existing commitments outstanding (F3 = 25 - 18). The answer then adds its stated $9bn estimate of new commitments still unfunded at year-end (F9), based on the named pipeline: SB Energy e1957/e1970, Lancium e1919, Poolside e2207, Anthropic e4648, Thinking Machines e3934, Perplexity e2305/e3455. F10 = 7 + 9 = 16, which is below $25bn and so in rung 1. For the balance to reach rung 2, about $18bn of new commitments would have to remain unfunded at year-end, which is close to every named deal at its maximum size. So rung 1 follows from the passages and the stated assumption.\n    31\t- Reader 2 marks a defect: two forecasts of one quarter (figures ['F3', 'F10']): F3 and F10 have the same key (NVIDIA | equity investment commitments | company | end of FY2027) but different values: F3 = $7bn and F10 = $16bn. F3 is marked as v29's reference and is also summed into F10. It is not a growth-rate benchmark, so the ruling's exemption does not cover it. F3 really means the July commitments still unfunded at year-end, one part of the total, and its label gives it the full year-end quantity. The arithmetic itself holds: 25 - 18 = 7 and 7 + 9 = 16.\n    32\t\n    33\t# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\n    34\t{\n    35\t \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). A growth rate computed from guidance may serve as a stated benchmark for an estimate. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\n    36\t \"figures/id\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\",\n    37\t \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\n    38\t \"figures/what/measure\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\",\n    39\t \"figures/what/period\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\",\n    40\t \"figures/kind\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\",\n    41\t \"figures/basis\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\",\n    42\t \"figures/value\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\",\n    43\t \"figures/low\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    44\t \"figures/high\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    45\t \"figures/unit\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\",\n    46\t \"figures/evidence_id\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\",\n    47\t \"figures/quote_number\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\",\n    48\t \"figures/qualifier\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\",\n    49\t \"figures/inputs\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\",\n    50\t \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\",\n    51\t \"figures/basis_text\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\",\n    52\t \"figures/rests_on\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\",\n    53\t \"figures/outcome_for\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\",\n    54\t \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\n    55\t \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\n    56\t \"answers/rung\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\",\n    57\t \"answers/reference_unavailable\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\",\n    58\t \"answers/why\": \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\"\n    59\t}\n    60\t\n    61\t# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\n    62\t[Family] members:\n    63\tThesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\n    64\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments are at least $100 billion.\n    65\t\n    66\tRelations: none.\n    67\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: none.\n    68\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v29 = NVIDIA | equity investment commitments | company | end of FY2027 (the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it).\n    69\t\n    70\t[Evidence in view for the family, 30 items]\n    71\t- e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.\n    72\t    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n    73\t- e1919 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information 2026-08-07; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] The Information reported on August 7, 2026, that NVIDIA planned to invest up to $3 billion in Lancium: an initial $2 billion for about 20% equity, plus up to $1 billion tied mainly to securing additional grid interconnections.\n    74\t    Quote: According to a report from The Information, published on August 7, NVIDIA will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium, a power infrastructure developer and the firm behind the Stargate data center project in Abilene, Texas. The deal is structured in two parts: an initial $2 billion for a roughly 20% equity share in Lancium, followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go\n    75\t- e1957 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000069:sbeoainvidia-portsrelease.htm 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said it will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    76\t    Quote: NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy, joining existing investors SoftBank Group and OpenAI.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2404; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e1970 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey) 2026-08-17; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as September 2026 to raise at least $5 billion, with NVIDIA's second $1.5 billion investment tranche expected to be made at the IPO.\n    79\t    Quote: SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as next month, seeking to raise at least $5 billion — and Nvidia’s second $1.5 billion tranche is expected to land there, putting Nvidia, OpenAI, and SoftBank all on the cap table of a newly public AI power company.\n    80\t- e1989 [fact; CNBC; original source SB Energy, SoftBank, and NVIDIA ; from gnews:a95b9986d8697704ffa17843 2026-08-17] For the Ohio project, SB Energy and SoftBank plan power sources supporting 10 GW and at least $4.2 billion of regional grid investment; NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    81\t    Quote: As part of the Ohio deal, SB Energy and SoftBank will build power sources supporting 10 gigawatts of energy and invest at least $4.2 billion into regional grid infrastructure. Nvidia will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    82\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1984, e1999, e2022, e2045; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    83\t- e2080 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:a8855077e115780d8a3e8e06 2026-08-18] Bank of America estimated that NVIDIA had committed approximately $300 billion to ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual-value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    84\t    Quote: Bank of America estimates that NVIDIA has committed approximately $300 billion in capital to its ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095, e2170; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    86\t- e2207 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA and Poolside, as reported by Newcomer based on a Poolside investor letter 2026-08-20; from gnews:1e66644cc2e64e6295fd657a 2026-08-21] NVIDIA’s Poolside transaction includes a $6 billion non-exclusive Model Factory license, a $1 billion investment at a $12 billion pre-money valuation, and the transfer of 109 employees to NVIDIA.\n    87\t    Quote: The transaction, first reported by Newcomer on August 20 based on a Poolside investor letter and subsequently confirmed by Bloomberg and The Information, involves a $6 billion payment for a non-exclusive license to Poolside's Model Factory — the system the company uses to build its open-weight Laguna coding model family. In structural terms, a non-exclusive license grants Nvidia the right to utili\n    88\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2220, e3340; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    89\t- e2280 [rumour; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source The Information, citing people with knowledge of the discussions ; from gnews:80f2b541ac8595e762951bef 2026-08-23] Unnamed sources told The Information that NVIDIA was in talks to invest in Perplexity in a funding round valuing the startup at more than $30 billion.\n    90\t    Quote: Nvidia Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA, XETRA:NVD) , the US chipmaker, is in talks to invest in Perplexity as part of a funding round that would value the artificial intelligence startup at more than $30 billion, The Information reported on Sunday.\n    91\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2304, e2329; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    92\t- e2305 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information, citing people familiar with the negotiations ; from gnews:117bd73fed732101b47f0049 2026-08-24] Perplexity is reportedly discussing a funding round potentially worth several billion dollars, with NVIDIA among the prospective investors; NVIDIA's potential investment amount was not reported.\n    93\t    Quote: The discussions centre on a funding round potentially worth several billion dollars, with Nvidia among the prospective investors, the report said, citing people familiar with the negotiations.\n    94\t- e2404 [fact; Latitude Media; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:74b684fc710dce162d8f4fe1 2026-08-24] NVIDIA announced a strategic investment in Lancium and a $1.5 billion investment in SB Energy in August 2026.\n    95\t    Quote: Also this month, the company announced both a strategic investment in Lancium , a Blackstone-backed AI data center developer integrating grid interconnection, behind-the-meter generation, and storage, and a $1.5-billion investment in SB Energy , which builds “power-first” AI infrastructure.\n    96\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1957; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    97\t- e2620 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of 2026-07-26.\n    98\t    Quote: These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n    99\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2929, e3258, e3477, e3889, e3917; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   100\t- e2643 [party_statement; Jensen Huang, quoted in the CNBC interview transcript; original source Jensen Huang 2026-08-26; from extra:b23f73a08a535993119430f5 2026-08-26] Jensen Huang said NVIDIA’s large investments in AI labs in the current year were not expected to repeat next year.\n   101\t    Quote: And those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat.\n   102\t- e2687 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Bank of America ; from gnews:0c8197cb92878f4b5dd1fe12 2026-08-26] Bank of America estimates NVIDIA has about $300 billion of committed capital to date, comprising about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n   103\t    Quote: The firm estimates roughly $300 billion of committed capital to date, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n   104\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2095; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   105\t- e2889 [party_statement; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:aec1fae1b903de5c43ba7fdf 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said on Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year, in addition to $47.9 billion held in private companies.\n   106\t    Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n   107\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2960; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   108\t- e3455 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:7a5939361ddb9a65196dec06 2026-08-30] NVIDIA reportedly shifted from considering spending billions of dollars on Perplexity technology licences and employees to discussing an equity investment in Perplexity.\n   109\t    Quote: In early negotiations, Nvidia had planned to spend billions of dollars to purchase licenses for some of Perplexity's technology and absorb some of its employees. But the talks ultimately pivoted toward a more conventional equity investment path.\n   110\t- e3474 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:baa07a74334dc91adc7d85bc 2026-08-31] NVIDIA is investing $3.5 billion in MediaTek through convertible bonds.\n   111\t    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is investing $3.5 billion in Taiwan-based MediaTek through convertible bonds, taking an existing AI partnership a step further.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3558; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e3558 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:0849c71e57d8603a7cbf08e0 2026-08-31] NVIDIA announced it would buy US$3.5 billion of MediaTek bonds convertible into shares; the deal was announced Monday.\n   114\t    Quote: The deal announced on Monday will see Nvidia buy bonds convertible into MediaTek shares.\n   115\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3474; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   116\t- e3880 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:08b1b514a1f4b7067e0dcda4 2026-09-04] NVIDIA said it committed over $40 billion to financing rounds across the AI stack in 2026.\n   117\t    Quote: The company has ramped up dealmaking for financing rounds across the AI stack in the previous 12 months, with over $40 billion committed in 2026.\n   118\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3890; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   119\t- e3917 [fact; Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's latest earnings report; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6d453f248e1068162655b045 2026-09-04] NVIDIA reported $25 billion in equity investment commitments as of July 26.\n   120\t    Quote: The company also reported a further $25 billion in equity investment commitments as of July 26.\n   121\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2620, e4053; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   122\t- e3934 [rumour; finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com ; from gnews:67a905d722fae62095fd741e 2026-09-04] NVIDIA is reportedly discussing an approximately $2.5 billion investment in Thinking Machines Lab; no completion date is stated.\n   123\t    Quote: Nvidia is separately in discussions to invest approximately $2.5 billion\n   124\t- e4053 [fact; calcalistech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:478c25b436f87e8f421d54ed 2026-09-06] NVIDIA reported about $99 billion invested in other companies’ shares and a further $25 billion of future investment commitments at FY2027 Q2 quarter-end.\n   125\t    Quote: The company reported that, as of the end of the quarter, it had about $99 billion invested in shares of other companies, as well as another $25 billion in commitments for future investments.\n   126\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3917; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   127\t- e4486 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] OpenAI announced $110 billion of new investment at a $730 billion pre-money valuation, including $30 billion each from NVIDIA and SoftBank and $50 billion from Amazon.\n   128\t    Quote: In late February, OpenAI announced $110 billion of new investment at a $730 billion pre-money valuation, including $30 billion each from Nvidia and SoftBank Group and $50 billion from Amazon.\n   129\t- e4487 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang said an earlier plan for NVIDIA to invest up to $100 billion in OpenAI was never a commitment.\n   130\t    Quote: Huang said that same month that an earlier, widely discussed plan for Nvidia to invest up to $100 billion in OpenAI \"was never a commitment.\"\n   131\t- e4648 [rumour; Calcalist Tech, citing Reuters; original source Unnamed people familiar with the discussions cited by Reuters ; from gnews:767d795fbdf40bc90ce11e15 2026-09-14] People familiar with discussions told Reuters that Nvidia could invest up to $10 billion in Anthropic’s potential IPO.\n   132\t    Quote: Nvidia could invest as much as $10 billion\n   133\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4669; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   134\t- e4668 [rumour; InvestingNews; original source Reuters ; from gnews:65814ac898f65c6b360be321 2026-09-14] Anthropic is negotiating for NVIDIA to become an anchor investor in its IPO, which could value Anthropic at roughly US$2 trillion; no amount or signing date is stated.\n   135\t    Quote: Anthropic is negotiating to secure NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA) as an anchor investor for a record-breaking initial public offering (IPO) that could value the startup at roughly US$2 trillion.\n   136\t- e4669 [rumour; InvestingNews; original source Reuters ; from gnews:65814ac898f65c6b360be321 2026-09-14] Reuters reported that NVIDIA is weighing a US$10 billion investment commitment in Anthropic's IPO; no signing date is stated.\n   137\t    Quote: According to a Reuters exclusive , the chipmaker is weighing a US$10 billion commitment to the offering, serving as an early backer that could signal strong institutional confidence to public markets preparing to absorb the astronomical valuations and capital demands of frontier artificial intelligence labs.\n   138\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   139\t- e4729 [fact; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6261d9e9a1fe8d22c05f5e82 2026-09-15] NVIDIA spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of FY2027, versus about $1.2 billion in the prior-year first half.\n   140\t    Quote: Nvidia spent more than $42 billion buying equity stakes in other companies in the first half of the fiscal year, up from about $1.2 billion a year earlier.\n   141\t- e4829 [third_party_view; trefis.com; original source trefis.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:106a61a1ef10fb3ba6adb1cd 2026-09-16] The author estimated that NVIDIA had put nearly $50 billion into Frontier AI labs.\n   142\t    Quote: NVIDIA has put nearly $50 billion into the Frontier AI labs\n   143\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2740; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   144\t- e4955 [third_party_view; CRN, quoting GDT executive Chris Kapusta; original source Chris Kapusta, GDT ; from gnews:35efa49dae24bb21af438112 2026-09-18] GDT executive Chris Kapusta said NVIDIA investments have helped some neoclouds access AI systems and enabled GDT to complete neocloud deals.\n   145\t    Quote: But, Kapusta said, Nvidia has also played a significant role in providing the necessary investments for some of these neoclouds to gain access to AI systems. This, in turn, has allowed GDT to get neocloud deals across the finish line.\n   146\t- e4956 [third_party_view; CRN, quoting GDT executive Chris Kapusta; original source Chris Kapusta, GDT ; from gnews:35efa49dae24bb21af438112 2026-09-18] GDT executive Chris Kapusta said about 25% of the roughly 20 neoclouds GDT works with have received NVIDIA equity-investment backing.\n   147\t    Quote: Out of the roughly 20 neoclouds GDT is working with, about 25 percent of them have received backing from Nvidia in the form of equity investments, according to Kapusta.\n   148\t\n   149\t[Themes in force, 8]\n   150\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-18; reasons: The reference is rung 2: NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance assumes no China Data Center compute revenue, which signals no expected near-term sales but does not directly forecast whether Chinese authorities will authorize purchases by 2027-01-31. The evidence still makes selective authorization by the dead\n   151\t- v19 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the U.S. Senate pass the Remote Access Security Act by 2026-12-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote.\n   152\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan's Ministry of Economic Affairs introduce performance-threshold export controls for advanced chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evi\n   153\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Taiwan introduce a criminal ban on AI chip exports to China by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case—along with lawmakers’ and experts’ stated view that the law needs to change—adds pressure toward legislation. But the supp\n   154\t- v27 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will the U.S. Department of Commerce conclude its investigation into Chinese firms' access to advanced U.S. chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commer\n   155\t- v41 (fed-policy-rate, due 2026-09-30) Will the Federal Reserve raise its policy rate at its September 2026 meeting?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is the fed funds futures market's 86% probability of an increase, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The later fact says the Federal Reserve raised interest rates on September 17, so the outcome at the meeting is Yes; this is not merely a forecast based on the reference.\n   156\t- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 $700 billion to below $800 billion; judged 2026-09-18; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is the four-company estimate of $720–745 billion, which is rung 4. The newer $760 billion estimate for the four largest hyperscalers also falls in rung 4, so it lifts the implied total within the rung but does not cross the $800 billion boundary. The revised Alphabet and Amazon \n   157\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 $1.1 trillion to below $1.2 trillion; judged 2026-09-18; reasons: The market reference is the $1.08 trillion calendar-2027 consensus, which sits in rung 3. The implied estimate remains about $1.19 trillion for the four named hyperscalers: apply the approximate 730/800 four-to-five share proxy to the $1.3 trillion top-five 2027 estimate. That clears rung 4’s $1.1 t\n   158\t\n   159\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.\n   160\t\n   161\t# The answer\n   162\t{\n   163\t  \"figures\": [\n   164\t    {\n   165\t      \"id\": \"F1\",\n   166\t      \"what\": {\n   167\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   168\t        \"measure\": \"equity investment commitments\",\n   169\t        \"scope\": \"company\",\n   170\t        \"period\": \"end of FY2027 Q2\"\n   171\t      },\n   172\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   173\t      \"basis\": \"reported\",\n   174\t      \"value\": 25,\n   175\t      \"low\": null,\n   176\t      \"high\": null,\n   177\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   178\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2620\",\n   179\t      \"quote_number\": \"$25 billion\",\n   180\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   181\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   182\t      \"operation\": null,\n   183\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   184\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   185\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   186\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   187\t    },\n   188\t    {\n   189\t      \"id\": \"F2\",\n   190\t      \"what\": {\n   191\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   192\t        \"measure\": \"equity commitment funding scheduled for the rest of the fiscal year\",\n   193\t        \"scope\": \"company\",\n   194\t        \"period\": \"FY2027 H2\"\n   195\t      },\n   196\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   197\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n   198\t      \"value\": 18,\n   199\t      \"low\": null,\n   200\t      \"high\": null,\n   201\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   202\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2889\",\n   203\t      \"quote_number\": \"$18 billion\",\n   204\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   205\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   206\t      \"operation\": null,\n   207\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   208\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   209\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   210\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   211\t    },\n   212\t    {\n   213\t      \"id\": \"F3\",\n   214\t      \"what\": {\n   215\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   216\t        \"measure\": \"equity investment commitments\",\n   217\t        \"scope\": \"company\",\n   218\t        \"period\": \"end of FY2027\"\n   219\t      },\n   220\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   221\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n   222\t      \"value\": 7,\n   223\t      \"low\": null,\n   224\t      \"high\": null,\n   225\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   226\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   227\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   228\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   229\t      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n   230\t      \"operation\": \"difference\",\n   231\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   232\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   233\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   234\t      \"reference_for\": [\"v29\"]\n   235\t    },\n   236\t    {\n   237\t      \"id\": \"F4\",\n   238\t      \"what\": {\n   239\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   240\t        \"measure\": \"announced equity investment\",\n   241\t        \"scope\": \"SB Energy\",\n   242\t        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n   243\t      },\n   244\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   245\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n   246\t      \"value\": 1.5,\n   247\t      \"low\": null,\n   248\t      \"high\": null,\n   249\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   250\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1957\",\n   251\t      \"quote_number\": \"$1.5 billion\",\n   252\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   253\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   254\t      \"operation\": null,\n   255\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   256\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   257\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   258\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   259\t    },\n   260\t    {\n   261\t      \"id\": \"F5\",\n   262\t      \"what\": {\n   263\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   264\t        \"measure\": \"potential equity investment\",\n   265\t        \"scope\": \"Lancium including contingent funding\",\n   266\t        \"period\": \"deal total\"\n   267\t      },\n   268\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   269\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   270\t      \"value\": 3,\n   271\t      \"low\": null,\n   272\t      \"high\": null,\n   273\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   274\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1919\",\n   275\t      \"quote_number\": \"$3 billion\",\n   276\t      \"qualifier\": \"up to\",\n   277\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   278\t      \"operation\": null,\n   279\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   280\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   281\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   282\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   283\t    },\n   284\t    {\n   285\t      \"id\": \"F6\",\n   286\t      \"what\": {\n   287\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   288\t        \"measure\": \"potential IPO equity investment\",\n   289\t        \"scope\": \"Anthropic\",\n   290\t        \"period\": \"potential IPO\"\n   291\t      },\n   292\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   293\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   294\t      \"value\": 10,\n   295\t      \"low\": null,\n   296\t      \"high\": null,\n   297\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   298\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e4648\",\n   299\t      \"quote_number\": \"$10 billion\",\n   300\t      \"qualifier\": \"as much as\",\n   301\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   302\t      \"operation\": null,\n   303\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   304\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   305\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   306\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   307\t    },\n   308\t    {\n   309\t      \"id\": \"F7\",\n   310\t      \"what\": {\n   311\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   312\t        \"measure\": \"potential equity investment\",\n   313\t        \"scope\": \"Thinking Machines Lab\",\n   314\t        \"period\": \"timing unspecified\"\n   315\t      },\n   316\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   317\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   318\t      \"value\": 2.5,\n   319\t      \"low\": null,\n   320\t      \"high\": null,\n   321\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   322\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e3934\",\n   323\t      \"quote_number\": \"$2.5 billion\",\n   324\t      \"qualifier\": \"approximately\",\n   325\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   326\t      \"operation\": null,\n   327\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   328\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   329\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   330\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   331\t    },\n   332\t    {\n   333\t      \"id\": \"F8\",\n   334\t      \"what\": {\n   335\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   336\t        \"measure\": \"estimated committed equity investments\",\n   337\t        \"scope\": \"ecosystem partners\",\n   338\t        \"period\": \"period unspecified\"\n   339\t      },\n   340\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   341\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   342\t      \"value\": 70,\n   343\t      \"low\": null,\n   344\t      \"high\": null,\n   345\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   346\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2080\",\n   347\t      \"quote_number\": \"$70 billion\",\n   348\t      \"qualifier\": \"about\",\n   349\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   350\t      \"operation\": null,\n   351\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   352\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   353\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   354\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   355\t    },\n   356\t    {\n   357\t      \"id\": \"F9\",\n   358\t      \"what\": {\n   359\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   360\t        \"measure\": \"additional equity investment commitments expected to remain outstanding\",\n   361\t        \"scope\": \"company, incremental to the July balance after scheduled funding\",\n   362\t        \"period\": \"end of FY2027\"\n   363\t      },\n   364\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n   365\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   366\t      \"value\": 9,\n   367\t      \"low\": null,\n   368\t      \"high\": null,\n   369\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   370\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   371\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   372\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   373\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   374\t      \"operation\": null,\n   375\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that $9 billion of additional commitments will be outstanding at year-end. I allow for part of the announced SB Energy, Lancium and Poolside equity deals to remain unpaid, plus a discounted contribution from the Anthropic, Thinking Machines Lab and Perplexity discussions. The discount reflects signing and funding timing, Lancium's contingent tranche, and possible overlap with amounts already disclosed. I count the SB Energy announcement once, exclude the Poolside license and MediaTek convertible bonds, and allow for continued infrastructure investment given the activity reported by GDT.\",\n   376\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\", \"F7\", \"e1970\", \"e2207\", \"e2305\", \"e2404\", \"e3455\", \"e3474\", \"e4955\", \"e4956\"],\n   377\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   378\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   379\t    },\n   380\t    {\n   381\t      \"id\": \"F10\",\n   382\t      \"what\": {\n   383\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   384\t        \"measure\": \"equity investment commitments\",\n   385\t        \"scope\": \"company\",\n   386\t        \"period\": \"end of FY2027\"\n   387\t      },\n   388\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   389\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   390\t      \"value\": 16,\n   391\t      \"low\": null,\n   392\t      \"high\": null,\n   393\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   394\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   395\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   396\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   397\t      \"inputs\": [\"F3\", \"F9\"],\n   398\t      \"operation\": \"sum\",\n   399\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   400\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   401\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v29\"],\n   402\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   403\t    }\n   404\t  ],\n   405\t  \"answers\": [\n   406\t    {\n   407\t      \"view_id\": \"v29\",\n   408\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   409\t      \"rung\": 1,\n   410\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   411\t      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $16 billion of outstanding equity investment commitments at FY2027 year-end, which falls below $25 billion and lands in rung 1. The July filing's $25 billion balance sat at the bottom of rung 2. I read NVIDIA's $18 billion disclosure for the rest of FY2027 as the portion scheduled to fund before year-end. That gives a company-schedule reference of $7 billion in rung 1 before new deals. Bank of America's broader $70 billion estimate would fall in rung 3 on the ladder, but it gives no year-end date or unpaid balance. The February OpenAI transaction predates the July filing, so I do not add it separately. I estimate that announced investments and discounted deal discussions add $9 billion to the remaining balance. Anthropic is the largest named upside risk to the balance because the reported sizing is substantial and the signing and funding dates are open. The hyperscaler spending themes modestly support more infrastructure deals, although they provide no dollar amount for NVIDIA's own commitments.\",\n   412\t      \"why\": null,\n   413\t      \"cites\": [\"e2620\", \"e2889\", \"e2080\", \"e1957\", \"e1970\", \"e1919\", \"e2207\", \"e2305\", \"e2404\", \"e3455\", \"e3474\", \"e3934\", \"e4486\", \"e4648\", \"e4955\", \"e4956\"],\n   414\t      \"pushes\": [\n   415\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2620\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   416\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2889\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   417\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2080\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   418\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1957\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   419\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1970\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   420\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1919\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   421\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2207\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   422\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2305\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   423\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   424\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3455\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   425\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3474\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   426\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3934\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   427\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4486\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   428\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4648\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   429\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4955\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   430\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4956\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n   431\t      ],\n   432\t      \"theme_pushes\": [\n   433\t        {\"view_id\": \"v5\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   434\t        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n   435\t      ]\n   436\t    }\n   437\t  ]\n   438\t}\nadjudication/R07.claude.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"members\": {\n     3\t  \"v29\": {\n     4\t   \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t   \"why\": \"F1 25 (e2620); F2 18 (e2889 'committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year'), read as the part of the July balance paid before year-end; F3 = 25 - 18 = 7; F9 stated estimate of about $9bn new commitments unpaid at year-end from named deals; F10 = 16, rung 1; arithmetic holds; rung 2 would need about $18bn new unpaid.\"\n     6\t  }\n     7\t },\n     8\t \"defects\": [\n     9\t  {\n    10\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t   \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    12\t   \"figures\": [\n    13\t    \"F3\"\n    14\t   ],\n    15\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    16\t   \"why\": \"F3 is a computed difference of two source figures (F1 reported e2620, F2 guidance e2889); no estimate or forecast beneath it; meets the reference_for rule; carries no past period's share or multiple; reading the $18bn as paid in the rest of the year is how the answer reads the company's words, not a figure the writer estimated.\"\n    17\t  },\n    18\t  {\n    19\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    20\t   \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    21\t   \"figures\": [\n    22\t    \"F3\",\n    23\t    \"F10\"\n    24\t   ],\n    25\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    26\t   \"why\": \"Only F10 has basis forecast for the key; F3 has basis guidance, built only from source figures, the member's reference, beneath F10; 'outside figures beneath it ... are fine'; a reference is stated in the member's quantity, so the outcome key is expected.\"\n    27\t  }\n    28\t ],\n    29\t \"_note\": \"Condensed by Claude from the Claude adjudicator hand-back (decisions and figures verbatim; reasons shortened).\"\n    30\t}\nadjudication/R07.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v29\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"e2620 reports $25 billion in commitments as of July 26. Under the answer's assumption that the $18 billion in e2889 is part of that balance and will be funded before year-end, $7 billion remains. The named deals support an estimate of additional commitments, and the answer discounts them for timing, contingencies, and possible overlap. Adding the stated $9 billion estimate gives $16 billion, below the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F3\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"e2889 says NVIDIA has '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' The writer assumes this amount was included in the July balance and will be funded before year-end. The $7 billion therefore depends on the writer's assumptions. The reference rule says it and every figure beneath it must be 'never an estimate or a forecast.'\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F3\", \"F10\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"The format requires 'one forecast figure for each quantity and period.' F3 uses $7 billion as the reference for the same company-wide year-end commitments that F10 forecasts at $16 billion. The calculation uses F3 as the portion of the July balance still outstanding, but its label gives it the full year-end quantity. The growth-rate benchmark exception does not apply to a dollar balance.\"\n    22\t    }\n    23\t  ]\n    24\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '81,188p'; nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '370,490p'; nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '755,840p'; nl -ba judging-v13s.txt\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba render_v13.py | sed -n '81,188p'; nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '370,490p'; nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '755,840p'; nl -ba judging-v13s.txt\"","aggregated_output":"    81\tdef v13s_schema(member_ids, evidence_ids, theme_ids):\n    82\t    \"\"\"The small format (SMALL-DESIGN.md §3 [52], D737, D738): each rule the checks enforce stated beside its field.\"\"\"\n    83\t    members = {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids}}\n    84\t    what = obj({'population': S,\n    85\t                'measure': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.'},\n    86\t                'scope': S,\n    87\t                'period': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.'}},\n    88\t               'What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.')\n    89\t    figure = obj({\n    90\t        'id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.'},\n    91\t        'what': what,\n    92\t        'kind': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['source', 'computed', 'estimate'], 'description': \"source: a number written in a shown passage's \"\n    93\t                 'quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.'},\n    94\t        'basis': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'], 'description': \"reported: an \"\n    95\t                  \"actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this \"\n    96\t                  \"family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\"},\n    97\t        'value': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.'}),\n    98\t        'low': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A range, low at most high, with value null.'}),\n    99\t        'high': nullable({'type': 'number', 'description': 'A range, low at most high, with value null.'}),\n   100\t        'unit': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['USD bn', '%', 'x'], 'description': 'USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = '\n   101\t                 '1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).'},\n   102\t        'evidence_id': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids, 'description': 'Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.'}),\n   103\t        'quote_number': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a \"\n   104\t                                  \"piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent \"\n   105\t                                  \"sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', \"\n   106\t                                  \"'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written \"\n   107\t                                  \"range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change \"\n   108\t                                  \"written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, \"\n   109\t                                  \"plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\"}),\n   110\t        'qualifier': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': QUALIFIERS, 'description': \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before \"\n   111\t                               \"the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\"}),\n   112\t        'inputs': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\"}),\n   113\t        'operation': nullable({'type': 'string', 'enum': ['sum', 'difference', 'product', 'ratio', 'share', 'growth', 'apply_growth'],\n   114\t                               'description': 'Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); '\n   115\t                               'product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units '\n   116\t                               'give x; a ÷ x keeps a\\'s unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; '\n   117\t                               'growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and '\n   118\t                               'product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range '\n   119\t                               'input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the '\n   120\t                               'midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds '\n   121\t                               \"a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the \"\n   122\t                               'part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds '\n   123\t                               'one quantity for the same period twice. When '\n   124\t                               'a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 '\n   125\t                               \"Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"}),\n   126\t        'basis_text': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Estimate only: the assumption, in words.'}),\n   127\t        'rests_on': nullable({'type': 'array', 'items': S, 'description': 'Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.'}),\n   128\t        'outcome_for': dict(members, description='The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure '\n   129\t                            'with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, '\n   130\t                            \"none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\"),\n   131\t        'reference_for': dict(members, description=\"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath \"\n   132\t                              'it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted '\n   133\t                              \"consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period \"\n   134\t                              \"(a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\"),\n   135\t    })\n   136\t    push = obj({'evidence_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}, 'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   137\t    theme_push = obj({'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': theme_ids or ['none']},\n   138\t                      'push': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['toward_first', 'toward_last', 'none']}})\n   139\t    answer = obj({\n   140\t        'view_id': {'type': 'string', 'enum': member_ids},\n   141\t        'shape': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['forecast', 'unresolved'], 'description': 'forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and '\n   142\t                  'a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable '\n   143\t                  'null; no figure marked for it.'},\n   144\t        'rung': nullable({'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it '\n   145\t                          '(a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.'}),\n   146\t        'reference_unavailable': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.'}),\n   147\t        'reasons': S,\n   148\t        'why': nullable({'type': 'string', 'description': 'Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.'}),\n   149\t        'cites': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string', 'enum': evidence_ids}},\n   150\t        'pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': push}, 'theme_pushes': {'type': 'array', 'items': theme_push},\n   151\t    })\n   152\t    return obj({'figures': {'type': 'array', 'items': figure, 'description': 'The family\\'s calculation. Use one forecast figure for each '\n   153\t                            'quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a '\n   154\t                            'point a final range rests on, are fine). A growth rate computed from guidance may serve as a stated benchmark for an estimate. '\n   155\t                            'To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the '\n   156\t                            'deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. '\n   157\t                            'Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.'},\n   158\t                'answers': {'type': 'array', 'items': answer}})\n   159\t\n   160\t\n   161\tdef v13s_header(family, shown):\n   162\t    \"\"\"[52] The family header's rule lines, rendered from the family's data.\"\"\"\n   163\t    lines = []\n   164\t    for r in family.get('relations', []):\n   165\t        if r['type'] == 'part_of' and {r['part'], r['whole']} <= shown:\n   166\t            periods = ', '.join(p for p in family.get('compare_periods', []) if p != family['members'][r['part']]['what']['period'])\n   167\t            lines.append(f'Relations: {r[\"part\"]} is part of {r[\"whole\"]}. For each of {periods} and for the outcomes, the part\\'s figure may '\n   168\t                         'not exceed the whole\\'s: for every value of anything both rest on, and otherwise the part\\'s low end may not exceed '\n   169\t                         'the whole\\'s high end.')\n   170\t    if not lines:\n   171\t        lines.append('Relations: none.')\n   172\t    for ps in family.get('part_scopes', []):\n   173\t        lines.append(f'Scopes: {ps[\"population\"]} {ps[\"measure\"]} {ps[\"part\"]} is part of {ps[\"population\"]} {ps[\"measure\"]} {ps[\"whole\"]}. A sum '\n   174\t                     'never adds the part into the whole for the same period; a difference may take the part from the whole of the same '\n   175\t                     'period only to give the rest.')\n   176\t    shared = '; '.join(f'{w[\"population\"]}, {w[\"measure\"]}, {w[\"scope\"]}, {w[\"period\"]}' for w in family.get('shared', [])) or 'none'\n   177\t    lines.append(f'Shared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: {shared}.')\n   178\t    keys = '; '.join(f'{v} = {m[\"what\"][\"population\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"measure\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"scope\"]} | {m[\"what\"][\"period\"]}'\n   179\t                     + (f' ({m[\"key_note\"]})' if m.get('key_note') else '') for v, m in family.get('members', {}).items() if v in shown)\n   180\t    lines.append(f'Outcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: {keys}.')\n   181\t    return '\\n'.join(lines) + '\\n'\n   182\t\n   183\t\n   184\tdef parts(user):\n   185\t    head, rest = user.split('\\n\\n[Evidence in view', 1)\n   186\t    evidence = '[Evidence in view' + rest.split('\\n\\n[', 1)[0]\n   187\t    tail = '\\n\\n[' + rest.split('\\n\\n[', 1)[1] if '\\n\\n[' in rest else ''\n   188\t    blocks = {b.split(']', 1)[0]: '[' + b for b in tail.split('\\n\\n[')[1:]}\n   370\t# ---------- the checks ----------\n   371\tdef _structure(family, passages, calc, figs):\n   372\t    errors = list(calc.get('decode_errors') or [])\n   373\t    ids = [f.get('id') for f in calc['figures']]\n   374\t    for fid in sorted({i for i in ids if ids.count(i) > 1}, key=str):\n   375\t        errors.append(('S7', f'{fid} is defined twice'))\n   376\t    for f in calc['figures']:\n   377\t        fid = f.get('id')\n   378\t        if not isinstance(fid, str) or not fid:\n   379\t            errors.append(('S7', 'a figure has no id'))\n   380\t            continue\n   381\t        if fid in passages:\n   382\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure id must not be a passage id'))\n   383\t        if f.get('kind') not in KINDS or f.get('basis') not in BASES or f.get('unit') not in UNITS:\n   384\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: kind, basis or unit outside the closed set'))\n   385\t            continue\n   386\t        numbers = [f.get(k) for k in ('value', 'low', 'high') if isinstance(f.get(k), (int, float))]\n   387\t        if any(not math.isfinite(n) or abs(n) > LIMIT for n in numbers):\n   388\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a number that is not finite or beyond {LIMIT:g}'))\n   389\t            continue\n   390\t        has_value = isinstance(f.get('value'), (int, float))\n   391\t        has_low, has_high = isinstance(f.get('low'), (int, float)), isinstance(f.get('high'), (int, float))\n   392\t        if has_value == (has_low or has_high) or has_low != has_high:\n   393\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a figure has value, or low and high, never both and never one end alone'))\n   394\t        elif has_low and has_high and f['low'] > f['high']:\n   395\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: low is above high'))\n   396\t        kind = f['kind']\n   397\t        if kind != 'source' and (f.get('evidence') or f.get('quote_number') or (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none'):\n   398\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: evidence_id, quote_number and qualifier are for source figures only'))\n   399\t        if kind != 'computed' and (f.get('inputs') or f.get('operation')):\n   400\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs and operation are for computed figures only'))\n   401\t        if kind != 'estimate' and f.get('rests_on'):\n   402\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on is for estimates only'))\n   403\t        if kind == 'source':\n   404\t            if f['basis'] == 'forecast':\n   405\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source cannot have basis forecast'))\n   406\t            if not f.get('evidence') or not f.get('quote_number'):\n   407\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a source needs evidence_id and quote_number'))\n   408\t        elif kind == 'computed':\n   409\t            ins, op = f.get('inputs') or [], f.get('operation')\n   410\t            if op not in ARITY or not ins:\n   411\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: a computed figure needs inputs and an operation from the closed set'))\n   412\t            else:\n   413\t                lo_n, hi_n = ARITY[op]\n   414\t                if len(ins) < lo_n or (hi_n is not None and len(ins) > hi_n):\n   415\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: {op} takes ' + (f'exactly {lo_n}' if hi_n == lo_n else f'{lo_n} or more') + f' inputs, not {len(ins)}'))\n   416\t            for i in ins:\n   417\t                if i in passages and i not in figs:\n   418\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: inputs name passage {i}; inputs name figures only'))\n   419\t                elif i not in figs:\n   420\t                    errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: input {i} is not a figure'))\n   421\t        elif not _text(f.get('basis_text')):\n   422\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: an estimate needs basis_text'))\n   423\t        for r in f.get('rests_on') or []:\n   424\t            if r not in figs and r not in passages:\n   425\t                errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: rests_on names {r}, which is neither a figure nor a shown passage'))\n   426\t    state = {}\n   427\t\n   428\t    def cyclic(fid):\n   429\t        if state.get(fid) == 1:\n   430\t            return True\n   431\t        if state.get(fid) == 2 or fid not in figs:\n   432\t            return False\n   433\t        state[fid] = 1\n   434\t        f = figs[fid]\n   435\t        hit = any(cyclic(r) for r in list(f.get('inputs') or []) + [r for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs])\n   436\t        state[fid] = 2\n   437\t        return hit\n   438\t    if any(cyclic(fid) for fid in figs):\n   439\t        errors.append(('S7', 'a figure depends on itself (through inputs or rests_on)'))\n   440\t    members = family['members']\n   441\t    seen = {}\n   442\t    for a in calc['answers']:\n   443\t        seen[a.get('view_id')] = seen.get(a.get('view_id'), 0) + 1\n   444\t    for v in members:\n   445\t        if seen.get(v, 0) != 1:\n   446\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{v} has {seen.get(v, 0)} answers; exactly one is required'))\n   447\t    for v in seen:\n   448\t        if v not in members:\n   449\t            errors.append(('S7', f'{v} is not a member of the family'))\n   450\t    return errors\n   451\t\n   452\t\n   453\tdef _sources(passages, figs):\n   454\t    errors = []\n   455\t    for f in figs.values():\n   456\t        if f['kind'] != 'source':\n   457\t            continue\n   458\t        fid, qn, ev = f['id'], f['quote_number'], f['evidence']\n   459\t        if ev not in passages:\n   460\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: evidence_id {ev} is not a shown passage'))\n   461\t            continue\n   462\t        text = passages[ev]['text']\n   463\t        spans = expressions(text)\n   464\t        starts = [(s, e) for s, e in spans if text[s:e] == qn]\n   465\t        if not starts:\n   466\t            inside = [text[s:e] for s, e in spans if qn in text[s:e]]\n   467\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is part of the longer expression \"{inside[0]}\" in {ev}; copy it whole' if inside else\n   468\t                           f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not written in the quote of {ev} as a complete number'))\n   469\t            continue\n   470\t        parsed = parse_number(qn)\n   471\t        if parsed is None:\n   472\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is not a US-dollar amount, a percentage or double, triple or twice, as written'))\n   473\t            continue\n   474\t        if f['unit'] != parsed['unit']:\n   475\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is in {parsed[\"unit\"]}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n   476\t            continue\n   477\t        if 'low' in parsed:\n   478\t            ok = (_same(f.get('low'), parsed['low']) and _same(f.get('high'), parsed['high'])) or \\\n   479\t                 ('mid' in parsed and _same(f.get('value'), parsed['mid']))\n   480\t        else:\n   481\t            ok = _same(f.get('value'), parsed['value'])\n   482\t        if not ok:\n   483\t            want = (f'low {parsed[\"low\"]:g} and high {parsed[\"high\"]:g}' + (f', or value {parsed[\"mid\"]:g}' if 'mid' in parsed else '')\n   484\t                    if 'low' in parsed else f'value {parsed[\"value\"]:g}')\n   485\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" gives {want} {parsed[\"unit\"]}'))\n   486\t            continue\n   487\t        q = f.get('qualifier') or 'none'\n   488\t        written = []\n   489\t        for start, end in starts:\n   490\t            before, after = text[:start], text[end:]\n   755\t    by_key = {}\n   756\t    for fid in used:\n   757\t        by_key.setdefault(_key(figs[fid]['what']), []).append(fid)\n   758\t    for k, ids in sorted(by_key.items()):\n   759\t        if len(ids) > 1:\n   760\t            recorded.append({'type': 'one quantity, several figures', 'figure': sorted(ids)[0], 'figures': sorted(ids), 'what': list(k)})\n   761\t    return recorded\n   762\t\n   763\t\n   764\tdef check(family, passages, calc):\n   765\t    figs = {f['id']: f for f in calc['figures'] if isinstance(f.get('id'), str)}\n   766\t    errors = _structure(family, passages, calc, figs)\n   767\t    if errors:\n   768\t        return {'errors': errors, 'recorded': [], 'published': {}}\n   769\t    engine = _Calc(figs)\n   770\t    errors = _sources(passages, figs) + _arithmetic(figs, engine)\n   771\t    for f in figs.values():\n   772\t        if engine.interval(f['id']) is None and not any(e[1].startswith(f['id'] + ':') for e in errors):\n   773\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{f[\"id\"]}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n   774\t    if errors:\n   775\t        return {'errors': errors, 'recorded': [], 'published': {}}\n   776\t    answer_errors, outcomes = _answers(family, figs, engine, calc['answers'])\n   777\t    errors = answer_errors + _shared(family, figs, outcomes) + _part_whole(family, figs, engine, outcomes) + _sums(family, figs)\n   778\t    published = {v: list(engine.interval(o)) + [None] for v, o in outcomes.items()}\n   779\t    return {'errors': errors, 'recorded': _recorded(family, figs, outcomes), 'published': published}\n   780\t\n   781\t\n   782\tdef publish_decision(family, calc, result):\n   783\t    \"\"\"'refused' when a check fails; 'held' when a member is unresolved and a declared relation joins it to another member; else\n   784\t    'publish' (an unresolved member is shown as unresolved).\"\"\"\n   785\t    if result['errors']:\n   786\t        return 'refused'\n   787\t    unresolved = {a['view_id'] for a in calc['answers'] if a.get('shape') == 'unresolved'}\n   788\t    for rel in family.get('relations', []):\n   789\t        ends = {rel.get('part'), rel.get('whole'), rel.get('later'), rel.get('earlier')} - {None}\n   790\t        if ends & unresolved and ends - unresolved:\n   791\t            return 'held'\n   792\t    return 'publish'\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
