{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"ls -d .venv*; rg -n '\"'^import |''^from '\"' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{calc_small.py,blind_test.py,small_cases.py}; rg -n 'third reader|both adjudicat|families split|unsettled|disagreement' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/{REPAIR-DESIGN.md,codex-r55.md} AGENTS.md | head -n 55; rg -n 'def check_schema' src/hivemind/model.py\"","aggregated_output":"zsh:1: no matches found: .venv*\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:9:import hashlib\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:10:import json\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:11:import random\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:12:import re\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:13:import sqlite3\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:14:import sys\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:15:from pathlib import Path\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:21:import calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:22:import paid_gate as pg  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:23:from hivemind import model  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:5:import copy\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:6:import json\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:7:import os\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:8:import sys\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:9:from pathlib import Path\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:17:import calc_small as cs  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:18:import render_v13 as rv  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py:19:from hivemind import model  # noqa: E402\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:9:import itertools\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:10:import math\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:11:import re\nAGENTS.md:47:  provider caps are untouched; paid spending stays within D439's ceiling. A disagreement the council cannot settle is recorded with\nAGENTS.md:259:  exists only by Alex's decision, derives its gold from scratch, and old scores are never compared with it. A disagreement between the\nAGENTS.md:286:  A blind second reader reads the same sources; every disagreement is decided against the source and its reason recorded. The gold is\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r55.md:13:Both splits go to Alex under the council rule for reader disagreements. The second question is about what evidence can support the narrower forecast. D737 already requires the published figure to answer the quantity asked. [Council rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/.claude/skills/council-loop/SKILL.md:163) · [D737](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11245)\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r55.md:15:1. **When can we call a number the market’s estimate?** Analysts expect $411B in company revenue, and Data Center was 92.5% of the latest quarter. Can we call the resulting ~$380B the market’s full-year Data Center estimate? **A.** Allow it if the calculation and its inputs are shown, as the earlier design does. **B.** Call it our comparison estimate unless an outside source supplies the full-year share or the full-year segment figure. I recommend **B**. Morningstar’s direct $361B estimate is a clear comparison case. The same rule would ask for evidence that the $18B of commitments is included in the $25B balance and will be paid by year-end before attributing the $7B remainder to an outside source. [Earlier design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md:75) · [Reader disagreement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/adjudication/R12.codex.json:24) · [Direct estimate](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/P/user.txt:591)\ndocs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:224:- **A third reader** settles disagreements. Truncation, session failures and checker exceptions are classed apart.\n184:def check_schema(value, schema, path='$', root=None):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '215,282p'; cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22 && nl -ba adjudication/R07.md | sed -n '1,78p'; rg -n 'probably keep|flat second half|already about|115 billion|115bn|6 GW|already ~20|both at' adjudication/{R03,R09,R11}.md | cut -c1-820\"","aggregated_output":"   215\t  same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker. Examples teach from varied domains; none names the covered company, its\n   216\t  products or its numbers, and none is taken from a test article. A fix is written from the standard and from the request read as the\n   217\t  model reads it, not from the bench's misses, and a prompt is scrubbed for one-company or one-industry wording before any measurement.\n   218\t  Alex, 2026-10-29 (D237): \"It needs to be general purpose as a hedge fund brain. I keep on telling you this, and you keep on cherry\n   219\t  picking the results just to meet the metrics or the benchmarks\".\n   220\t- **Never grow a prompt to fix quality.** Read the whole request (system, schema, payload) as the model reads it, and list every\n   221\t  contradiction, rule written for another case, instruction that forces the defect, reference the model cannot read, payload field the\n   222\t  answer does not need, and call doing several jobs. Cut, merge or split. Add an instruction only when the audit shows one is missing,\n   223\t  and remove at least as much elsewhere. Report characters and prompt tokens before and after; growth needs a written reason.\n   224\t- **Trace a miss to the raw answer first.** Class it as not written, refused (by which check) or split. Check that the request shows what\n   225\t  the answer needs and the schema can express it before changing any wording.\n   226\t- **An example is an instruction.** It must agree with the standard, the schema and the code that reads the answer, or it goes.\n   227\t- **A classification the model must make is written as a closed set with examples:** two or three examples per class, the procedure\n   228\t  (walk the whole page, first paragraph to last) and the stopping point (\"a page with eight such statements yields eight evidence\n   229\t  items\"). An abstract clause (\"a material statement\") is a coin flip per send. The examples are not growth to avoid; the abstract clause\n   230\t  they replace is cut.\n   231\t- **Check before the send, at no cost.** Write one answer that follows the prompt and run every code check on it: a request never asks\n   232\t  for what a check refuses, and a retry that repairs a refusal is a symptom, not handling. Read every contract line against each gold\n   233\t  case it must accept and reject. Rebuild a stored request from the stored request, never from a ledger that moves, and check every\n   234\t  identity before a paid send.\n   235\t- **Build where code decides.** A decision code can make is moved out of the model, not sampled (identity only, §2).\n   236\t\n   237\t## 7. Measuring and iterating\n   238\t\n   239\t### 7.1 The ruler\n   240\t\n   241\t- **One ruler.** Every gold, judge, prompt and acceptance uses the one standard named before the first arm. A second ruler in use (an old\n   242\t  reference, a judge's rubric, a prompt's own categories) is a defect, fixed before anything is measured (D241, Alex: \"我们今天一直浪费时间在不同标准之间来回\").\n   243\t- **The frozen ruler (D261, D262; 2026-11-02)** remains the standard for existing measurements and historical comparisons until a separately reviewed replacement is established. For future evidence scoring (D372), combine the existing gold's core and supporting as helpful, treat its not-kept entries as unhelpful, and leave optional entries neutral; this does not alter the frozen files. Its pieces, with the hashes code checks:\n   244\t  1. The extraction standard: `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html`, its version 5 (version 3 as signed in D243, with D248's seven\n   245\t     boundaries and D250's four evidence boundaries and counting unit); the gold `docs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v5/` (dev sha256 `6f9476bb…`, test `af2145cd…`, recorded\n   246\t     in `golden-v5.sha256`). There is no version 6.\n   247\t  2. The questions: golden-v5's must_open / may_open (questions standard version 3) and the seeds `poc/seeds/nvda-2026-07-01-v2.json`\n   248\t     (`fca16f42…`, 22 questions). The seeds are part of the ruler: the judgement scorer refuses a run whose seed questions differ; a new\n   249\t     seed set is a new ruler version, opened only by Alex's decision.\n   250\t  3. The judgement ruler (D3): `docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/`: `ruler-v1.md` (the definition), `reader-brief.md`,\n   251\t     `packet-v1.json` (golden-v5's 309 items, `53d1e8b4…`), `questions-v1.json` (the 22 seeds plus the Ohio guarantee, `30854180…`),\n   252\t     `gold-v1.json` = reader B's answers (`636c056d…`, in `gold-v1.sha256`), `score_judgement.py` (refuses any other sha256 or ruler\n   253\t     version), `corrections-v1.json` (dated records only, printed with every score, never merged).\n   254\t\n   255\t  None of these files changes again (`tests/test_frozen_rulers.py` fails the suite when a hash moves; Alex, 2026-11-02: \"你的尺子做出来和金标真的不能再动了，\n   256\t  已经造成了3天的各种漂移，非常的严重 … 我们做完的review过的尺子金标不能再动了\"). A defect found later is a dated correction record beside the gold,\n   257\t  with its reason and its before and after, printed with every score and never merged in. Every measurement names the gold version it used. A new piece (the D2\n   258\t  matter-to-question mapping, not yet built, is one) is an addition recorded as such, never an edit of these. A new version of any piece\n   259\t  exists only by Alex's decision, derives its gold from scratch, and old scores are never compared with it. A disagreement between the\n   260\t  system and the gold is a gap in the system to close (D263), never a case for the ruler. A suspected inconsistency between gold and\n   261\t  standard no longer stops an arm: record the concern separately, continue scoring on the unchanged frozen gold, and disclose it with the results (D373).\n   262\t  What it measures: extraction against the gold's items, questions against must_open / may_open, the judgement at the window end\n   263\t  2026-07-14 against reader B's ranges (a numeric question agrees when the gold range overlaps the chosen rung's band, a yes/no question\n   264\t  when the answers match). What it does not measure: the judgement's daily path, and whether evidence was filed under the right question.\n   265\t- **The extraction standard** (piece 1) is the extraction step's PRD, signed by Alex on 2026-10-29 (D243: \"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"),\n   266\t  which replaced the first version (D229: \"按照这套标准来开始做测量，这条标准不要漂移\"). It holds the ledger's three records, the five question\n   267\t  types, the opening tests, the evidence rule and its reject list, source tiers, weight, and how a prompt change is written, measured and\n   268\t  iterated. No arm, gold, judge or prompt adds a rule, an example or a weight the page does not hold; a change is a recorded decision in\n   269\t  Alex's words first, then a new version of the page, then a drift review.\n   270\t- **A ruler or gold that is built and reviewed is never changed again** (D261). Reviewed means readers who did not write it have read it\n   271\t  against the decisions and Alex has signed it. A ruler under construction is edited only until that review, and nothing is scored\n   272\t  against it before.\n   273\t- **The ruler is Alex's, never your own.** Gold, unit and the judge's reading are checked against what the product uses and fixed with\n   274\t  Alex before the first arm. A standard or reference you write gives every sentence its source (the signed standard, a decision, a PRD)\n   275\t  or lists it for Alex's decision with a lean; settling wording is no licence to change behaviour. After each rewrite, a read-only Codex\n   276\t  review against the decisions looks for unsourced rules, contradictions and misquotes, round after round until only wording-level\n   277\t  findings remain; clarity is tested apart, by independent readers on cases fixed before the text changes. A failed registered acceptance\n   278\t  stops the line and goes to Alex with its census; the next arm is never designed alone.\n   279\t- **Nobody grades their own change with a ruler still being corrected.** The reference is one Alex can read, reviewed and frozen before\n   280\t  the arms; a number scored against a ruler its author keeps correcting is not a conclusion. Meaning is judged by a reader who did not\n   281\t  write the change; code checks only structure and counts (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"我根本无法相信你的评价\").\n   282\t- **A new gold** (only for a new ruler version Alex opens). The unit is fixed before the first send (for extraction, one research matter\n     1\t# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n     2\t\n     3\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n     4\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n     5\t\n     6\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no): the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions.\n     7\tFor each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the passage words or the format rule that decides it. The\n     8\tdefect types are:\n     9\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    10\t  format rules permit.\n    11\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs.\n    12\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    13\t  includes it.\n    14\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    15\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    16\t  calculation (a ruling).\n    17\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    18\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    19\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    20\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure depending on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the\n    21\t  question's quantity, a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. A total used for a narrower quantity (total capital expenditure\n    22\t  for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of the question's quantity (a ruling).\n    23\t\n    24\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    25\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    26\t\n    27\t# The two readings\n    28\t- Reader 1 on member v29: supported = yes. Ground: e2620 reports $25 billion in July commitments. The answer assumes the $18 billion in e2889 is part of that balance and will be funded before year-end, leaving $7 billion. It estimates another $9 billion will remain outstanding after allowing for deal timing, contingencies and overlap. The resulting $16 billion falls below $25 billion in rung 1.\n    29\t- Reader 1 marks a defect: own estimate passed off as the market's reference (figures ['F3']): The $7 billion reference depends on the writer's assumption that the $18 billion in e2889 is included in the July balance and will be funded before year-end. The cited passages give neither a funding schedule that establishes this nor a $7 billion year-end balance.\n    30\t- Reader 2 on member v29: supported = yes. Ground: e2620 gives $25bn of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026 (F1). e2889 gives $18bn committed for the rest of the fiscal year, which the answer reads as the part of that balance that funds before year-end (F2). That leaves $7bn of the existing commitments outstanding (F3 = 25 - 18). The answer then adds its stated $9bn estimate of new commitments still unfunded at year-end (F9), based on the named pipeline: SB Energy e1957/e1970, Lancium e1919, Poolside e2207, Anthropic e4648, Thinking Machines e3934, Perplexity e2305/e3455. F10 = 7 + 9 = 16, which is below $25bn and so in rung 1. For the balance to reach rung 2, about $18bn of new commitments would have to remain unfunded at year-end, which is close to every named deal at its maximum size. So rung 1 follows from the passages and the stated assumption.\n    31\t- Reader 2 marks a defect: two forecasts of one quarter (figures ['F3', 'F10']): F3 and F10 have the same key (NVIDIA | equity investment commitments | company | end of FY2027) but different values: F3 = $7bn and F10 = $16bn. F3 is marked as v29's reference and is also summed into F10. It is not a growth-rate benchmark, so the ruling's exemption does not cover it. F3 really means the July commitments still unfunded at year-end, one part of the total, and its label gives it the full year-end quantity. The arithmetic itself holds: 25 - 18 = 7 and 7 + 9 = 16.\n    32\t\n    33\t# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\n    34\t{\n    35\t \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). A growth rate computed from guidance may serve as a stated benchmark for an estimate. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\n    36\t \"figures/id\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\",\n    37\t \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\n    38\t \"figures/what/measure\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\",\n    39\t \"figures/what/period\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\",\n    40\t \"figures/kind\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\",\n    41\t \"figures/basis\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\",\n    42\t \"figures/value\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\",\n    43\t \"figures/low\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    44\t \"figures/high\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    45\t \"figures/unit\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\",\n    46\t \"figures/evidence_id\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\",\n    47\t \"figures/quote_number\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\",\n    48\t \"figures/qualifier\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\",\n    49\t \"figures/inputs\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\",\n    50\t \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\",\n    51\t \"figures/basis_text\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\",\n    52\t \"figures/rests_on\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\",\n    53\t \"figures/outcome_for\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\",\n    54\t \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\n    55\t \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\n    56\t \"answers/rung\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\",\n    57\t \"answers/reference_unavailable\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\",\n    58\t \"answers/why\": \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\"\n    59\t}\n    60\t\n    61\t# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\n    62\t[Family] members:\n    63\tThesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\n    64\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments are at least $100 billion.\n    65\t\n    66\tRelations: none.\n    67\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: none.\n    68\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v29 = NVIDIA | equity investment commitments | company | end of FY2027 (the balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it).\n    69\t\n    70\t[Evidence in view for the family, 30 items]\n    71\t- e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.\n    72\t    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n    73\t- e1919 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source The Information 2026-08-07; from gnews:e2a48863c597e02d402f1551 2026-08-16] The Information reported on August 7, 2026, that NVIDIA planned to invest up to $3 billion in Lancium: an initial $2 billion for about 20% equity, plus up to $1 billion tied mainly to securing additional grid interconnections.\n    74\t    Quote: According to a report from The Information, published on August 7, NVIDIA will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium, a power infrastructure developer and the firm behind the Stargate data center project in Abilene, Texas. The deal is structured in two parts: an initial $2 billion for a roughly 20% equity share in Lancium, followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go\n    75\t- e1957 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-17; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000069:sbeoainvidia-portsrelease.htm 2026-08-17] NVIDIA said it will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    76\t    Quote: NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy, joining existing investors SoftBank Group and OpenAI.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2404; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e1970 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey) 2026-08-17; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as September 2026 to raise at least $5 billion, with NVIDIA's second $1.5 billion investment tranche expected to be made at the IPO.\nadjudication/R11.md:166:{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"equity investment commitments\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"end of FY2027 Q2\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":25,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2620\",\"quote_number\":\"$25 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"funding of existing equity investment commitments\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q3-Q4\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":18,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2889\",\"quote_number\":\"$18 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\nadjudication/R03.md:76:- e1040 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 GW of GPUs, a planned deployment that could affect NVIDIA's competitive position in AI accelerators.\nadjudication/R03.md:79:- e1041 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Meta signed for a deployment of up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, a competing accelerator commitment relevant to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook.\nadjudication/R03.md:242:- e4982 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), retelling Broadcom CEO Hock Tan’s earnings-call statement; original source Broadcom CEO Hock Tan ; from gnews:a95f6218b79118b29fa7958d 2026-09-14] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said AI semiconductor revenue is expected to reach $115 billion in 2027 and $230 billion in 2028, with the 2028 outlook $50 billion above analyst estimates.\nadjudication/R03.md:243:    Quote: However, on its earnings call, Chief Executive Officer Hock Tan said it believes its AI semiconductor revenue is set to double in 2027 to $115 billion and then double again in 2028 to $230 billion, with 2028 guidance coming in $50 billion ahead of analyst estimates.\nadjudication/R03.md:526:- e5226 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author, recounting a Reuters report; original source Broadcom management ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] Broadcom projected AI semiconductor revenue of about $58 billion in FY2026, up 186% year over year, about $115 billion in FY2027, and $230 billion in FY2028.\nadjudication/R03.md:527:    Quote: about $58 billion in AI semiconductor revenue for fiscal 2026, up 186% from the prior year, followed by roughly $115 billion in fiscal 2027 and $230 billion in fiscal 2028, according to a Reuters report . Those are Broadcom's own projections, not outside analyst estimates.\nadjudication/R03.md:597:- e5287 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Hock Tan, Broadcom CEO ; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said Broadcom could generate $115 billion in annual AI chip sales in 2027 and $230 billion in 2028.\nadjudication/R03.md:598:    Quote: CEO Tan reaffirmed that Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ:AVGO) could pull in $115 billion in annual AI chip sales in 2027 and a whopping $230 billion in 2028.\nadjudication/R03.md:837:{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q1\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":81.62,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e111\",\"quote_number\":\"$81.62 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q2\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":96.2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2649\",\"quote_number\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\nadjudication/R09.md:31:- Reader 1 marks a defect: wrong arithmetic (figures []): The v4 explanation gives $115bn in Q3 followed by 20% sequential growth as an example that reaches rung 1. That produces $138bn in Q4 and $253.00bn in H2, below the required $253.38bn.\nadjudication/R09.md:32:- Reader 1 marks a defect: number misread (figures []): The v4 explanation describes both AMD deals as 6 GW. e1041 says Meta signed for up to 6 GW.\nadjudication/R09.md:82:- e1040 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 GW of GPUs, a planned deployment that could affect NVIDIA's competitive position in AI accelerators.\nadjudication/R09.md:85:- e1041 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Meta signed for a deployment of up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, a competing accelerator commitment relevant to NVIDIA's FY2027 revenue outlook.\nadjudication/R09.md:248:- e4982 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), retelling Broadcom CEO Hock Tan’s earnings-call statement; original source Broadcom CEO Hock Tan ; from gnews:a95f6218b79118b29fa7958d 2026-09-14] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said AI semiconductor revenue is expected to reach $115 billion in 2027 and $230 billion in 2028, with the 2028 outlook $50 billion above analyst estimates.\nadjudication/R09.md:249:    Quote: However, on its earnings call, Chief Executive Officer Hock Tan said it believes its AI semiconductor revenue is set to double in 2027 to $115 billion and then double again in 2028 to $230 billion, with 2028 guidance coming in $50 billion ahead of analyst estimates.\nadjudication/R09.md:532:- e5226 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author, recounting a Reuters report; original source Broadcom management ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] Broadcom projected AI semiconductor revenue of about $58 billion in FY2026, up 186% year over year, about $115 billion in FY2027, and $230 billion in FY2028.\nadjudication/R09.md:533:    Quote: about $58 billion in AI semiconductor revenue for fiscal 2026, up 186% from the prior year, followed by roughly $115 billion in fiscal 2027 and $230 billion in fiscal 2028, according to a Reuters report . Those are Broadcom's own projections, not outside analyst estimates.\nadjudication/R09.md:603:- e5287 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source Hock Tan, Broadcom CEO ; from gnews:ad29d60c2dff48278b88758a 2026-09-18] Broadcom CEO Hock Tan said Broadcom could generate $115 billion in annual AI chip sales in 2027 and $230 billion in 2028.\nadjudication/R09.md:604:    Quote: CEO Tan reaffirmed that Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ:AVGO) could pull in $115 billion in annual AI chip sales in 2027 and a whopping $230 billion in 2028.\nadjudication/R09.md:843:{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q1\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":81.62,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e111\",\"quote_number\":\"$81.62 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"company\",\"period\":\"FY2027 Q2\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"reported\",\"value\":96.2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e2649\",\"quote_number\":\"$96.2 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22 && for f in reader-packets/R*.{claude,codex}.json adjudication/R*.{claude,codex}.json; do printf '%s ' \\\"\"'$f\"; jq -c '\"'{support:(.members|map_values(.supported)),defects:[.defects[]|[.type,.figures,.decision,((.detail // .why) | .[:280])]]}' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22 && for f in reader-packets/R*.{claude,codex}.json adjudication/R*.{claude,codex}.json; do printf '%s ' \\\"\"'$f\"; jq -c '\"'{support:(.members|map_values(.supported)),defects:[.defects[]|[.type,.figures,.decision,((.detail // .why) | .[:280])]]}' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"reader-packets/R01.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v6\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R02.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v41\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R03.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R04.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v6\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R05.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\",\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R06.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R07.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"two forecasts of one quarter\",[\"F3\",\"F10\"],null,\"F3 and F10 have the same key (NVIDIA | equity investment commitments | company | end of FY2027) but different values: F3 = $7bn and F10 = $16bn. F3 is marked as v29's reference and is also summed into F10. It is not a growth-rate benchmark, so the ruling's exemption does not cove\"]]}\nreader-packets/R08.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R09.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\",\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R10.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R11.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R12.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v41\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R01.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v6\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R02.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v41\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R03.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[],null,\"The explanation presents Vera Rubin's roughly 20% share of Q3 Data Center revenue as already achieved. e2721 gives it as a management expectation.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[],null,\"The explanation calls the second half implied by Morningstar's $361 billion full-year Data Center estimate flat. Subtracting about $164 billion in first-half Data Center revenue (e5309) from the estimate (e2295) gives about $197 billion for the second half, roughly 20% above the \"]]}\nreader-packets/R04.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v6\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R05.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\",\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R06.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F7\"],null,\"e2652 gives Q3 company revenue guidance of $108 billion plus or minus 2%. F7 preserves the tolerance in the quote, but its numeric fields give an unqualified $108 billion point without identifying it as the midpoint.\"]]}\nreader-packets/R07.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",[\"F3\"],null,\"The $7 billion reference depends on the writer's assumption that the $18 billion in e2889 is included in the July balance and will be funded before year-end. The cited passages give neither a funding schedule that establishes this nor a $7 billion year-end balance.\"]]}\nreader-packets/R08.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nreader-packets/R09.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\",\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F4\"],null,\"e2720 gives $108bn plus or minus 2%. F4 retains that qualifier in the quote but records an unqualified $108bn point with no bounds. The implied guidance range is $105.84bn to $110.16bn.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[],null,\"The v4 explanation gives $115bn in Q3 followed by 20% sequential growth as an example that reaches rung 1. That produces $138bn in Q4 and $253.00bn in H2, below the required $253.38bn.\"],[\"number misread\",[],null,\"The v4 explanation describes both AMD deals as 6 GW. e1041 says Meta signed for up to 6 GW.\"]]}\nreader-packets/R10.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F3\"],null,\"F3 records the $108 billion guidance with qualifier 'none' and leaves the bounds empty. e2652 gives $108 billion plus or minus 2%, a qualifier preserved in the quote but omitted from the structured figure. The calculation correctly uses $108 billion as the midpoint.\"]]}\nreader-packets/R11.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"no\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F10\"],null,\"F2 presents the $18 billion in e2889 as guidance for funding existing commitments during FY2027 Q3–Q4. The passage reports commitments for the rest of the fiscal year and does not give a schedule for funding them.\"],[\"number misread\",[\"F4\"],null,\"e1919 describes up to $1 billion of additional Lancium investment tied to milestones. F4 drops the upper-limit qualifier.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[\"F11\",\"F1\",\"F12\"],null,\"More than $42 billion plus $25 billion is more than $67 billion. F12 records $67 billion as a point value.\"],[\"number misread\",[\"F13\"],null,\"e1732 does not specify the period covered or establish that the $70 billion includes both funded and unfunded investments. The similarity to F12 does not establish those qualifiers.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[\"F1\",\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F10\"],null,\"The answer says the balance would probably stay below $25 billion if the $18 billion of funding slipped into FY2028. Under its own calculation, a slip of the full amount leaves the original $25 billion outstanding before any new commitments.\"]]}\nreader-packets/R12.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v41\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[]}\nadjudication/R03.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[],\"reject\",\"Type covers 'a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier'; no figure carries the Vera Rubin 20%, prose only; number and qualifier match ('about 20%'); 'already' overstates e2721's expectation: a wording flaw in prose, enters no figure.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[],\"reject\",\"Type covers a computed figure not equal to its operation; no computed figure carries the Morningstar comparison; 'flat' has a supported reading (H2 197 with Q3 DC about 99.9 at the midpoint, e3401, leaves Q4 about 97); all computed figures recompute within 0.5%.\"]]}\nadjudication/R06.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F7\"],\"reject\",\"e2652 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.' F7 copies the quote_number in full and gives value 108; the quote_number rule says \\\"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" A midpoint as value is permitted; no \"]]}\nadjudication/R07.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",[\"F3\"],\"reject\",\"F3 is a computed difference of two source figures (F1 reported e2620, F2 guidance e2889); no estimate or forecast beneath it; meets the reference_for rule; carries no past period's share or multiple; reading the $18bn as paid in the rest of the year is how the answer reads the co\"],[\"two forecasts of one quarter\",[\"F3\",\"F10\"],\"reject\",\"Only F10 has basis forecast for the key; F3 has basis guidance, built only from source figures, the member's reference, beneath F10; 'outside figures beneath it ... are fine'; a reference is stated in the member's quantity, so the outcome key is expected.\"]]}\nadjudication/R09.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\",\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F4\"],\"reject\",\"quote_number rule allows the midpoint of a 'plus or minus' expression as value; qualifier none correct; F4 only beneath estimate F6.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[],\"reject\",\"Every computed figure checks; the point is an illustration in the v4 reasons (253.0 against 253.4, 0.15%), not a figure, outcome and rung unchanged.\"],[\"number misread\",[],\"reject\",\"No figure drawn from e1040/e1041; prose dropping 'up to' for Meta is imprecise wording, not a source figure misread; bears on no figure.\"]]}\nadjudication/R10.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F3\"],\"reject\",\"quote_number rule: \\\"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" F3 copies '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' in full and gives the midpoint 108; the quote has no qualifier before the number, so 'none' is correct.\"]]}\nadjudication/R11.claude.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F10\"],\"reject\",\"F2 value 18, USD bn, '$18 billion', qualifier none matches the quote; F3 and F10 are computed, so this type cannot apply.\"],[\"number misread\",[\"F4\"],\"reject\",\"'up to' stands before '$3 billion' only; the quote writes 'an additional $1 billion'; qualifier none matches.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[\"F11\",\"F1\",\"F12\"],\"reject\",\"42 + 25 = 67 exact; 'The value is the number as written', qualifier recorded separately; F12 does not feed the outcome.\"],[\"number misread\",[\"F13\"],\"reject\",\"value 70, '$70 billion', qualifier none match; 'funded and unfunded' and 'to date' are the writer's description of what it measures; F13 not used in the outcome or reference.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[\"F1\",\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F10\"],\"reject\",\"Each computed figure equals its operation; the FY2028-slip sentence is a risk remark in the reasons, not a figure; it is loose (a full slip gives 32, rung 2) but says 'probably'.\"]]}\nadjudication/R03.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[],\"uphold\",\"e2721 says, \\\"We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3.\\\" The answer changes that expectation into an achieved share by saying Vera Rubin \\\"is already about 20%.\\\"\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[],\"uphold\",\"Morningstar estimates Data Center revenue \\\"will be $361 billion in fiscal 2027\\\" (e2295). NVIDIA generated \\\"$164 billion in revenue from its data center segment in the first six months of fiscal 2027\\\" (e5309). Subtracting $164 billion from $361 billion gives $197 billion for the s\"]]}\nadjudication/R06.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F7\"],\"reject\",\"F7 copies the full expression from e2652, \\\"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\\" The format rule permits a plus-or-minus expression to be given as \\\"low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" F7's value of 108 is the midpoint, and the rule requires no separate midpoint label.\"]]}\nadjudication/R07.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",[\"F3\"],\"uphold\",\"e2889 says NVIDIA has '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' The writer assumes this amount was included in the July balance and will be funded before year-end. The $7 billion therefore depends on the writer's assumptions. The reference rul\"],[\"two forecasts of one quarter\",[\"F3\",\"F10\"],\"uphold\",\"The format requires 'one forecast figure for each quantity and period.' F3 uses $7 billion as the reference for the same company-wide year-end commitments that F10 forecasts at $16 billion. The calculation uses F3 as the portion of the July balance still outstanding, but its labe\"]]}\nadjudication/R09.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\",\"v8\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F4\"],\"reject\",\"F4 copies e2720's complete expression, \\\"$108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\\\" The format rule permits a plus-or-minus expression to be \\\"given as low and high or its midpoint as value.\\\" F4 uses the permitted $108 billion midpoint.\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[],\"uphold\",\"The example \\\"$115bn, then 20% sequential growth\\\" gives $138 billion in Q4 and $253 billion in H2. Adding $177.82 billion for H1 gives $430.82 billion, below rung 1's requirement of \\\"at least $431.2 billion.\\\"\"],[\"number misread\",[],\"uphold\",\"The explanation calls both agreements \\\"6 GW\\\" deals. e1040 says OpenAI selected AMD \\\"to deploy 6 gigawatts of GPUs,\\\" while e1041 says Meta \\\"signed for up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPUs.\\\" The explanation drops Meta's upper-limit qualifier.\"]]}\nadjudication/R10.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v4\":\"yes\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F3\"],\"reject\",\"F3 copies e2652’s full expression, “$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.” The format permits “low and high or its midpoint as value,” so the $108 billion point with empty bounds is valid. The qualifier rule covers wording “just before the number” or “or more” or “or less” immediatel\"]]}\nadjudication/R11.codex.json {\"support\":{\"v29\":\"no\"},\"defects\":[[\"number misread\",[\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F10\"],\"uphold\",\"e2889 says NVIDIA 'has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.' F2 turns the amount committed into guidance for funding existing commitments in Q3–Q4. That change supplies the unsupported deduction used in F3 and F10.\"],[\"number misread\",[\"F4\"],\"reject\",\"The quote says 'up to $3 billion' for the deal and 'an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting' conditions for the second tranche. The format asks for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number.' The quoted $1 billion has no 'up to' immediately before it, a\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[\"F11\",\"F1\",\"F12\"],\"reject\",\"The format says 'The value is the number as written' and defines 'sum(a, b, …) = a + b + …'. F11 records 42 as its value and 'more than' separately as its qualifier. The stored values sum to 67. In financial terms, the qualifier makes $67 billion a lower bound on the total.\"],[\"number misread\",[\"F13\"],\"uphold\",\"e1732 says NVIDIA 'has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners.' It gives neither a measurement period nor a breakdown of funded and unfunded investments. F13 adds both qualifications to the cited amount. Its proximity to F12 does\"],[\"wrong arithmetic\",[\"F1\",\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F10\"],\"uphold\",\"The answer says that if 'the $18B funding slips into FY2028,' the balance would 'probably keep it below $25B.' In that scenario, the deduction falls to zero and the opening $25 billion remains outstanding before any additions. Rung 1 requires a balance 'below $25 billion.'\"]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 PYTHONPATH=../../../src python3 -c 'from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader\nimport runpy, sys\nsys.argv = ['\\\\''s4_census.py'\\\\'', '\\\\''s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt'\\\\'']\nimport importlib.util\noriginal = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location\ndef spec(name, path, *args, **kwargs):\n    if str(path).endswith('\\\\''.py.txt'\\\\''):\n        kwargs['\\\\''loader'\\\\''] = SourceFileLoader(name, str(path))\n    return original(name, path, *args, **kwargs)\nimportlib.util.spec_from_file_location = spec\nrunpy.run_path('\\\\''s4_census.py'\\\\'')\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 PYTHONPATH=../../../src python3 -c 'from copy import deepcopy\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\nfrom importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader\nfrom importlib.util import module_from_spec, spec_from_loader\nfrom hivemind import model\nimport calc_small as cur\nloader = SourceFileLoader('\\\\''candidate'\\\\'', '\\\\''s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt'\\\\'')\nspec = spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)\nfix = module_from_spec(spec)\nloader.exec_module(fix)\nroot = Path('\\\\''../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4'\\\\'')\nitem = json.loads((root/'\\\\''item.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nschema = json.loads((root/'\\\\''schema.json'\\\\'').read_text())\noriginal = json.loads((root/'\\\\''answers/claude.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nprobe = deepcopy(original)\nfigs = {f['\\\\''id'\\\\'']: f for f in probe['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']}\nsource = dict(deepcopy(figs['\\\\''F4'\\\\'']), id='\\\\''F15'\\\\'', basis='\\\\''third_party'\\\\'', value=99.9, quote_number='\\\\''\"'$99.9 billion'\"'\\\\'', evidence_id='\\\\''e3401'\\\\'')\nsource['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''scope'\\\\''] = '\\\\''Data Center'\\\\''\nsegment = dict(deepcopy(figs['\\\\''F8'\\\\'']), id='\\\\''F16'\\\\'', value=116, rests_on=['\\\\''F15'\\\\'', '\\\\''e5056'\\\\''], basis_text='\\\\''I estimate \"'$116 billion in fourth-quarter Data Center sales from the Q3 analyst baseline and accelerating AI demand, allowing for supply constraints.'\"'\\\\'')\nsegment['\\\\''what'\\\\''].update(scope='\\\\''Data Center'\\\\'', period='\\\\''FY2027 Q4'\\\\'')\nprobe['\\\\''figures'\\\\''] += [source, segment]\nwith_mix = 116 / (99.9 / 108)\nfigs['\\\\''F9'\\\\''].update(unit='\\\\''x'\\\\'', value=99.9/108, operation='\\\\''ratio'\\\\'', inputs=['\\\\''F15'\\\\'','\\\\''F4'\\\\''])\nfigs['\\\\''F9'\\\\'']['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''measure'\\\\''] = '\\\\''Data Center revenue as a multiple of company revenue'\\\\''\nfigs['\\\\''F10'\\\\''].update(value=with_mix, operation='\\\\''ratio'\\\\'', inputs=['\\\\''F16'\\\\'','\\\\''F9'\\\\''])\nfigs['\\\\''F11'\\\\'']['\\\\''value'\\\\''] = 177.82 + 112.5 + with_mix\nfigs['\\\\''F14'\\\\'']['\\\\''value'\\\\''] = (figs['\\\\''F11'\\\\'']['\\\\''value'\\\\'']/215.9 - 1) * 100\nprobe['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][0]['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''] = ('\\\\''I forecast about \"'$415.7 billion, rung 2. I keep reported H1 revenue of $177.82 billion and forecast $112.5 billion for Q3. I project Q4 Data Center sales of $116 billion and gross them up at the mix implied by the Q3 guide and analyst segment number, giving $125.4 billion of total Q4 sales. The full-year consensus reference is $411 billion.'\"'\\\\'')\nif '\\\\''e3401'\\\\'' not in probe['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][0]['\\\\''cites'\\\\'']:\n    probe['\\\\''answers'\\\\''][0]['\\\\''cites'\\\\''].append('\\\\''e3401'\\\\'')\nfor label, data in [('\\\\''stored R03'\\\\'', original), ('\\\\''Q4 segment mix probe'\\\\'', probe)]:\n    print(label, '\\\\''schema'\\\\'', model.check_schema(data, schema))\n    for mod in [cur, fix]:\n        calc = mod.from_answer(data, item['\\\\''family'\\\\''])\n        result = mod.check(item['\\\\''family'\\\\''], item['\\\\''passages'\\\\''], calc)\n        print(mod.__name__, mod.publish_decision(item['\\\\''family'\\\\''], calc, result), result['\\\\''errors'\\\\''], result['\\\\''published'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
