{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'failed registered|the next step|D439|D509' AGENTS.md | tail -n 13; nl -ba .claude/skills/council-loop/SKILL.md | sed -n '140,185p'; ls -la docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calibration-D741\"","aggregated_output":"41:- **While Alex is not active, the council decides (D439).** A choice or a next step that would go to Alex, including opening a new\n44:  they reach a consensus; the consensus is recorded as a decision citing D439, then executed and measured, every trace read by Claude\n45:  itself, every round logged with what it taught (Alex, 2026-11-13: \"把这个goal 改成更多的要求council in the loop 来自行的根据数据和目标做决策。我接下来不会active,需要你有问题的时候就fire council 然后和claude 掰扯直到你们能达到共识为止再下去执行，并仔细的测量，自己看trace 然后把每一轮log 做学习总结，不要饭一样的错，词学迭代完成我们的PRD生产目标\"; 2026-11-15, going inactive again (D509): \"我要睡觉了，下来你自行按照我的准则操作，需要深度的审视每一轮run 用council\").\n47:  provider caps are untouched; paid spending stays within D439's ceiling. A disagreement the council cannot settle is recorded with\n277:  findings remain; clarity is tested apart, by independent readers on cases fixed before the text changes. A failed registered acceptance\n300:  fields the product stores (original day, original speaker, tier, the summary's hedges) and what the next step does with the record\n310:- **Measure end to end before a step's arm, and read the parked designs first.** A step's noise that the next step removes is not the\n311:  defect, and a step's recall that the next step throws away is. Before any arm on one step: the production replay through the whole path\n344:  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n363:- **One recommendation, one test.** What reaches Alex as the next step of work is one recommendation and one test of it, reached after\n   140\t## Traps already paid for\n   141\t\n   142\t- **Relaxing a signed rule on reader judgment.** Readers called an existing 6.3-billion-dollar arrangement \"core\"; the frozen gold marks it not\n   143\t  kept (D250 boundary 1). The relaxed rule let 10 noise items in. Check the gold before relaxing.\n   144\t- **Tuning on probe differences inside sampling noise** (K3 against K4). Stop and run the whole bench instead.\n   145\t- **One draw read as stable.** Two draws of the same configuration shared 188 of 214 helpful hits.\n   146\t- **Library judged without article text.** Always attach the source for verdicts that decide the pass line.\n   147\t- **A fixed-state probe cannot show cascade effects;** only a forward run shows how one article's choices shape the next.\n   148\t- **A budget limit copied from a smaller run stops a big run halfway.** Scale `max_usd`, `max_seconds` and `max_calls`.\n   149\t- **zsh does not split an unquoted variable into arguments.** Write the arguments out.\n   150\t- **A stale bytecode cache makes a mutant pass** when the mutated line has the same length and the same mtime second.\n   151\t- **Low reasoning effort mis-applies judgment rules** (it over-suppressed big matters). Test an effort arm before blaming a rule.\n   152\t- **Pair-level accuracy is the wrong unit when records bundle facts.** Step 2's pair agreement was 82%, yet per incoming record, on\n   153\t  a forward simulation, detection was close. Score the decision the product takes per record on the library it would really see; a\n   154\t  fixed-history replay assumes every earlier merge was right.\n   155\t- **Model readers over-flag materiality.** Two readers flagged 66 of 102 merges as losing a material fact, but a hand-read of a random\n   156\t  12 found only 3 clearly material. A materiality gate shown to the model (Jev's stricter repeat) behaves the same way. Hand-read a\n   157\t  random sample before quoting the readers' count.\n   158\t- **Cuts made to keep a prompt length-neutral are levers too.** In the unfiled round, u1 cut two sentences beside its one change; the\n   159\t  cuts, not the change, moved filing and noise. Make each cut its own arm, or let the prompt grow with a written reason.\n   160\t- **Calibrate the readers before trusting a per-item count.** Two reader families judged the same 48 control answers as 15 against 6\n   161\t  should-be-filed items. Run the source-backed adjudication of disagreements (step 6) before reading arm differences smaller than the\n   162\t  readers' own gap.\n   163\t- **Model readers carry their family's threshold.** Two adjudicators given the same rubric sided with their own families (17 of 52\n   164\t  agreed). When the families split on \"how big is big\", take a balanced set of boundary cases to Alex; his rulings are the ruler.\n   165\t- **Signed text copied piecemeal into another contract can contradict it.** Audit the whole request, closing check included, before\n   166\t  proposing a sourced restoration.\n   167\t- **Keep reviewing a design with a fresh reviewer each round until one finds nothing serious.** The conditions design took eight\n   168\t  rounds, and round 7 found a latent defect in existing code: Step 2 cut passages at 600 characters for 16% of items. \"Ready\" means the\n   169\t  design rounds are clean; the prerequisites (tests, reports, an independently reviewed reference) are build work done before the send.\n   170\t- **A truncated printout miscounts anchors.** A printer that cut summaries at 120 characters hid a margin figure inside combined\n   171\t  items; the control was called 0/6 when it was 5/6. Count an anchor by searching passages and summaries for the figure.\n   172\t- **A design gate is not a prompt audit.** Eight design rounds passed, and the arm's text still kept wider than Alex's ruling, linked\n   173\t  narrower than his ruling and widened \"big matter\". Walk each of Alex's rulings through the changed request at no cost before any send.\n   174\t- **One rare bundle of a widely repeated statement cascades.** One Step 1 record holding both a report and the company's denial (1 in 9\n   175\t  samples) turned six later denials into wrong merges. Read wrong merges for one shared original before blaming the comparator, and\n   176\t  fix the unit at Step 1 (\"one party's statement per item\"), not with a probability cut.\n   177\t- **The judge's criterion and the line's test must be one ruler.** Jev's \"same evidence\" against the readers' \"main statement\" made\n   178\t  4 of 8 wrong merges. Aligning Jev's wording to the line's test removed them.\n   179\t- **An output cap binds on the longest articles.** At high effort, reasoning alone can exceed 32k tokens. A cut answer is not JSON: retry\n   180\t  it as a format failure, and size the cap from the longest completions seen.\n   181\t- **Number records by a stable identity, never by a count of completed rows.** One failed item shifted every later id and broke the\n   182\t  reference mapping (34% mapped instead of 86%).\n   183\t- **Check theses against the frozen question gold, not only the readers.** Readers called most theses \"bets\" that the gold lists\n   184\t  must_not. When the two references split widely, that is Alex's decision.\n   185\t- **A fix found by reading a probe is a new arm.** Tonight four arms (c4, c5, c6 and the Step 2 criteria) went out after trace reading and\nls: docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calibration-D741: No such file or directory\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
