{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22 && nl -ba CHECKED.json | sed -n '134,152p'; nl -ba adjudication/R11.claude.json | sed -n '5,58p'; nl -ba E/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-190; nl -ba O/answers/codex.json | sed -n '27,73p'; nl -ba reader-packets/R03.md | sed -n '29,46p'; nl -ba adjudication/R07.md | sed -n '35,56p' | cut -c1-360\"","aggregated_output":"   134\t  },\n   135\t  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n   136\t },\n   137\t {\n   138\t  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n   139\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n   140\t  \"sha256\": \"a4afb41bbbdcbce71c54edcf161c9b50139275d5218241a9f0ad4c6c938f1044\",\n   141\t  \"stage\": \"checked\",\n   142\t  \"errors\": [\n   143\t   [\n   144\t    \"S4\",\n   145\t    \"v4: uses F4 for NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027 Q3 beside its forecast figure F8\"\n   146\t   ]\n   147\t  ],\n   148\t  \"recorded\": [\n   149\t   {\n   150\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n   151\t    \"figure\": \"F6\"\n   152\t   },\n     5\t   \"why\": \"F1 25 (e2620); e2889 'for the rest of its fiscal year' puts a time on the $18bn, so reading it as paid in Q3-Q4 follows from the quote; the answer states it treats the $18bn as part of the $25bn; F3 = 7; F9 = 7 stated probability-weighted estimate (e1919, e1970, e3474, e4669, e3934, e2280/e2305; e2643, e4487 against); F10 = 14, rung 1. Reader 1's alternative (none paid by year end) goes against the quote's timing: a risk, not a break.\"\n     6\t  }\n     7\t },\n     8\t \"defects\": [\n     9\t  {\n    10\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t   \"figures\": [\n    13\t    \"F2\",\n    14\t    \"F3\",\n    15\t    \"F10\"\n    16\t   ],\n    17\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    18\t   \"why\": \"F2 value 18, USD bn, '$18 billion', qualifier none matches the quote; F3 and F10 are computed, so this type cannot apply.\"\n    19\t  },\n    20\t  {\n    21\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    22\t   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    23\t   \"figures\": [\n    24\t    \"F4\"\n    25\t   ],\n    26\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    27\t   \"why\": \"'up to' stands before '$3 billion' only; the quote writes 'an additional $1 billion'; qualifier none matches.\"\n    28\t  },\n    29\t  {\n    30\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    31\t   \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n    32\t   \"figures\": [\n    33\t    \"F11\",\n    34\t    \"F1\",\n    35\t    \"F12\"\n    36\t   ],\n    37\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    38\t   \"why\": \"42 + 25 = 67 exact; 'The value is the number as written', qualifier recorded separately; F12 does not feed the outcome.\"\n    39\t  },\n    40\t  {\n    41\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    42\t   \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    43\t   \"figures\": [\n    44\t    \"F13\"\n    45\t   ],\n    46\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    47\t   \"why\": \"value 70, '$70 billion', qualifier none match; 'funded and unfunded' and 'to date' are the writer's description of what it measures; F13 not used in the outcome or reference.\"\n    48\t  },\n    49\t  {\n    50\t   \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    51\t   \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n    52\t   \"figures\": [\n    53\t    \"F1\",\n    54\t    \"F2\",\n    55\t    \"F3\",\n    56\t    \"F10\"\n    57\t   ],\n    58\t   \"decision\": \"reject\",\n     1\t{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":20,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"un\n    27\t    {\n    28\t      \"id\": \"F2\",\n    29\t      \"what\": {\n    30\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    31\t        \"measure\": \"equity commitment funding scheduled for the rest of the fiscal year\",\n    32\t        \"scope\": \"company\",\n    33\t        \"period\": \"FY2027 H2\"\n    34\t      },\n    35\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    36\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    37\t      \"value\": 18,\n    38\t      \"low\": null,\n    39\t      \"high\": null,\n    40\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    41\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2889\",\n    42\t      \"quote_number\": \"$18 billion\",\n    43\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    44\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    45\t      \"operation\": null,\n    46\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    47\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    48\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    49\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    50\t    },\n    51\t    {\n    52\t      \"id\": \"F3\",\n    53\t      \"what\": {\n    54\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    55\t        \"measure\": \"equity investment commitments\",\n    56\t        \"scope\": \"company\",\n    57\t        \"period\": \"end of FY2027\"\n    58\t      },\n    59\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n    60\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    61\t      \"value\": 7,\n    62\t      \"low\": null,\n    63\t      \"high\": null,\n    64\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    65\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    66\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    67\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    68\t      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n    69\t      \"operation\": \"difference\",\n    70\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    71\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    72\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    73\t      \"reference_for\": [\"v29\"]\n    29\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    30\t\n    31\t# The request as the writer saw it\n    32\t\n    33\t[Family] members:\n    34\tThesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\n    35\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\n    36\t\n    37\tRelations: none.\n    38\tScopes: NVIDIA revenue Data Center is part of NVIDIA revenue company. A sum never adds the part into the whole for the same period; a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest.\n    39\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each, through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes: NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q3; NVIDIA, revenue, company, FY2027 Q4.\n    40\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v4 = NVIDIA | revenue | company | FY2027.\n    41\t\n    42\t[Evidence in view for the family, 296 items]\n    43\t- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.\n    44\t    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\n    45\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273, e274, e326, e478, e1401, e1425; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    46\t- e1040 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source OpenAI ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] OpenAI selected AMD to deploy 6 GW of GPUs, a planned deployment that could affect NVIDIA's competitive position in AI accelerators.\n    35\t \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). A growth rate computed from guidance may serve as a stated benchmark for an estimate. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (d\n    36\t \"figures/id\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\",\n    37\t \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\n    38\t \"figures/what/measure\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\",\n    39\t \"figures/what/period\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\",\n    40\t \"figures/kind\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\",\n    41\t \"figures/basis\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\",\n    42\t \"figures/value\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\",\n    43\t \"figures/low\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    44\t \"figures/high\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    45\t \"figures/unit\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\",\n    46\t \"figures/evidence_id\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\",\n    47\t \"figures/quote_number\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only \n    48\t \"figures/qualifier\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\",\n    49\t \"figures/inputs\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\",\n    50\t \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % ch\n    51\t \"figures/basis_text\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\",\n    52\t \"figures/rests_on\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\",\n    53\t \"figures/outcome_for\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\",\n    54\t \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix appli\n    55\t \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\n    56\t \"answers/rung\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22 && nl -ba reader-key.json | sed -n '1,17p'; rg -n -C 1 'netting|balance|commit' adjudication/R02.{claude,codex}.json | cut -c1-430; cd ../../../docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba BLIND-RESULT.md | sed -n '58,67p'; nl -ba BLIND2-RESULT.md | sed -n '11,16p;30,37p;65,89p'; rg -n 'e2721|e1041' ../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/{P,P-v4}/user.txt | cut -c1-300\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22 && nl -ba reader-key.json | sed -n '1,17p'; rg -n -C 1 'netting|balance|commit' adjudication/R02.{claude,codex}.json | cut -c1-430; cd ../../../docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba BLIND-RESULT.md | sed -n '58,67p'; nl -ba BLIND2-RESULT.md | sed -n '11,16p;30,37p;65,89p'; rg -n 'e2721|e1041' ../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/{P,P-v4}/user.txt | cut -c1-300\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\n     2\t \"R01\": {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n     5\t },\n     6\t \"R02\": {\n     7\t  \"case\": \"O\",\n     8\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n     9\t },\n    10\t \"R03\": {\n    11\t  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n    12\t  \"writer\": \"claude\"\n    13\t },\n    14\t \"R04\": {\n    15\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    16\t  \"writer\": \"codex\"\n    17\t },\nadjudication/R02.codex.json-4-      \"supported\": \"yes\",\nadjudication/R02.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"The forecast treats the $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027 as part of the $25 billion July balance and assumes it is paid by year-end, leaving $7 billion. The low case adds $1 billion for Lancium. The high case also adds $1.5 billion for SB Energy, $10 billion for Anthropic, and a stated $4.5 billion allowance for other deals or delays. Those assumptions yield $8–24 billion\nadjudication/R02.codex.json-6-    }\n--\nadjudication/R02.codex.json-34-      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\nadjudication/R02.codex.json:35:      \"why\": \"The reference rule says the figure and every figure beneath it may be “never an estimate or a forecast.” E2889 says “$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.” It does not say that the $18 billion is part of the July 26 balance or that it will all be paid by year-end. F3 relies on both assumptions, so its $7 billion cannot be attributed to NVIDIA's\nadjudication/R02.codex.json-36-    }\n    58\t**Unsettled: the two families split** (the Codex adjudicator upholds, the Claude adjudicator rejects).\n    59\t- **A computed market reference.**\n    60\t  - The cases:\n    61\t    - consensus company revenue ($411B) × the reported Q2 Data Center share (92.5%) gives a Data Center reference of about $380B\n    62\t      (R05, R08, R12);\n    63\t    - the reported $25B balance − the $18B \"committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year\" gives $7B (R02).\n    64\t  - **The Codex adjudicator's view:** applying a quarter's share to a full year, or netting the schedule, is the writer's assumption,\n    65\t    so it is not the market's reference.\n    66\t  - **The Claude adjudicator's view:** the rule allows a figure computed only from consensus, guidance and reported figures.\n    67\t  - The original design took the Claude view: DESIGN.md C5 passes q02's \"$392B consensus × 92% reported mix\" as a reference.\n    11\tEvery other registered line passes:\n    12\t- Part A (104 cases, 40 mutants, 19 rehearsal checks) was green before the run.\n    13\t- The run is complete: 12 first replies, 24 readings, 6 adjudication packets, each read by two adjudicators.\n    14\t- Every case has at least one usable reply. The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R05 and R09).\n    15\t- No accepted reply carries a settled defect: no defect was upheld by both adjudicators, and no reader pair marked the same defect.\n    16\t\n    30\t| E | publish: 36, rung 3 | publish: 36, rung 3 |\n    31\t\n    32\t- 12 of 12 were schema-valid.\n    33\t- 11 of 12 passed every code check.\n    34\t- D741's three rulings were applied:\n    35\t  - S applies a stated deduction from the total in both replies (Claude 3% non-data-center share; Codex 92% data-center share).\n    36\t  - No v8 reference carries a past quarter's share (the S5 [D741] check refused none).\n    37\t  - D740's \"from … to …\" fix is exercised: E's writers copied the CPU market figures without a range refusal.\n    65\t**Unsettled: the families split** (in every split, the Codex adjudicator upholds and the Claude adjudicator rejects). They fall into\n    66\ttwo classes.\n    67\t1. **O's \"$18 billion\" (R07, R11).** e2889: \"it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of its fiscal year\".\n    68\t   Both O writers read it as the part of e2620's $25B balance funded by year-end, stated that reading as their assumption, and built\n    69\t   the reference as 25 − 18 = 7. The rung depends on that reading: without the deduction the balance starts at $25B, which is rung 2.\n    70\t   - **R07, \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\" on F3.** D741 ruling 2 decides it: the market's reference may be \"a\n    71\t     calculation that carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period\". F3 is reported − guidance with no\n    72\t     carried share, so by the ruling it is not this defect.\n    73\t   - **R07, \"two forecasts of one quarter\" on F3 and F10.** F3 has the outcome's key but is the July balance still unfunded, a part of\n    74\t     the year-end total. The Claude adjudicator calls it the company-schedule baseline beneath the forecast, which the format allows.\n    75\t     The Codex adjudicator calls it a part labelled as the whole.\n    76\t   - **R11.** The Codex family calls F2's \"guidance to fund in Q3–Q4\" an unsupported schedule and the forecast not supported. The\n    77\t     Claude family says \"for the remainder of its fiscal year\" gives the timing and the answer states the reading.\n    78\t   - The first test split the same way on the same passage (D740, R02).\n    79\t2. **Statements in the answer's prose that no figure carries** (R03, R09, R11). Codex upholds them as \"number misread\" or \"wrong\n    80\t   arithmetic\". Claude rejects them because those types are defined on source and computed figures. I read each one against its\n    81\t   passage:\n    82\t   - R11: \"if the $18B funding slips into FY2028 … probably keep it below $25B\" is wrong on the answer's own numbers: a full slip gives\n    83\t     25 + 7 = 32, rung 2.\n    84\t   - R09: \"$115bn, then 20% sequential growth\" is offered as reaching rung 1, but it gives 430.8 against the 431.2 edge.\n    85\t   - R09: \"both 6 GW\" drops e1041's \"up to\".\n    86\t   - R03: \"Vera Rubin is already about 20%\" states e2721's expectation as achieved.\n    87\t   - R03 \"flat second half\" is disputed: the Claude adjudicator shows a reading under which it holds.\n    88\t   None of these enters a figure, an outcome or a rung. They are what the card would show an analyst. Code does not check prose\n    89\t   (D737), and the registered defect types do not cover it.\n../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:17:- e1041 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Meta signed for a deployment of up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, a competing accelerator commitment relevant to NVIDIA'\n../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:49:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2721, e3213, e3405, e3589; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:604:- e2721 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in Q3 FY2027.\n../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P/user.txt:20:- e1041 [fact; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source Meta ; from gnews:4f1770366917aea8eed6ae4c 2026-07-24] Meta signed for a deployment of up to 6 GW of AMD Instinct GPUs, a competing accelerator commitment relevant to NVIDIA's F\n../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P/user.txt:52:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2721, e3213, e3405, e3589; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n../../../poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P/user.txt:607:- e2721 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in Q3 FY2027.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22 && for f in */answers/*.json; do printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; jq -r '\"'.answers[]|.reasons' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22 && for f in */answers/*.json; do printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$f\"; jq -r '\"'.answers[]|.reasons' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"E/answers/claude.json\nReference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress said the company still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects that to more than double in FY2028 (e3660). That puts the guided floor at $40 billion (F3), right at the lower edge of rung 2. Other outlets that say the same thing, including Huang's 'more than double' (e4691, e4764, e4856), are one claim and are counted once. The sources use 'CPU revenue' and 'Vera CPU revenue' for the same figure. Forecast: I discount the guided floor to about $36 billion (F10), which is in rung 3 ($30-40 billion). Reasons for the discount: (1) The guidance is a floor for a product whose volume the evidence does not show. There is no reported Vera revenue or shipment number, and the FY2027 $20 billion base is itself an unreported expectation, described in e4856 as a 'potential'. FY2028 runs from about February 2027 to January 2028, so the whole year is still to be sold and delivered. (2) The demand evidence is real but has no size. Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), and Anthropic is evaluating it (e5044). Neither gives a unit or dollar figure that would show the doubling is already in hand. (3) The competitive evidence leans against it. AMD's own benchmarks claim leads of 1.2x per core, 2.24x per platform, 3.4x rack throughput, and higher memory bandwidth (1.6 vs 1.2 TB/s) over Vera (e5399-e5402, e5404). These are a competitor's chosen tests, but they are specific and could slow Vera's wins as a standalone CPU. (4) Market size as a sanity check: BofA puts the CPU market at $35 billion in 2026, rising to $170 billion by 2030 (e4857). AMD says server CPU revenue grows 50% a year (e4687). Together they give about $52.5 billion for 2027 (F7), so the guided FY2028 floor would be about 76% of the whole market (F8). That says the target is aggressive, or that NVIDIA counts CPU revenue more widely; I weigh it lightly. Supporting factors: Arm servers already take 45% of data-center revenue share (e4692), and the server CPU market is growing fast (e4687, e4857). Themes: v7 (2027 hyperscaler capex just above $1.1 trillion) means a large spending pool in FY2028's window and pushes toward higher rungs. But growth from v6's roughly $750 billion in 2026 is about 47%, short of a doubling, so a doubling depends on Vera winning new attach and share; v6 itself pushes neither way. Net: the outcome falls just under the $40 billion boundary, so rung 3 rather than rung 2. What would move it: reported FY2027 CPU revenue at or above $20 billion with Vera volume visible, or a sized hyperscaler order, would lift the forecast into rung 2. A clear miss on the FY2027 base would push it toward rung 4.\nE/answers/codex.json\nManagement's outlook puts the reference at the $40 billion threshold for rung 2. The Vera-specific account describes upside beyond that threshold by comparing FY2028 Vera revenue with the broader FY2027 CPU outlook. The repeated reports add no independent confirmation. NVIDIA expects deployments across major customers, and Anthropic's evaluation shows interest in a specific workload. The evidence gives no shipment volumes or sized orders to turn that interest into revenue. AMD's claimed 20% per-core lead in its benchmark is a reason to discount Vera's adoption, with the related tests adding context to the same competitive signal. Hyperscaler spending supports demand, especially in calendar 2027, but gives no estimate of spending on Vera. My estimate is $36 billion, below the reference and within rung 3.\nO/answers/claude.json\nStarting point: NVIDIA reported $25B of equity investment commitments at the end of Q2 FY2027 (F1), exactly at the rung 1/rung 2 boundary. The company said $18B of commitments will be funded in the rest of the fiscal year (F2). I read that as part of the $25B, which leaves about $7B of today's commitments outstanding at year end (F3). For the balance to stay at $25B or above, about $18B of new commitments would have to be signed after July 26 and still be unfunded on the FY2027 balance-sheet date. The new deals shown are mostly small or fund at closing: Poolside $1B, SB Energy's first $1.5B tranche, Lancium's initial $2B and probably most of the $3.5B MediaTek convertible bonds. What could remain outstanding: Lancium's $1B milestone tranche, SB Energy's second $1.5B tranche if the IPO slips, a reported but unsigned US$10B Anthropic IPO anchor commitment, an approximately $2.5B Thinking Machines stake under discussion, and an unsized Perplexity stake. Weighted by how likely each is to be signed and still unfunded, that comes to about $7B (F9). The forecast is about $14B (F10), in rung 1. Even if every rumoured deal were signed and none closed, the total would only just reach the mid-$20Bs, and Huang's comment that the large AI-lab investments will not repeat (e2643) plus his statement that the $100B OpenAI plan was never a commitment (e4487) both point against another large commitment. Bank of America's $70B 'committed equity investments' (e1732, e2080, e2687) measures something else: more than $42B already spent on stakes in H1 (F11) plus the $25B outstanding gives about $67B (F12), close to BofA's figure (F13). So it counts funded investments too, is not the year-end unfunded balance, and moves nothing here. The same applies to the 'over $40 billion committed in 2026' (e3880). Upside risks: the $18B funding slips into FY2028, or an Anthropic commitment is signed with the IPO only in 2027. Either would raise the balance but probably keep it below $25B. No theme in force bears directly on NVIDIA's own commitments balance: the AI-capex themes (v5, updated; v6) describe hyperscaler spending, not NVIDIA's unfunded equity pledges.\nO/answers/codex.json\nI forecast $16 billion of outstanding equity investment commitments at FY2027 year-end, which falls below $25 billion and lands in rung 1. The July filing's $25 billion balance sat at the bottom of rung 2. I read NVIDIA's $18 billion disclosure for the rest of FY2027 as the portion scheduled to fund before year-end. That gives a company-schedule reference of $7 billion in rung 1 before new deals. Bank of America's broader $70 billion estimate would fall in rung 3 on the ladder, but it gives no year-end date or unpaid balance. The February OpenAI transaction predates the July filing, so I do not add it separately. I estimate that announced investments and discounted deal discussions add $9 billion to the remaining balance. Anthropic is the largest named upside risk to the balance because the reported sizing is substantial and the signing and funding dates are open. The hyperscaler spending themes modestly support more infrastructure deals, although they provide no dollar amount for NVIDIA's own commitments.\nP-v4/answers/claude.json\nReference: the analyst consensus for FY2027 revenue is $411 billion (e4333, repeated in e4594 and e5204). That sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion). Build: reported Q1 ($81.62 billion) and Q2 ($96.2 billion) give $177.82 billion for H1. Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, and it assumes no China data-center compute revenue. NVIDIA beat its Q2 midpoint by 5.7% ($96.2 billion against $91.0 billion, e3200). It also beat its Q4 FY2026 guidance ($68 billion against $65 billion, e2452). A third-party record puts recent actuals about 3.1% above the high end of guidance, which would mean roughly $113.6 billion (e5055, e5056). Supply covers only about 70% of demand, capacity is sold out and supply stays constrained through FY2028 (e3733, e4911, e5067), so I take a smaller beat: Q3 at about $112.5 billion (my estimate). For Q4 I carry the guided 12.27% sequential growth forward, which gives about $126.3 billion. That rate is not aggressive. Management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e4283). Vera Rubin is already about 20% of Q3 data-center revenue (e2721), with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler (e2693). Supply commitments rose from $119 billion to $279 billion (e2759). On the other side, memory and power limits argue against a much faster step. FY2027 comes out at about $416.6 billion, or about 93% growth on FY2026's $215.9 billion. That growth is consistent with the guided Q3 growth of about 89%, and the total is inside rung 2. Boundaries: rung 1 needs Q3 plus Q4 of at least about $253 billion, which means Q4 sequential growth above roughly 20% even after a strong Q3. That is unlikely under the stated supply limits. Rung 3 needs Q3 plus Q4 below about $214 billion, which means a roughly flat Q4 at the low end of Q3 guidance. That contradicts the guided Q4 reacceleration. The items saying 70% growth for the full fiscal year (e3280, e3890, e4628) I read as the FY2028 outlook. The call transcript (e2691) and e5227 place the roughly 70% at FY2028. Applied to FY2026, 70% would require Q4 revenue far below the guided Q3, which H1 actuals and Q3 guidance rule out. Morningstar's $361 billion data-center estimate (e2295) predates the Q2 report and implies a flat second half, so it moves the forecast little. SemiAnalysis's call that second-half data-center revenue will beat estimates by 20% (e1135) points up, but it gives no base. Competing AMD and custom-chip commitments matter more for later years than for a supply-constrained FY2027. Themes: CY2027 hyperscaler capex is judged above the market reference (v7), and China H200 purchases are judged likely to see limited authorization (v9), which Q3 guidance excludes. Both are small upside within rung 2. I have not sized them separately; the beat and Q4 assumptions absorb them, so China is not judged at zero. CY2026 capex (v6) is in line with its reference.\nP-v4/answers/codex.json\nThe $411 billion full-year analyst consensus sits in rung 2. I forecast $416.4 billion, also in rung 2. After the reported first two quarters, I expect $112 billion in Q3. Rubin shipments and the prior guidance beat support upside to NVIDIA's $108 billion midpoint, while supply constraints temper the larger beat in the outside estimate. The separate Q4 analyst growth estimate implies $112.2 billion, essentially flat against my Q3 forecast. NVIDIA expects hyperscaler growth to pick up as Rubin supply expands, and CoreWeave has already put a multi-rack system online. I carry forward the guided sequential growth at a slightly faster pace to reach about $126.6 billion in Q4. Memory constraints and long delivery times make the much larger shipment increase needed for rung 1 unlikely. Calendar 2026 hyperscaler spending supports near-term demand, and higher spending in calendar 2027 mainly supports later deliveries. The absence of additional restrictions would preserve existing sales channels. Chinese H200 authorization would add some late-quarter upside if licenses and deliveries follow in time, while the continuing Commerce investigation leaves the immediate sales outlook unchanged.\nP-v8/answers/claude.json\nReference: Morningstar's FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361 billion (F18, e2295, dated August 19, before the Q2 results) sits in rung 2. No consensus figure for the segment itself is shown; company-level consensus is $411 billion (F11, e4333). Derivation: reported Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89.0 billion in Q2, so $164.25 billion for H1 (e5309 confirms about $164 billion). Q3 company revenue is guided at $108.0 billion plus or minus 2% (e2652). NVIDIA beat the high end of guidance in each of the last 12 quarters, by 3.1% on average (e5054, e5055), which would give about $113.6 billion (e5056). It also beat the Q2 midpoint by $5.2 billion (e3200). But supply is sold out and capped by memory, power and packaging (e3733, e3115, e5067, e4911), so I take a smaller beat: $112 billion. For Q4, management says hyperscale growth speeds up again as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e4283, e5061). Vera Rubin is already about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue (e2721), at higher revenue per gigawatt ($40 billion versus $25 billion, e4202). Supply commitments rose from $119 billion to $279 billion (e2759), and FY2028 growth is put at about 70% (e2691). Against the guided 12% Q3 sequential step (e5005), I use 11% for Q4, giving $124.3 billion. That puts FY2027 company revenue at about $414 billion (F10), slightly above the $411 billion consensus. Data Center's share of revenue rose every quarter, reaching 92.5% in Q2 (e3399). I carry it to 92.7% in Q3 and 92.9% in Q4. That gives Q3 Data Center revenue of $103.8 billion, Q4 $115.5 billion and a full year of about $383.6 billion: rung 2, the same rung as the reference, with the outcome about $23 billion above it. Boundaries: reaching $400 billion would need H2 company revenue of about $254 billion. On a $112 billion Q3, that means Q4 up about 27% sequentially, which current supply limits make unlikely. SemiAnalysis' call that H2 Data Center revenue will beat estimates by 20% (e1135) leans toward the upper part of the band. Falling below $350 billion would need H2 company revenue near $200 billion, less than two quarters at the low end of Q3 guidance. The 'just under $330 billion run rate' (e5310) is H1 annualised flat and is not used. The '70% full-year growth' readings (e3280, e3890) clash with the reported H1 and the Q3 guidance: they would imply a Q4 near $81 billion. The CFO's own words (e2691) place the roughly 70% at FY2028, so I read them that way. The $83.7 billion Q2 Data Center figure in e4907 contradicts the company's reported $89.0 billion and is set aside. Q3 guidance assumes no China Data Center compute revenue (e2681). The theme that China will likely allow limited H200 purchases by January 31, 2027 (v9) adds a little upside. With supply bound, though, such sales would mostly replace other shipments within FY2027, and the 11% Q4 step leaves room for them. The updated hyperscaler capex theme for calendar 2027 ($1.1–1.2 trillion, v7) supports Q4 demand. The calendar 2026 capex theme (v6) is in line with the market and does not move this. Competitor deployments (AMD's 6 GW deals with OpenAI and Meta, Broadcom's 2027 ramp) land mostly after FY2027 ends in January 2027.\nP-v8/answers/codex.json\nI forecast $388.57 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2 alongside Morningstar's $361 billion segment reference. NVIDIA reported $164.27 billion in the first half. I estimate $104 billion in Q3 from a measured beat of company guidance, with AI clouds and enterprise customers driving most of the segment's growth. I estimate $120.3 billion in Q4 as Rubin supply expands and hyperscaler sales pick up. CoreWeave's live multi-rack deployment supports the ramp, while memory constraints and long delivery times limit the volume that can ship by fiscal year-end. The same quarterly build implies $418.6 billion of company revenue against the $411 billion company consensus. Stronger 2026 hyperscaler spending supports near-term deliveries, and the higher 2027 spending outlook adds some late-Q4 demand. Possible H200 authorization adds limited late-year upside because orders still have to clear licensing and ship before the fiscal close. The other policy themes imply no material change to booked FY2027 sales. A faster supply ramp could take the segment above $400 billion, but the shipment and capacity evidence favors rung 2.\nP/answers/claude.json\nReference: the analyst consensus for FY2027 is $411bn (F10; e4594, repeated in e4333 and e5204), which sits in rung 2 ($392bn to $431.2bn). Zacks' 87.4% growth estimate (e4990, about $405bn on FY2026's $215.9bn) falls in the same rung. Outcome: H1 is already reported at $177.82bn (Q1 $81.62bn, Q2 $96.2bn; e4992 gives $177.8bn). Q3 guidance is $108bn plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA beat the Q2 midpoint by $5.2bn, beat its Q4 FY2026 guide by $3bn, and has topped the high end for 12 quarters. But it is selling only about 70% of demand and capacity is sold out (e3733, e4911), so my Q3 estimate is $111bn. Q4 carries the guided 12% sequential growth forward to $124.3bn. That pace is supported by management saying hyperscale growth picks up again in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e4283), and by supply commitments rising from $119bn to $279bn (e2759). Supply limits through FY2028 (e5067) rule out a much faster pace. FY2027 = $413.1bn, in rung 2 and close to consensus. Rung 1 would need Q3 plus Q4 of at least $253.4bn (for example $115bn, then 20% sequential growth), which the supply limits do not allow. Rung 3 would need H2 below $214.2bn, meaning Q3 and Q4 well below guidance. Several items say Kress guided '70% growth for the full fiscal year' (e3280, e4628, e3890). The transcript shows the 70% is next year's supply-limited growth (e2691: customers' forecasts point to doubling, but 'we expect to grow approximately 70%'), and e5227 reads it as FY2028. Read as FY2027, it would mean Q4 falling to about $81bn after a $108bn Q3, so it does not bear on this year. AMD's 6 GW OpenAI and Meta deals (e1040, e1041) and hyperscalers' own chips deploy mostly from 2027. They do not reduce NVIDIA's supply-limited FY2027 revenue. The China foreclosure (e2776) is already in guidance, which assumes no China data-center compute. My Q4 figure is not set to zero for China. Theme v9 judges a limited H200 authorization likely; that pushes up, but a few billion in November to January cannot reach rung 1. Theme v6 (four-hyperscaler CY2026 capex near $760bn) supports demand above supply. Theme v7 (CY2027 capex, updated) mostly lands in FY2028, so it leaves FY2027 unchanged.\nReference: Morningstar's $361bn estimate for FY2027 Data Center revenue (F17; e2295, made before the Q2 report) sits in rung 2 ($350bn to $400bn). No Data Center consensus figure is supplied. Outcome: H1 Data Center revenue is already reported at $164.25bn (Q1 $75.25bn, Q2 $89.0bn; e5309 gives $164bn). Data Center's share of revenue rose every quarter for a year, to 92.5% in Q2 (e3399). The second-half drivers are all Data Center: Vera Rubin at about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue (e2721), ACIE leading Q3, and hyperscale picking up again in Q4 (e5061, e3406). So I apply an estimated 92.7% to the family's H2 company forecast of $235.32bn, which gives $218.1bn. That implies Q3 Data Center revenue of about $102.9bn, against Sparks' $99.9bn at the guidance midpoint (e3401). FY2027 Data Center = $382.4bn, in rung 2. It fits inside the company total of $413.1bn, leaving about $30.8bn for the rest (Edge Computing was $7.2bn in Q2, up 13% sequentially). Rung 1 would need H2 Data Center revenue of $235.75bn, meaning company H2 of about $254bn, beyond the supply-limited path (e5067, e4911). Rung 3 would need H2 Data Center below $185.75bn, which is less than Q3 guidance held flat through Q4 (about $200bn). SemiAnalysis' call that H2 Data Center revenue beats estimates by 20% (e1135) leans up but gives no level. The roughly $330bn run rate in e5310 annualises H1 flat, so it is not used. e4907's $83.7bn Q2 figure conflicts with NVIDIA's reported $89.0bn (e2650, e2723) and is set aside. Themes are as for v4. v6 (CY2026 hyperscaler capex near $760bn) supports demand. v9 (a limited China H200 authorization judged likely) could add some Data Center revenue in Q4 that guidance excludes, but not enough to cross $400bn. v7 (CY2027 capex, updated) mostly lands in FY2028.\nP/answers/codex.json\nMy FY2027 revenue forecast is $419.22 billion, above the $411 billion analyst consensus and in the same rung 2 band. Reported first-half revenue anchors the forecast. The recent guidance-beat history, ACIE demand and a live multi-rack Rubin deployment support $113.6 billion in Q3 against the $108 billion guidance midpoint. I carry roughly the guided sequential pace into Q4, when expanding Rubin supply should lift hyperscaler shipments, for $127.8 billion. Memory constraints and long channel lead times keep the forecast below the $431.2 billion threshold. The calendar 2026 capex judgment supports shipments within FY2027. The updated calendar 2027 capex judgment adds a smaller lift through procurement ahead of deployments, since most of that spending period follows fiscal year-end. Limited H200 authorization adds modest late-quarter upside. The other four export-control judgments imply no incremental change to the shipment baseline this fiscal year.\nMy FY2027 Data Center forecast is $388.32 billion, above Morningstar's $361 billion outside estimate and in the same rung 2 band. I add the two reported quarters to the Data Center share of the shared Q3 and Q4 company forecasts, calculated by deducting forecast Edge Computing revenue. ACIE drives the near-term increase, and the live CoreWeave deployment and expected Q4 hyperscaler acceleration support higher Rubin shipments. SemiAnalysis's earlier upside call supports the direction, with no dollar baseline for translating it into a current forecast. Memory and delivery constraints limit shipment upside and keep the forecast below $400 billion. Calendar 2026 hyperscaler capex directly supports segment demand. The updated calendar 2027 capex outlook offers a smaller benefit through orders that can ship this fiscal year. Limited H200 authorization adds modest late-quarter sales. The other four export-control judgments leave existing shipment constraints in place for this forecast.\nS/answers/claude.json\nReference: Zacks expects the four (Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta) to spend roughly $720-745bn of capital expenditure in 2026, which sits in rung 4 ($700-800bn). Building the figure from company guidance: Alphabet raised its 2026 plan to $195-205bn, Meta to $125-145bn and Amazon to $220bn, together $540-570bn. Microsoft gives no calendar-year number. Its January-March quarter was $30.88bn, up 84%, and is still ramping, so I estimate about $165bn for 2026. That is inside the $150-205bn the four-company estimate implies for Microsoft. At current guidance the four total $705-735bn. Every company raised its plan during the year, memory costs are still rising, and the latest trackers put the four at about $745bn and $760bn. Meta's own plan of about $130bn keeps Meta low in its range. I forecast about $740bn of total capital spending. Data-center infrastructure is nearly all of it: taking out about 3% for Amazon's warehouses and logistics and for offices gives about $718bn of data-center spending, inside rung 4. Outside data-center estimates agree: about $750bn for tech giants' data centers in 2026. NVIDIA puts the five largest at nearly $800bn and BofA puts the four plus Oracle at about $795bn, both consistent with the four near $740-750bn. The main downside is physical limits that could delay spending into 2027: blocked or delayed projects, power, electricians and memory supply. If total spending landed near $720bn and the non-data-center share were larger, the result could slip just below $700bn (rung 3). That is the main risk to this rung. Rung 5 would need more than $820bn of total spending from the four, and no source supports that.\nS/answers/codex.json\nI forecast $680.8 billion in calendar 2026 data-center capex, which puts spending on rung 3. The closest market reference is the four companies' indicated AI infrastructure spending of more than $730 billion. Its quoted threshold sits on rung 4 and uses a broader spending label. Four-company total capex is tracking at roughly $720 billion to $745 billion. Alphabet's higher guidance, Amazon's memory-driven increase and Microsoft's Azure backlog support my $740 billion total capex estimate. Allocating an estimated 92% to data centers puts the forecast below the rung 4 boundary. Reported project delays and slow grid connections add downside risk. The national project tally covers multiple owners and project lifetimes, so it cannot quantify a deduction for these companies this year. NVIDIA's repeated spending estimate covers five companies, and the expanded AWS GPU deployment is scheduled for later years.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"agent_message","text":"**STOP on one confirmed line. Reject the blanket `%` and `x` cutoff.** The saved answer used $108B to derive a growth rate and $112.5B as its Q3 revenue. The proposed fix also accepts an answer that uses $108B as the dollar starting point for Q4 while counting $112.5B for Q3.\n\n1. **Serious — The verdict is right under the adjudication procedure used in both runs.** Both refusal reviewers judged the P-v4 answer right. I reconciled all 12 first replies with the saved requests and checker results. All 12 passed the schema, 11 published, and one was refused. There are 24 first readings and 12 adjudication readings across six packets. Every case has at least one usable reply, and both joint replies have both forecasts supported. The Part A logs show 104 passing cases, 40 caught mutants plus the control, and 19 passing rehearsal checks. I independently reran the 104 cases against both checker versions. [Registered lines](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [Refusal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:138) · [Refusal review](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/refusal-review/P-v4-claude.codex.json:3) · [Part A logs](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-cases.log:105) · [Mutants](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-mutants.log:42) · [Rehearsal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-rehearsal.log:20)\n\n   **Wording — R07 has two disputed defects on F3.** One reader flagged an estimate presented as the market’s reference. The other flagged two values for the same quantity. The registered line requires agreement on the named defect itself. Each adjudicator pair split, so neither defect adds a confirmed stop under the first run’s agreement rule. I would write “one confirmed stop, with unresolved marks” because the registration says a third reader settles disputes, and the procedure used here added two adjudicators who could still disagree. Across the six packets, five marks were jointly rejected, nine split, and zero were jointly upheld. [R07 votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:157) · [First run’s procedure](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md:37) · [Registered wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:252)\n\n   **Minor — Two details in the write-up need correction.** Only E’s Claude writer copied the two CPU-market figures and exercised the range fix. The write-up also omits R11’s split over F13, which assigns a time period and a funded/unfunded scope to an outside $70B figure. F13 feeds neither the outcome nor the reference. [E source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:30) · [E Codex reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:1) · [F13 votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:377)\n\n2. **Serious — The proposed checker change is wider than Alex’s ruling.** I reproduced the reported census: one of 24 saved replies changes, and the candidate passes all 104 existing cases. I then tested a variation of the saved P-v4 reply. It keeps the $112.5B Q3 forecast in the annual sum and projects Q4 by applying the guided growth factor twice from Q2: `96.2 × (108 / 96.2)² = 121.25`. The first multiplication reconstructs the $108B Q3 guidance as the dollar base for the second. The reply passes the schema. The current checker refuses it on S4 alone, and the candidate publishes $411.57B. A second probe also flipped when Q4 company sales were calculated as `$116B ÷ ($99.9B / $108B)`. Neither probe needs to relabel the original Q3 guidance. Alex approved using guidance to set a growth benchmark while the forecast supplies the quarter’s dollar value. The cutoff lets a ratio carry the second dollar value into the outcome. [Alex’s exact question and answer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348) · [Saved P-v4 reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/answers/claude.json:1) · [$99.9B source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/P-v4/user.txt:613) · [Candidate cutoff](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt:638)\n\n3. **Serious — Change both request sentences with the narrower checker fix.** The system asks writers to carry guided growth forward. The schema says the rate can inform an `estimate`, which is a specific figure type, and the header can be read as requiring every calculation path to go through the quarter forecast. A code-only change leaves that tension in place. I would say that a growth rate derived from guidance may inform an estimate or be used in `apply_growth` with the shared quarter’s forecast as the dollar base. The header should require the single forecast wherever the calculation uses that quarter’s **value**. Drop the reflection’s parenthetical that a growth rate is another quantity. The unit alone cannot tell whether the calculation uses it as a benchmark or to recover a second dollar value. Give readers the same distinction. [System text](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [Schema wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:152) · [Header](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/render_v13.py:176) · [Proposed wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r56.md:21)\n\n4. **Serious — Disclose the $18B split as affecting O’s answer. D741 ruling 2 leaves it open.** The sources report a $25B July balance and $18B committed for the rest of the year. They do not establish that the $18B is included in the July balance or will all be paid by year-end. Those assumptions can support a conditional forecast. They do not establish a $7B company-schedule reference just because the subtraction carries no past share. R11 presents the funding schedule as something the company said and states only the overlap as an assumption. Without the deduction, the July balance alone starts in rung 2. The first run split on the same passage. This adds no registered stop and needs no Alex ruling before the $0 checker test. If the council still disagrees when deciding whether to advance, put the reference question to Alex explicitly. [Original passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/O/user.txt:36) · [D741 ruling 2](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11356) · [R07 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:9) · [First run’s split](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md:58)\n\n   **Minor — Wrong numbers confined to prose are a disclosure under this test.** The registered definitions cover copied and computed figures. The errors still matter to a reader. R09’s example reaches $430.82B against a $431.2B threshold, and R11’s full-slip scenario gives $32B on its own assumptions. Neither adds a registered stop or needs Alex before the next $0 test. Making prose accuracy a stop condition would be a separate decision. [Reader definitions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:169) · [R09 arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/adjudication/R09.codex.json:20) · [R11 arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/adjudication/R11.claude.json:49)\n\n5. **Recommendation and falsifier.** Narrow the checker to the demonstrated use of guidance as a growth benchmark, align the request and reader wording, and replay the 24 saved replies. The repair fails if R03 still refuses, either counterexample publishes, or another saved decision changes. Once that check passes, record the council decision and run **one fresh $0 blind test** with the revised request and the disagreement procedure stated up front. The old replies can prove what the checker does. Fresh replies show how writers read the new wording. Any registered stop keeps the work in council. A clean run goes to Alex with both disclosures for the separate paid-run decision. [Council authority](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:4871) · [Registered next step](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:267)\n\nNo files were edited. The two counterexamples ran in memory."}}
